library Number_Theory_Torser_Atlas

%% NUMBER THEORY THROUGH THE TESSERACT TORSER -- ONE STANDALONE DEVELOPMENT
%% Constructive H24/paracollatz revision 2026-11-25. Declarations, actual views,
%% normalization algorithms, proof witnesses, source
%% boundaries and compact replay instructions are contained in this file.
%% There are no external Hets library imports and no companion source files.
%%
%% READ THE GRAPH BY THE PREMISES NEEDED, NOT BY THE SIZE OF THE FINAL UNION.
%% H24_*: one natural carrier, one divisor fold, one parameterized ring;
%% sections, equivariance, typed composition, Boolean groups and adjunctions.
%% GC26_*: infinite Galois, inverse limits, all-modulus character values,
%% conductor/CRT sources, exact cyclic presentations and finite Fourier laws.
%% DA26_*: the same characters' Gauss/Jacobi/twisted-Kloosterman sums and twists.
%% MT26_*/MX26_*: Eisenstein/theta/lambda/Weierstrass, the faithful S3 algebra,
%% its bilinear Q-dagger, character theta and the exact normalizations.
%% RH26_*: the full original RH and Dirichlet-GRH formulas, Robin n>5040,
%% Lagarias n>=1, strictness for n>1, and separately labelled source branches.
%% TR26_*/MBR26_*: original four-bit torsor, Collatz words/parity return,
%% fibre adjunctions, base change, institutions and the same frame matrices.
%% HC24_*: actual tagged proof trees, local calibration, whole proof cost,
%% assumption support, composable improvement paths and full proof packets.
%% AT26_*: the joined divisor/character/analytic/matrix/original-zero returns.
%% CN26_*: canonical read representatives, marked naturality, split observations.
%% MG26_*: typed route fusion, exact defect, sparse/C2 calculations, bend return.
%% TP26_*: idempotent costs, FOUR support, finite witnesses, additive valuations.
%%
%% ENTRY POINT: AT25_ConstructivePolynomialAtlas joins the requested number
%% theory to the actual minimum constructor, native normalization, explicit
%% reversible producer, retained original returns and polynomial clocks.
%% AT26_CalculationAtlas remains its metagraph/canonicity/tropical base.
%% AT26_NumberTheoryAtlas remains the original conditional subject endpoint.
%% Neither base imports an unqualified RH/GRH premise.
%% AT26_WithGRHPremise records the named scenario assumption.
%% AT26_WithExternal2026DirichletTheorem records the separate external source
%% and its actual foundational dependencies. Neither label erases an axiom.
%% Select narrow specifications for proving; the complete union is for scope.
%%
%% COMPOSITION: F#=E_Y F D_X; G#=E_Z G D_Y. The only cancellation used for
%% G#F#=(GF)# is D_Y E_Y=id. Source return additionally uses endpoint sections.
%% Native identity is E D; it is identity on the encoded image. The constructed
%% packet chart keeps the whole payload and makes the original frame explicit.
%% Fixed-point reflection needs only D E=id and T E=E S. The original object,
%% original character, original zero and original proof are returned by name.
%% The 16-point controller is the chart base; its fibre retains the full source.
%%
%% ASSUMPTION BOUNDARIES: a ring suffices for polynomial frame laws; a specified
%% inverse of 3 supports the full six-coefficient decoder and Q-dagger. This
%% algebra admits Z_2 as well as complex instances. Natural divisor coefficients
%% need only the ring image of the shared natural fold. Field, characteristic
%% zero, real order, analytic convergence, Galois topology and source theorem
%% contracts are introduced in their own layers. The scalar ports are typed
%% instances, not an identification of ordered reals with complex scalars.
%%
%% HILBERT 24: local excess=w+sum(Phi(premises))-Phi(conclusion). Structural
%% induction gives Work=Phi(conclusion)+Excess for actual proof trees. Local
%% nonnegative calibration bounds every valid proof of that same judgement.
%% HP25 constructs the zero-excess minimizer from an admitted ranked tight
%% policy, recursively retaining every selected premise. GT25 specializes the
%% recovered original Candidate objective to guarded native questions and
%% composes its actual producer with a paid certificate-retaining wrapper. An admitted improvement
%% also requires Support(after) subset Support(before). Proof sharing and search
%% cost require their own charging model; no universal shortest-proof oracle or
%% globally weakest axiomatization is inferred from a successful prover run.
%% The executable typed normalizer preserves source type, original marks,
%% operation word, endpoints and support; hashes are indices, not equalities.
%% Recharts cancel while source actions remain. A valid k-leaf route uses
%% 2 chart calls after fusion instead of 2k. The same faithful block product
%% has 12M+6A and shared-C2 11M+8A+N programs, with exact ring certificates.
%% The second program wins in a supplied charge model when M >= 2A+N.
%%
%% STATUS CONVENTION: an unmarked sentence is a definition or an explicitly
%% supplied source contract. A %implied sentence is a proof obligation; it is
%% NOT automatically a completed proof. Written derivations accompany families
%% of obligations. The final RECEIPTS section distinguishes actual source-only
%% E proofs, checked acyclic lemma composition, exact symbolic checks, induction
%% arguments and remaining native obligations. Static Hets success checks syntax
%% and signatures, not every proof or every view's sentence preservation.
%%
%% CONVENTIONS: theta q=exp(pi*i*tau); Eisenstein Q=exp(2*pi*i*tau)=q^2;
%% MT26_E(k,...) has weight 2*k; classical j=1728*J; elliptic Kpar uses parameter
%% m=k^2. Character theta excludes conductor one explicitly; full Dirichlet GRH
%% includes it and imprimitive characters. Completion zero equivalence is now a
%% derived cancellation goal, not a supplied zero-equivalence contract. Gamma
%% and power definedness remain explicit on the open strip.
%%
%% ORIGINAL REQUESTED SECTIONS (primary sources are beside their source laws):
%% https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fundamental_theorem_of_Galois_theory#Infinite_case
%% https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eisenstein_series
%% https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Modular_lambda_function#Relations_to_other_functions
%% https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theta_function#Relation_to_the_Weierstrass_elliptic_function
%% https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Theta_function#Theta_series_of_a_Dirichlet_character
%% https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet_character#Applications
%% https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dirichlet_character#Classification_of_characters
%% Robin: G. Robin, J. Math. Pures Appl. 63 (1984), 187--213.
%% Lagarias: https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0008177 (Theorem 1.1 and Problem E).
%% Gamma nonvanishing: https://dlmf.nist.gov/5.2 ; L completion: https://dlmf.nist.gov/25.15
%% Hets/CASL semantics and views: https://hets.eu/ ; https://www.cofi.info/
%% Prior programme constructors recovered from Hexaholographic_Full_Goals_RH_GRH.het
%% version 23, Tesseract_Moonshine_Theta_Return.het version 17 and the native
%% shortest-proof development. Their actual constructions are instantiated here;
%% those earlier files are not required to parse or use this standalone source.


%% 2026-11-25 CONSTRUCTIVE JOIN: THE PROGRAMMES ACTUALLY COMPOSE
%%
%% HP25: ranked tight policy -> complete original proof -> minimum and source return.
%% PN25: exact dyadic paracollatz normalizer, archived matrix/dagger, all-cut return.
%% PG25: actual clean Fredkin--Toffoli word, 330*m gates at precision m.
%% PP25: full proof packet x coefficient port; normalization retains both returns.
%% PC25: constructed minimum chart route for the same literal source programme.
%% GT25: recovered guarded tomography constructor -> original Hit/Zero -> the
%%       original two-level Candidate minimum over ALL finite-path competitors.
%% BT25: fixed producer's actual paid bit clock, prefix reserve and polynomial
%%       composition; it never identifies proof grade with discovery work.
%%
%% Read the narrow endpoint matching the mathematical task. The aggregate is
%% an atlas, not a requirement that elementary ring proofs assume its analytics.
%% The same natural carrier, free-word machinery and matrix theory are reused.
%% PN25Scalar is a renamed scalar INSTANCE so dyadic ideals do not collapse
%% inside the separate complex field. Source proof payloads remain complete.
%%
%% CONCRETE POLYNOMIAL STATEMENT. For the precisely parsed guarded class Q_gt,
%% the recovered constructor builds its original Answer, with work <=C*(N+m+2)^60.
%% It generates its own finite observation algebra and queue reserve. For an
%% explicit polynomial M with m<=M(N), P(N)=C*(N+M(N)+2)^60 bounds the same fixed
%% producer at ORIGINAL input length N. The archived Candidate objective has
%% grade N+1 for a verified original hit and N+2 for literal fallback. Its actual
%% Hit/Zero-to-minimum wrapper preserves the certificate and has paid bound
%% P(N)+cW*(N+P(N)+1). The competitor family contains every finite original path;
%% there is no search-depth truncation. GT25_GuardedMinimumClock exposes this
%% specialization. The generic HP25 proof-tree constructor has its own policy,
%% acquisition and output-size account, as recorded by BT25.
%%
%% The specialized six-stream inverse costs 330*m logical reversible gates,
%% including compute/copy/uncompute. Its supplied input already has 6*m bits.
%% A fixed finite controller and sequential tapes give a constant simulation
%% overhead. The addressed Python circuit is an exact replay, with its own
%% serialization/interpreter cost. Wider source pipelines pay their actual
%% acquisition, setup, checking and readback in addition to normalization.
%%
%% CANONICITY. N is the explicit inverse of R=I-2U, hence its solution is unique.
%% All finite cuts commute; separation identifies the already constructed full
%% point. A minimum proof can have several representatives. Frame naturality
%% preserves the chosen complete proof; it does not identify distinct minima.
%% The six-channel U, its involution U^3, the Gram dagger and the S3 frame action
%% retain their separate definitions. Tropical grade, FOUR support and paid
%% runtime retain their separate observables and original source returns.
%%
%% STATUS. The guarded closed theorem is a recovered source proof, imported by
%% name and included verbatim in the embedded evidence archive. The new Hets
%% transfer and induction obligations have written derivations. Native static
%% analysis is reported separately from E refutations, symbolic certificates,
%% and executable checks. No RH/GRH premise is added to the original base atlas.

logic CASL

%% One free-word constructor and one fold/append theory, instantiated at
%% source operations, proof moves and Collatz branch words.
spec H24_WordCarrier =
  sort H24Letter
  free type H24Word ::= h24Nil | h24Cons(H24Letter;H24Word)
end
spec H24_Words = H24_WordCarrier then
  op h24Append : H24Word * H24Word -> H24Word
  forall a : H24Letter; u,v : H24Word
  . h24Append(h24Nil,v)=v %(h24_word_append_nil)%
  . h24Append(h24Cons(a,u),v)=h24Cons(a,h24Append(u,v)) %(h24_word_append_cons)%
then
  forall u,v,w : H24Word
  . h24Append(u,h24Nil)=u %(h24_word_right_identity)% %implied
  . h24Append(h24Append(u,v),w)=h24Append(u,h24Append(v,w))
      %(h24_word_associativity)% %implied
end
spec H24_WordAction = H24_Words then
  sort H24FoldValue
  ops h24Step : H24Letter * H24FoldValue -> H24FoldValue;
      h24Fold : H24Word * H24FoldValue -> H24FoldValue
  forall a : H24Letter; u : H24Word; x : H24FoldValue
  . h24Fold(h24Nil,x)=x %(h24_word_fold_nil)%
  . h24Fold(h24Cons(a,u),x)=h24Fold(u,h24Step(a,x)) %(h24_word_fold_cons)%
then
  forall u,v : H24Word; x : H24FoldValue
  . h24Fold(h24Append(u,v),x)=h24Fold(v,h24Fold(u,x))
      %(h24_fold_append_fusion)% %implied
end
%% Proofs are structural induction on the first word with all other
%% arguments arbitrary. Cons reduces to the induction hypothesis at Step(a,x).
%% This single induction supplies HC24 path composition and MG26 program fusion.

%% SHARED FOUNDATIONS. One declaration of each law; instances use CASL maps.
%% Equality/sections need no arithmetic. Ring laws do not imply a field.
spec H24_AdditiveGroup =
  sort H24Scalar
  ops h24Zero : H24Scalar;
      h24Add : H24Scalar * H24Scalar -> H24Scalar;
      h24Neg : H24Scalar -> H24Scalar
  forall x,y,z : H24Scalar
  . h24Add(h24Add(x,y),z)=h24Add(x,h24Add(y,z)) %(h24_add_associative)%
  . h24Add(x,y)=h24Add(y,x) %(h24_add_commutative)%
  . h24Add(x,h24Zero)=x %(h24_add_identity)%
  . h24Add(x,h24Neg(x))=h24Zero %(h24_add_inverse)%
end

spec H24_Ring = H24_AdditiveGroup then
  ops h24One : H24Scalar;
      h24Mul : H24Scalar * H24Scalar -> H24Scalar
  forall x,y,z : H24Scalar
  . h24Mul(h24Mul(x,y),z)=h24Mul(x,h24Mul(y,z)) %(h24_mul_associative)%
  . h24Mul(x,y)=h24Mul(y,x) %(h24_mul_commutative)%
  . h24Mul(x,h24One)=x %(h24_mul_identity)%
  . h24Mul(x,h24Add(y,z))=h24Add(h24Mul(x,y),h24Mul(x,z)) %(h24_distributive)%
end

%% A specified left inverse suffices for zero reflection; no global field.
spec H24_ZeroMonoid =
  sort H24M
  ops h24M0,h24M1 : H24M; h24Times : H24M * H24M -> H24M
  forall a,b,c : H24M
  . h24Times(h24Times(a,b),c)=h24Times(a,h24Times(b,c)) %(h24_monoid_associative)%
  . h24Times(h24M1,a)=a %(h24_monoid_left_identity)%
  . h24Times(a,h24M0)=h24M0 %(h24_monoid_right_zero)%
then
  forall a,b,x : H24M
  . h24Times(b,a)=h24M1 => (h24Times(a,x)=h24M0 <=> x=h24M0)
      %(h24_specified_left_inverse_reflects_zero)% %implied
end
%% Proof: x=1*x=(b*a)*x=b*(a*x); if a*x=0, the last term is b*0=0.

spec H24_Naturals =
  free type GC26Nat ::= GC26zero | GC26succ(GC26Nat)
  ops GC26nadd,GC26nmul,GC26npow : GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat;
      GC26pred : GC26Nat -> GC26Nat;
      GC26one,GC26two,GC26three,GC26four,h24Five,h24Six,h24Ten : GC26Nat
  . GC26one=GC26succ(GC26zero) %(GC26_one_definition)%
  . GC26two=GC26succ(GC26one) %(GC26_two_definition)%
  . GC26three=GC26succ(GC26two) %(GC26_three_definition)%
  . GC26four=GC26succ(GC26three) %(GC26_four_definition)%
  . h24Five=GC26succ(GC26four) %(h24_five_definition)%
  . h24Six=GC26succ(h24Five) %(h24_six_definition)%
  . h24Ten=GC26nmul(GC26two,h24Five) %(h24_ten_definition)%
  forall m,n : GC26Nat
  . GC26nadd(m,GC26zero)=m %(GC26_add_zero)%
  . GC26nadd(m,GC26succ(n))=GC26succ(GC26nadd(m,n)) %(GC26_add_step)%
  . GC26nmul(m,GC26zero)=GC26zero %(GC26_mul_zero)%
  . GC26nmul(m,GC26succ(n))=GC26nadd(GC26nmul(m,n),m) %(GC26_mul_step)%
  . GC26npow(m,GC26zero)=GC26one %(GC26_power_zero)%
  . GC26npow(m,GC26succ(n))=GC26nmul(GC26npow(m,n),m) %(GC26_power_step)%
  . GC26pred(GC26zero)=GC26zero %(GC26_predecessor_zero)%
  . GC26pred(GC26succ(n))=n %(GC26_predecessor_step)%
end

spec H24_NaturalOrder = H24_Naturals then
  preds GC26positive : GC26Nat;
        GC26divides,h24LeN,h24LtN : GC26Nat * GC26Nat
  forall m,n : GC26Nat
  . GC26positive(n) <=> not n=GC26zero %(GC26_positive_definition)%
  . GC26divides(m,n) <=> (exists k : GC26Nat . GC26nmul(m,k)=n)
      %(GC26_divisibility_definition)%
  . h24LeN(m,n) <=> (exists k : GC26Nat . GC26nadd(m,k)=n) %(h24_natural_order)%
  . h24LtN(m,n) <=> h24LeN(GC26succ(m),n) %(h24_natural_strict_order)%
end

%% The alternative left-recursive arithmetic clauses follow by induction.
spec H24_NaturalRecursionReturns = H24_Naturals then
  forall m,n : GC26Nat
  . GC26nadd(GC26zero,n)=n %(h24_add_left_zero)% %implied
  . GC26nadd(GC26succ(m),n)=GC26succ(GC26nadd(m,n)) %(h24_add_left_step)% %implied
  . GC26nmul(GC26zero,n)=GC26zero %(h24_mul_left_zero)% %implied
  . GC26nmul(GC26succ(m),n)=GC26nadd(n,GC26nmul(m,n)) %(h24_mul_left_step)% %implied
end

%% Retraction is the common return rule; the image need not exhaust Target.
spec H24_Retraction =
  sorts H24Source,H24Target
  ops h24Encode : H24Source -> H24Target; h24Decode : H24Target -> H24Source
  forall x : H24Source
  . h24Decode(h24Encode(x))=x %(h24_source_section)%
then
  forall x,y : H24Source
  . h24Encode(x)=h24Encode(y) => x=y %(h24_section_reflects_equality)% %implied
end

spec H24_EquivariantReturn = H24_Retraction then
  ops h24SourceMove : H24Source -> H24Source; h24TargetMove : H24Target -> H24Target
  forall x : H24Source
  . h24TargetMove(h24Encode(x))=h24Encode(h24SourceMove(x)) %(h24_intertwining)%
then
  forall x : H24Source
  . h24TargetMove(h24Encode(x))=h24Encode(x) <=> h24SourceMove(x)=x
      %(h24_equivariant_fixed_point_return)% %implied
end
%% Apply Decode to intertwining; source-section cancels the chart twice.

%% A three-object typed square. Only D_i E_i=id is used in composition.
spec H24_TranslationComposition =
  sorts H24X,H24Y,H24Z,H24NX,H24NY,H24NZ
  ops h24EX : H24X -> H24NX; h24DX : H24NX -> H24X;
      h24EY : H24Y -> H24NY; h24DY : H24NY -> H24Y;
      h24EZ : H24Z -> H24NZ; h24DZ : H24NZ -> H24Z;
      h24F : H24X -> H24Y; h24G : H24Y -> H24Z;
      h24Fsharp : H24NX -> H24NY; h24Gsharp : H24NY -> H24NZ;
      h24GFsharp : H24NX -> H24NZ
  forall x : H24X; y : H24Y; z : H24Z; nx : H24NX; ny : H24NY
  . h24DX(h24EX(x))=x %(h24_x_section)%
  . h24DY(h24EY(y))=y %(h24_y_section)%
  . h24DZ(h24EZ(z))=z %(h24_z_section)%
  . h24Fsharp(nx)=h24EY(h24F(h24DX(nx))) %(h24_f_sharp_definition)%
  . h24Gsharp(ny)=h24EZ(h24G(h24DY(ny))) %(h24_g_sharp_definition)%
  . h24GFsharp(nx)=h24EZ(h24G(h24F(h24DX(nx)))) %(h24_gf_sharp_definition)%
then
  forall x : H24X; nx : H24NX
  . h24DY(h24Fsharp(h24EX(x)))=h24F(x) %(h24_original_arrow_return)% %implied
  . h24Gsharp(h24Fsharp(nx))=h24GFsharp(nx) %(h24_middle_chart_cancellation)% %implied
  . h24DZ(h24Gsharp(h24Fsharp(h24EX(x))))=h24G(h24F(x))
      %(h24_original_composite_return)% %implied
end
%% Expand E_Z G D_Y E_Y F D_X and cancel D_Y E_Y at the SAME middle Y.

%% Shared tagged-function theorem: values and labels determine the source.
spec H24_TaggedInvolution =
  sorts H24Object,H24Index,H24Tag,H24Value
  ops h24Tag : H24Object -> H24Tag;
      h24Evaluate : H24Object * H24Index -> H24Value;
      h24ObjectDagger : H24Object -> H24Object;
      h24ValueDagger : H24Value -> H24Value
  forall a,b : H24Object; i : H24Index; v : H24Value
  . h24ValueDagger(h24ValueDagger(v))=v %(h24_value_involution)%
  . h24Tag(h24ObjectDagger(a))=h24Tag(a) %(h24_dagger_retains_tag)%
  . h24Evaluate(h24ObjectDagger(a),i)=h24ValueDagger(h24Evaluate(a,i))
      %(h24_evaluation_intertwines_dagger)%
  . h24Tag(a)=h24Tag(b) /\ (forall j : H24Index . h24Evaluate(a,j)=h24Evaluate(b,j))
      => a=b %(h24_tagged_extensionality)%
then
  forall a : H24Object
  . h24ObjectDagger(h24ObjectDagger(a))=a %(h24_tagged_dagger_return)% %implied
end

%% Minimal Boolean-group presentation; commutativity is a derived law.
spec H24_BooleanGroup =
  sort H24Bit
  ops h24Off : H24Bit; h24Xor : H24Bit * H24Bit -> H24Bit
  forall a,b,c : H24Bit
  . h24Xor(h24Xor(a,b),c)=h24Xor(a,h24Xor(b,c)) %(h24_xor_associative)%
  . h24Xor(a,h24Off)=a %(h24_xor_right_identity)%
  . h24Xor(a,a)=h24Off %(h24_xor_self_inverse)%
then
  forall a,b : H24Bit
  . h24Xor(h24Off,a)=a %(h24_xor_left_identity)% %implied
  . h24Xor(h24Xor(a,b),b)=a %(h24_xor_right_cancellation)% %implied
  . h24Xor(a,h24Xor(a,b))=b %(h24_xor_left_cancellation)% %implied
  . h24Xor(a,b)=h24Xor(b,a) %(h24_xor_commutativity_derived)% %implied
end
%% Left identity: (a+a)+a=a+(a+a)=a+0=a. The cancellation laws follow
%% by association. Since each element is its own inverse, (a+b)^-1=b+a
%% and (a+b)^-1=a+b; equivalently cancel a and b in (a+b)+(a+b)=0.

%% Preorders suffice for adjunction units/counits; equality needs antisymmetry.
spec H24_PreorderAdjunction =
  sorts H24A,H24B
  ops h24Left : H24A -> H24B; h24Right : H24B -> H24A
  preds h24BelowA : H24A * H24A; h24BelowB : H24B * H24B
  forall a,b,c : H24A; x,y,z : H24B
  . h24BelowA(a,a) %(h24_preorder_a_reflexive)%
  . h24BelowB(x,x) %(h24_preorder_b_reflexive)%
  . h24BelowA(a,b) /\ h24BelowA(b,c) => h24BelowA(a,c) %(h24_preorder_a_transitive)%
  . h24BelowB(x,y) /\ h24BelowB(y,z) => h24BelowB(x,z) %(h24_preorder_b_transitive)%
  . h24BelowB(h24Left(a),x) <=> h24BelowA(a,h24Right(x)) %(h24_adjunction)%
then
  forall a,b : H24A; x,y : H24B
  . h24BelowA(a,h24Right(h24Left(a))) %(h24_adjunction_unit)% %implied
  . h24BelowB(h24Left(h24Right(x)),x) %(h24_adjunction_counit)% %implied
  . h24BelowA(a,b) => h24BelowB(h24Left(a),h24Left(b)) %(h24_left_monotone)% %implied
  . h24BelowB(x,y) => h24BelowA(h24Right(x),h24Right(y)) %(h24_right_monotone)% %implied
end

spec H24_PosetAdjunction = H24_PreorderAdjunction then
  forall a,b : H24A; x,y : H24B
  . h24BelowA(a,b) /\ h24BelowA(b,a) => a=b %(h24_poset_a_antisymmetric)%
  . h24BelowB(x,y) /\ h24BelowB(y,x) => x=y %(h24_poset_b_antisymmetric)%
then
  forall a : H24A; x : H24B
  . h24Left(h24Right(h24Left(a)))=h24Left(a) %(h24_left_triple_return)% %implied
  . h24Right(h24Left(h24Right(x)))=h24Right(x) %(h24_right_triple_return)% %implied
  . h24Right(h24Left(h24Right(h24Left(a))))=h24Right(h24Left(a))
      %(h24_adjunction_closure_idempotent)% %implied
end
%% Unit/counit are adjunction instances of reflexivity. Transitivity gives
%% monotonicity. Unit at G(b) and G applied to counit give opposite orders;
%% antisymmetry gives GFG=G. Substitute b=F(a) for closure idempotence.

%% One natural-valued divisor fold serves Eisenstein coefficients and the
%% Robin/Lagarias arithmetic. Scalar coefficients are images of this fold.
spec H24_DivisorSums = H24_NaturalOrder then
  ops h24DivisorPrefix : GC26Nat * GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat;
      h24DivisorSum : GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat
  forall k,n,b : GC26Nat
  . h24DivisorPrefix(k,n,GC26zero)=GC26zero %(h24_divisor_fold_zero)%
  . GC26divides(GC26succ(b),n) => h24DivisorPrefix(k,n,GC26succ(b))=
      GC26nadd(h24DivisorPrefix(k,n,b),GC26npow(GC26succ(b),k))
      %(h24_divisor_fold_included)%
  . not GC26divides(GC26succ(b),n) =>
      h24DivisorPrefix(k,n,GC26succ(b))=h24DivisorPrefix(k,n,b)
      %(h24_divisor_fold_omitted)%
  . h24DivisorSum(k,n)=h24DivisorPrefix(k,n,n) %(h24_divisor_sum_definition)%
end
%% Simultaneous recursion on the bound b gives the finite positive-divisor
%% sum, including b=0. No infinite sum, analytic assumption or field is used.

%% =====================================================================
%% GC26: INFINITE GALOIS THEORY AND DIRICHLET CHARACTER CONSTRUCTORS
%% Primary sources:
%% https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BMI (Lemmas 9.22.1--3)
%% https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BML (Theorem 9.22.4)
%% https://math.mit.edu/classes/18.785/2019fa/LectureNotes18.pdf
%% https://kskedlaya.org/cft/sec_profinite.html
%% Every GC26_source_* sentence is an explicitly imported interface law.
%% A %implied sentence is a derived obligation, not an ATP proof receipt.
%% All definitions below use ordinary CASL semantics. Constructive written
%% proofs establish the stated returns relative to the supplied operations.
%% =====================================================================

spec GC26_GaloisConnection =
  sorts GC26Element, GC26Automorphism, GC26Subgroup, GC26Subfield
  ops GC26act : GC26Automorphism * GC26Element -> GC26Element;
      GC26fixed : GC26Subgroup -> GC26Subfield;
      GC26stabilizer : GC26Subfield -> GC26Subgroup;
      GC26algebraicClosure : GC26Subgroup -> GC26Subgroup
  preds GC26inGroup : GC26Automorphism * GC26Subgroup;
        GC26inField : GC26Element * GC26Subfield;
        GC26groupIncluded : GC26Subgroup * GC26Subgroup;
        GC26fieldIncluded,GC26fieldOppositeIncluded : GC26Subfield * GC26Subfield
  forall x : GC26Element; h : GC26Subgroup
  . GC26inField(x,GC26fixed(h)) <=>
      (forall g : GC26Automorphism . GC26inGroup(g,h) => GC26act(g,x) = x)
      %(GC26_fixed_definition)%
  forall g : GC26Automorphism; m : GC26Subfield
  . GC26inGroup(g,GC26stabilizer(m)) <=>
      (forall x : GC26Element . GC26inField(x,m) => GC26act(g,x) = x)
      %(GC26_stabilizer_definition)%
  forall h,k : GC26Subgroup
  . GC26groupIncluded(h,k) <=>
      (forall g : GC26Automorphism . GC26inGroup(g,h) => GC26inGroup(g,k))
      %(GC26_group_inclusion_definition)%
  . (forall g : GC26Automorphism . GC26inGroup(g,h) <=> GC26inGroup(g,k))
      => h = k %(GC26_subgroup_extensionality)%
  forall m,n : GC26Subfield
  . GC26fieldOppositeIncluded(m,n) <=> GC26fieldIncluded(n,m) %(h24_field_opposite_order)%
  . GC26fieldIncluded(m,n) <=>
      (forall x : GC26Element . GC26inField(x,m) => GC26inField(x,n))
      %(GC26_field_inclusion_definition)%
  . (forall x : GC26Element . GC26inField(x,m) <=> GC26inField(x,n))
      => m = n %(GC26_subfield_extensionality)%
  forall h : GC26Subgroup
  . GC26algebraicClosure(h) = GC26stabilizer(GC26fixed(h))
      %(GC26_algebraic_closure_definition)%
then
  forall h,k : GC26Subgroup; m,n : GC26Subfield
  . GC26groupIncluded(h,GC26stabilizer(m)) <=>
      GC26fieldIncluded(m,GC26fixed(h)) %(GC26_antitone_adjunction)% %implied
  . GC26groupIncluded(h,k) => GC26fieldIncluded(GC26fixed(k),GC26fixed(h))
      %(GC26_fixed_antitone)% %implied
  . GC26fieldIncluded(m,n) =>
      GC26groupIncluded(GC26stabilizer(n),GC26stabilizer(m))
      %(GC26_stabilizer_antitone)% %implied
  . GC26groupIncluded(h,GC26algebraicClosure(h))
      %(GC26_closure_extensive)% %implied
  . GC26fixed(GC26stabilizer(GC26fixed(h))) = GC26fixed(h)
      %(GC26_fixed_triple_return)% %implied
  . GC26stabilizer(GC26fixed(GC26stabilizer(m))) = GC26stabilizer(m)
      %(GC26_stabilizer_triple_return)% %implied
  . GC26algebraicClosure(GC26algebraicClosure(h)) = GC26algebraicClosure(h)
      %(GC26_closure_idempotent)% %implied
end
%% COMPLETE WRITTEN PROOF. The adjunction expands on either side to:
%% for every g in h and x in m, act(g,x)=x. Exchanging the two universal
%% binders gives both implications directly. Inclusion h<=k permits an
%% h-member to be used as a k-member; this proves fixed(k)<=fixed(h).
%% The same argument with fields proves the second antitonicity law.
%% Every h-member fixes fixed(h), giving extensivity. Every x in fixed(h)
%% is fixed by stabilizer(fixed(h)) by the stabilizer definition. Conversely,
%% h is included in that stabilizer, so fixed of that stabilizer is included
%% in fixed(h). Extensionality proves the first triple return. Interchanging
%% the two sides proves the second. Substitute fixed(h) in that second
%% return and unfold algebraicClosure to prove idempotence. No selection,
%% excluded-middle step, or enumeration is used in these arguments.

spec GC26_InfiniteGaloisSource = GC26_GaloisConnection then
  op GC26topologicalClosure : GC26Subgroup -> GC26Subgroup
  preds GC26closed, GC26open, GC26normal : GC26Subgroup;
        GC26finiteOverBase, GC26galoisOverBase : GC26Subfield
  forall h : GC26Subgroup
  . GC26closed(h) <=> GC26topologicalClosure(h) = h
      %(GC26_source_closed_fixed_point)%
  . GC26algebraicClosure(h) = GC26topologicalClosure(h)
      %(GC26_source_infinite_galois_subgroup_return)%
  . GC26closed(h) =>
      (GC26open(h) <=> GC26finiteOverBase(GC26fixed(h)))
      %(GC26_source_open_finite_correspondence)%
  . GC26closed(h) =>
      (GC26normal(h) <=> GC26galoisOverBase(GC26fixed(h)))
      %(GC26_source_normal_galois_correspondence)%
  forall m : GC26Subfield
  . GC26fixed(GC26stabilizer(m)) = m
      %(GC26_source_infinite_galois_field_return)%
then
  forall h : GC26Subgroup
  . GC26closed(h) => GC26stabilizer(GC26fixed(h)) = h
      %(GC26_closed_subgroup_exact_return)% %implied
  . GC26fixed(GC26topologicalClosure(h)) = GC26fixed(h)
      %(GC26_dense_subgroup_same_fixed_field)% %implied
end
%% IMPORT SCOPE. Interpret these sorts in a fixed algebraic Galois L/K,
%% with its Krull topology. The cited Stacks theorem supplies the source
%% laws; its full arbitrary-field proof is not claimed here to be free of
%% foundational extension/compactness assumptions. The preceding constructive
%% adjunction is separate. PROOF of the first return: substitute the source
%% closure equality and the defining equation for closed(h). For the second,
%% substitute topologicalClosure=stabilizer.fixed in the triple return.
%% A merely dense subgroup returns its closure, not itself. Quotient G/H
%% as a Galois group additionally requires H closed and normal.

spec GC26_CompatibleAutomorphismFamilies =
  sorts GC26Stage, GC26GlobalAuto, GC26Family, GC26FiniteAuto
  ops GC26restriction : GC26GlobalAuto * GC26Stage -> GC26FiniteAuto;
      GC26component : GC26Family * GC26Stage -> GC26FiniteAuto;
      GC26transition : GC26Stage * GC26Stage * GC26FiniteAuto -> GC26FiniteAuto;
      GC26encode : GC26GlobalAuto -> GC26Family;
      GC26decode : GC26Family -> GC26GlobalAuto
  pred GC26stageIncluded : GC26Stage * GC26Stage
  forall f : GC26Family; i,j : GC26Stage
  . GC26stageIncluded(i,j) => GC26component(f,i) =
      GC26transition(j,i,GC26component(f,j)) %(GC26_family_compatibility)%
  forall g : GC26GlobalAuto; i : GC26Stage
  . GC26component(GC26encode(g),i) = GC26restriction(g,i)
      %(GC26_encode_coordinate_definition)%
  forall f : GC26Family; i : GC26Stage
  . GC26restriction(GC26decode(f),i) = GC26component(f,i)
      %(GC26_source_union_reconstruction)%
  forall g,h : GC26GlobalAuto
  . (forall i : GC26Stage . GC26restriction(g,i) = GC26restriction(h,i))
      => g = h %(GC26_source_union_extensionality)%
  forall f,h : GC26Family
  . (forall i : GC26Stage . GC26component(f,i) = GC26component(h,i))
      => f = h %(GC26_family_extensionality)%
then
  forall g : GC26GlobalAuto
  . GC26decode(GC26encode(g)) = g %(GC26_inverse_limit_global_return)% %implied
  forall f : GC26Family
  . GC26encode(GC26decode(f)) = f %(GC26_inverse_limit_family_return)% %implied
end
%% CONSTRUCTION AND COMPLETE RETURN PROOF. For a presented directed union
%% L=colim E and a supplied compatible family (sigma_E), define sigma(x)
%% as sigma_E(x) for any stage E containing x. At a common larger stage,
%% compatibility makes every such value equal, hence this specifies one
%% function without making a global choice of stages. A common stage for
%% x,y proves additivity and multiplicativity. The compatible family of
%% inverse automorphisms constructs its inverse. This proves the displayed
%% source reconstruction law. Now restrict decode(encode(g)) to stage i:
%% reconstruction then the encode definition give restriction(g,i). Union
%% extensionality gives the first return. Component i of encode(decode(f))
%% equals restriction(decode(f),i)=component(f,i); family extensionality gives
%% the second. The interface presupposes the presented compatible-family
%% object; it does not manufacture a point from arbitrary nonempty fibres.

spec GC26_NaturalArithmetic = H24_NaturalOrder then
  op GC26gcd : GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat
end
%% gcd below is a source arithmetic operation with its usual interpretation.
%% None of the derived return proofs depends on an unlisted gcd identity.

%% Shared CHARACTER SIGNATURE: no CRT, primitive-root, or conductor theorem.
%% The tagged carrier contains characters of every positive modulus.
spec GC26_CharacterSignature = GC26_NaturalArithmetic then
  sorts GC26DirichletCharacter, GC26Integer
  ops GC26modulus,GC26conductor : GC26DirichletCharacter -> GC26Nat;
      GC26primitiveAncestor,GC26conjugateCharacter : GC26DirichletCharacter -> GC26DirichletCharacter;
      GC26induce : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26Nat -> GC26DirichletCharacter
  preds GC26primitive : GC26DirichletCharacter;
        GC26factorsAt : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26Nat
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter
  . GC26positive(GC26modulus(c)) %(GC26_source_positive_character_moduli)%
  . GC26primitive(c) <=> GC26conductor(c)=GC26modulus(c)
      %(GC26_primitive_definition)%
end
spec GC26_UnitGroupClassificationSource = GC26_NaturalArithmetic then
  sort GC26GroupPresentation
  ops GC26units,GC26cyclic : GC26Nat -> GC26GroupPresentation;
      GC26groupProduct : GC26GroupPresentation * GC26GroupPresentation
          -> GC26GroupPresentation
  preds GC26isomorphic : GC26GroupPresentation * GC26GroupPresentation;
        GC26oddPrime : GC26Nat
  forall m,n : GC26Nat
  . not m=GC26zero /\ not n=GC26zero /\ GC26gcd(m,n)=GC26one =>
      GC26isomorphic(GC26units(GC26nmul(m,n)),
        GC26groupProduct(GC26units(m),GC26units(n))) %(GC26_source_CRT_units)%
  forall p,e : GC26Nat
  . GC26oddPrime(p) => GC26isomorphic(GC26units(GC26npow(p,GC26succ(e))),
      GC26cyclic(GC26nmul(GC26npow(p,e),GC26pred(p))))
      %(GC26_source_odd_prime_power_units)%
  . GC26isomorphic(GC26units(GC26npow(GC26two,GC26nadd(e,GC26three))),
      GC26groupProduct(GC26cyclic(GC26two),
                       GC26cyclic(GC26npow(GC26two,GC26succ(e)))))
      %(GC26_source_dyadic_units)%
  . GC26isomorphic(GC26units(GC26two),GC26cyclic(GC26one))
      %(GC26_source_units_mod_two)%
  . GC26isomorphic(GC26units(GC26four),GC26cyclic(GC26two))
      %(GC26_source_units_mod_four)%
end

spec GC26_ConductorSource = GC26_CharacterSignature then
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter; f : GC26Nat
  . GC26positive(GC26conductor(c)) %(GC26_source_positive_conductor)%
  . GC26divides(GC26conductor(c),GC26modulus(c)) /\
      GC26factorsAt(c,GC26conductor(c)) %(GC26_source_conductor_factorization)%
  . GC26factorsAt(c,f) => GC26divides(GC26conductor(c),f)
      %(GC26_source_conductor_minimality)%
  . GC26modulus(GC26primitiveAncestor(c))=GC26conductor(c) /\
      GC26primitive(GC26primitiveAncestor(c)) %(GC26_source_primitive_ancestor)%
  . GC26induce(GC26primitiveAncestor(c),GC26modulus(c))=c
      %(GC26_source_induced_character_return)%
end
%% SOURCE INTERPRETATION. Character objects retain their specified modulus;
%% factorsAt(c,f) means f divides that modulus and the UNIT-GROUP homomorphism
%% descends under reduction. Equivalently it kills the reduction kernel.
%% The primitive ancestor has equal values on units of the original modulus;
%% its zero extension can differ on other integers. Induce is only meaningful
%% at multiples of its source conductor; the displayed return is in that domain.
%% CRT uses explicit Bezout coefficients. For a presentation U_q=product C_dj,
%% coordinates b_j determine chi_a(b)=product zeta_dj^(a_j*b_j). Roots of unity
%% can be stored as exact exponents modulo d_j. Hence coordinate addition gives
%% character multiplication, negation gives conjugation, and the generator
%% where two coordinate tuples differ supplies a separating witness. Odd prime
%% powers use a supplied primitive root; dyadic powers use -1 and 5. No claim
%% about polynomial-time factorization or discrete logarithms is made here.

%% The ring equations are written once, in H24_Ring. Conjugation and the
%% two independent scalar strengthenings are added only by their users.
spec GC26_ScalarInvolution = H24_Ring with
  H24Scalar |-> GC26Scalar, h24Zero |-> GC26sz, h24One |-> GC26so,
  h24Add |-> GC26sadd, h24Neg |-> GC26sneg, h24Mul |-> GC26smul
then
  op GC26conjugate : GC26Scalar -> GC26Scalar
  forall x,y : GC26Scalar
  . GC26conjugate(GC26conjugate(x))=x %(GC26_conjugation_involution)%
  . GC26conjugate(GC26smul(x,y))=
      GC26smul(GC26conjugate(x),GC26conjugate(y)) %(GC26_conjugation_multiplicative)%
  . GC26conjugate(GC26so)=GC26so %(GC26_conjugation_one)%
end

spec GC26_NontrivialScalars = GC26_ScalarInvolution then
  . not GC26sz=GC26so %(GC26_scalar_nontrivial)%
end

spec GC26_CharacterScalarDomain = GC26_NontrivialScalars then
  forall x,y : GC26Scalar
  . GC26smul(x,y)=GC26sz => x=GC26sz \/ y=GC26sz
      %(GC26_scalar_no_zero_divisors)%
end

spec GC26_DirichletValues =
  GC26_CharacterSignature and GC26_NontrivialScalars then
  ops GC26evaluate : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26Integer -> GC26Scalar;
      GC26iadd,GC26imul : GC26Integer * GC26Integer -> GC26Integer;
      GC26naturalInteger : GC26Nat -> GC26Integer;
      GC26integerGcd : GC26Integer * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat
  preds GC26isUnitAt : GC26Integer * GC26Nat;
        GC26congruent : GC26Integer * GC26Integer * GC26Nat
  forall a : GC26Integer; q : GC26Nat
  . GC26isUnitAt(a,q) <=> GC26integerGcd(a,q) = GC26one
      %(GC26_integer_unit_definition)%
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter; a,b : GC26Integer
  . GC26evaluate(c,GC26imul(a,b)) =
      GC26smul(GC26evaluate(c,a),GC26evaluate(c,b))
      %(GC26_dirichlet_multiplicativity)%
  . GC26evaluate(c,GC26iadd(a,GC26naturalInteger(GC26modulus(c)))) =
      GC26evaluate(c,a) %(GC26_dirichlet_periodicity)%
  . GC26evaluate(c,a) = GC26sz <=> not GC26isUnitAt(a,GC26modulus(c))
      %(GC26_dirichlet_zero_extension)%
  . GC26evaluate(c,GC26naturalInteger(GC26one)) = GC26so
      %(GC26_dirichlet_normalization)%
  . GC26evaluate(GC26conjugateCharacter(c),a) = GC26conjugate(GC26evaluate(c,a))
      %(GC26_character_conjugation_definition)%
  . GC26modulus(GC26conjugateCharacter(c)) = GC26modulus(c)
      %(GC26_character_conjugation_modulus)%
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter; f : GC26Nat
  . GC26factorsAt(c,f) <=>
      GC26positive(f) /\ GC26divides(f,GC26modulus(c)) /\
      (forall a,b : GC26Integer . GC26isUnitAt(a,GC26modulus(c)) /\
        GC26isUnitAt(b,GC26modulus(c)) /\ GC26congruent(a,b,f) =>
        GC26evaluate(c,a) = GC26evaluate(c,b)) %(GC26_factor_descent_definition)%
  forall c,d : GC26DirichletCharacter
  . GC26modulus(c) = GC26modulus(d) /\
      (forall a : GC26Integer . GC26evaluate(c,a) = GC26evaluate(d,a))
      => c = d %(GC26_modulus_tagged_character_extensionality)%
then
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter
  . GC26conjugateCharacter(GC26conjugateCharacter(c)) = c
      %(GC26_dirichlet_dagger_involution)% %implied
end

%% Conductor descent is an optional source extension, not a dependency of
%% character conjugation, coefficient twists, or finite-sum definitions.
spec GC26_ConductorValuesSource = GC26_DirichletValues and GC26_ConductorSource then
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter; a : GC26Integer
  . GC26isUnitAt(a,GC26modulus(c)) =>
      GC26evaluate(GC26primitiveAncestor(c),a)=GC26evaluate(c,a)
      %(GC26_source_ancestor_values_on_units)%
end

%% Public classification aggregate, explicitly imported by the full atlas.
spec GC26_UnitClassificationSource = GC26_UnitGroupClassificationSource and
  GC26_ConductorValuesSource
end
%% The integer operations, congruence and gcd have their standard arithmetic
%% interpretation; this interface does not construct that integer model.
%% COMPLETE PROOF: conjugation preserves the specified modulus twice. At
%% every integer a, applying the value definition twice gives conjugate of
%% conjugate of evaluate(c,a), hence evaluate(c,a). Tagged extensionality
%% proves the dagger involution. The factorsAt definition records descent of
%% the actual unit-group function; the source primitive-ancestor theorem
%% supplies the unique minimal modulus, rather than changing zero extensions.

spec GC26_FiniteCharacterFourierData = GC26_NontrivialScalars then
  sorts GC26Unit, GC26Character
  ops GC26value : GC26Character * GC26Unit -> GC26Scalar;
      GC26separatingCharacter : GC26Unit * GC26Unit -> GC26Character;
      GC26separatingUnit : GC26Character * GC26Character -> GC26Unit;
      GC26rowSum : GC26Unit * GC26Unit -> GC26Scalar;
      GC26columnSum : GC26Character * GC26Character -> GC26Scalar;
      GC26cardinality : GC26Scalar
  forall c,d : GC26Character; u,v : GC26Unit
  . GC26smul(GC26value(c,u),GC26conjugate(GC26value(c,u))) = GC26so
      %(GC26_source_character_unit_norm)%
  . not u = v => not GC26value(GC26separatingCharacter(u,v),u) =
      GC26value(GC26separatingCharacter(u,v),v) %(GC26_source_dual_separation)%
  . not c = d => not GC26value(c,GC26separatingUnit(c,d)) =
      GC26value(d,GC26separatingUnit(c,d)) %(GC26_source_character_separation)%
  . GC26rowSum(u,u) = GC26cardinality %(GC26_source_row_diagonal_sum)%
  . GC26columnSum(c,c) = GC26cardinality %(GC26_source_column_diagonal_sum)%
  . GC26smul(GC26smul(GC26value(c,u),GC26conjugate(GC26value(c,v))),
             GC26rowSum(u,v)) = GC26rowSum(u,v) %(GC26_source_dual_reindex_sum)%
  . GC26smul(GC26smul(GC26value(c,u),GC26conjugate(GC26value(d,u))),
             GC26columnSum(c,d)) = GC26columnSum(c,d) %(GC26_source_group_reindex_sum)%
end

%% Off-diagonal cancellation needs a domain, but no inverse group order.
spec GC26_FiniteCharacterOrthogonality = GC26_FiniteCharacterFourierData and
  GC26_CharacterScalarDomain then
  forall u,v : GC26Unit
  . not u=v => GC26rowSum(u,v)=GC26sz %(GC26_row_orthogonality)% %implied
  forall c,d : GC26Character
  . not c=d => GC26columnSum(c,d)=GC26sz %(GC26_column_orthogonality)% %implied
end

%% Diagonal normalization only needs the displayed unit; no domain law.
spec GC26_NormalizedFiniteCharacterFourier = GC26_FiniteCharacterFourierData then
  op GC26inverseCardinality : GC26Scalar
  . GC26smul(GC26inverseCardinality,GC26cardinality)=GC26so
      %(GC26_source_invertible_group_order)%
then
  forall u : GC26Unit
  . GC26smul(GC26inverseCardinality,GC26rowSum(u,u))=GC26so
      %(GC26_normalized_row_diagonal)% %implied
  forall c : GC26Character
  . GC26smul(GC26inverseCardinality,GC26columnSum(c,c))=GC26so
      %(GC26_normalized_column_diagonal)% %implied
end

spec GC26_FiniteCharacterFourierSource = GC26_FiniteCharacterOrthogonality and
  GC26_NormalizedFiniteCharacterFourier
end
%% EXACT FINITE-SUM INTERPRETATION AND COMPLETE PROOF.
%% rowSum(u,v)=sum_c value(c,u)*conjugate(value(c,v)); columnSum(c,d)
%% is the same summand summed over u. Their displayed source equations are
%% only finite-sum reindexing/counting interfaces, not orthogonality axioms.
%% Multiplying characters by a fixed character permutes the finite dual;
%% translating units by a fixed unit permutes the group. Distribute the sums
%% to obtain the two reindexing equations. Unit norm makes each diagonal
%% summand one. CRT cyclic coordinates identify both group cardinalities.
%% For u!=v choose the supplied c separating them. Put a=value(c,u),
%% b=value(c,v), S=rowSum(u,v), and t=a*conjugate(b). If t=1, multiplying
%% by b and using b*conjugate(b)=1 gives a=b, contradicting separation.
%% Reindexing gives t*S=S, hence (t-1)*S=0 by distributivity and additive
%% inverses. Since t-1!=0, no-zero-divisors gives S=0. This proves row
%% orthogonality. For c!=d use separatingUnit(c,d), interchange the two
%% finite sums, and repeat the identical argument for column orthogonality.
%% The diagonal normalized returns follow by substitution of the diagonal
%% sum equations into inverseCardinality*cardinality=1. Nondegeneracy and
%% denominator invertibility are explicit hypotheses. For complex character
%% values they hold; they cannot be silently transported to characteristic
%% dividing |U_q|. In particular dyadic normalization needs powers of 2
%% inverted; the integral unnormalized sum identities remain separate.

%% CONSTRUCTED CYCLIC COORDINATES: symbolic tuples, exact roots, CRT returns.
spec GC26_CyclotomicScalars = GC26_NaturalArithmetic and GC26_NontrivialScalars then
  ops GC26modN,GC26negativeModN : GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat;
      GC26root : GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Scalar
  forall d,a,b,c : GC26Nat
  . not d = GC26zero => GC26root(d,GC26modN(GC26zero,d)) = GC26so
      %(GC26_source_root_zero)%
  . not d = GC26zero => (GC26root(d,a) = GC26root(d,b) <=>
      GC26modN(a,d) = GC26modN(b,d)) %(GC26_source_root_primitive_exactness)%
  . not d = GC26zero =>
      GC26root(d,GC26modN(GC26nmul(GC26modN(GC26nadd(a,b),d),c),d)) =
      GC26smul(GC26root(d,GC26modN(GC26nmul(a,c),d)),
               GC26root(d,GC26modN(GC26nmul(b,c),d)))
      %(GC26_source_root_addition_modulus)%
  . not d = GC26zero =>
      GC26root(d,GC26modN(GC26nmul(GC26negativeModN(a,d),b),d)) =
      GC26conjugate(GC26root(d,GC26modN(GC26nmul(a,b),d)))
      %(GC26_source_root_negation_modulus)%
end

spec GC26_CyclicCoordinateWords = GC26_CyclotomicScalars then
  free type GC26CoordWord ::= GC26coordNil |
      GC26coordCons(GC26Nat;GC26Nat;GC26CoordWord)
  ops GC26coordAdd : GC26CoordWord * GC26CoordWord -> GC26CoordWord;
      GC26coordNeg : GC26CoordWord -> GC26CoordWord;
      GC26coordEvaluate : GC26CoordWord * GC26CoordWord -> GC26Scalar
  pred GC26sameShape : GC26CoordWord * GC26CoordWord
  . GC26sameShape(GC26coordNil,GC26coordNil) %(GC26_shape_base)%
  forall d,a : GC26Nat; w : GC26CoordWord
  . not GC26sameShape(GC26coordNil,GC26coordCons(d,a,w)) %(GC26_shape_left_end)%
  . not GC26sameShape(GC26coordCons(d,a,w),GC26coordNil) %(GC26_shape_right_end)%
  forall d,e,a,b : GC26Nat; w,v : GC26CoordWord
  . GC26sameShape(GC26coordCons(d,a,w),GC26coordCons(e,b,v)) <=>
      d = e /\ not d = GC26zero /\ GC26sameShape(w,v) %(GC26_shape_step)%
  . GC26coordAdd(GC26coordNil,GC26coordNil) = GC26coordNil %(GC26_coord_add_base)%
  . GC26coordAdd(GC26coordCons(d,a,w),GC26coordCons(d,b,v)) =
      GC26coordCons(d,GC26modN(GC26nadd(a,b),d),GC26coordAdd(w,v))
      %(GC26_coord_add_step)%
  . GC26coordNeg(GC26coordNil) = GC26coordNil %(GC26_coord_neg_base)%
  . GC26coordNeg(GC26coordCons(d,a,w)) =
      GC26coordCons(d,GC26negativeModN(a,d),GC26coordNeg(w)) %(GC26_coord_neg_step)%
  . GC26coordEvaluate(GC26coordNil,GC26coordNil) = GC26so %(GC26_coord_eval_base)%
  . GC26coordEvaluate(GC26coordCons(d,a,w),GC26coordCons(d,b,v)) =
      GC26smul(GC26root(d,GC26modN(GC26nmul(a,b),d)),GC26coordEvaluate(w,v))
      %(GC26_coord_eval_step)%

then
  forall a,b,x : GC26CoordWord
  . GC26sameShape(a,x) /\ GC26sameShape(b,x) =>
      GC26coordEvaluate(GC26coordAdd(a,b),x) =
      GC26smul(GC26coordEvaluate(a,x),GC26coordEvaluate(b,x))
      %(GC26_coordinate_character_multiplication)% %implied
  . GC26sameShape(a,x) => GC26coordEvaluate(GC26coordNeg(a),x) =
      GC26conjugate(GC26coordEvaluate(a,x))
      %(GC26_coordinate_character_conjugation)% %implied
end
%% EXACT SOURCE INTERPRETATION: modN(a,d) is the least residue, negativeModN
%% is the residue of -a, and root(d,r)=exp(2*pi*i*r/d) for d>0. Equivalently
%% store (d,r mod d) as an exact root-of-unity name. The imported root laws
%% follow by adding/negating exponents and primitive-root uniqueness; no floating
%% approximation occurs. Their d>0 guards leave totalized d=0 values unused.
%% COMPLETE PROOF: induct on the finite coordinate word. At nil, both claims
%% are 1=1*1 and 1=conjugate(1). The sameShape premises expose identical
%% positive head moduli. Apply the appropriate root law at that head and the
%% induction hypothesis to the tails. Commutativity/associativity multiply
%% the two products in the first claim; multiplicative conjugation combines
%% the factors in the second. This proves all lengths, not a table of cases.

spec GC26_PresentedCRTCharacterReturn = GC26_CyclicCoordinateWords then
  sorts GC26PresentedUnit, GC26PresentedCharacter
  ops GC26unitCoordinates : GC26PresentedUnit -> GC26CoordWord;
      GC26unitFromCoordinates : GC26CoordWord -> GC26PresentedUnit;
      GC26characterCoordinates : GC26PresentedCharacter -> GC26CoordWord;
      GC26characterFromCoordinates : GC26CoordWord -> GC26PresentedCharacter;
      GC26presentedValue : GC26PresentedCharacter * GC26PresentedUnit -> GC26Scalar;
      GC26coordinateShape : GC26CoordWord
  preds GC26admissible,GC26normalized : GC26CoordWord
  . GC26normalized(GC26coordNil) %(GC26_coordinate_normalized_base)%
  forall d,a : GC26Nat; w : GC26CoordWord
  . GC26normalized(GC26coordCons(d,a,w)) <=>
      not d = GC26zero /\ GC26modN(a,d) = a /\ GC26normalized(w)
      %(GC26_coordinate_normalized_step)%
  forall w : GC26CoordWord
  . GC26admissible(w) <=> GC26sameShape(w,GC26coordinateShape) /\ GC26normalized(w)
      %(GC26_admissible_coordinate_definition)%
  forall u,v : GC26PresentedUnit
  . GC26admissible(GC26unitCoordinates(u)) %(GC26_source_unit_coordinate_domain)%
  . GC26unitCoordinates(u) = GC26unitCoordinates(v) => u = v
      %(GC26_source_CRT_coordinate_separation)%
  forall c,d : GC26PresentedCharacter
  . GC26admissible(GC26characterCoordinates(c)) %(GC26_source_character_coordinate_domain)%
  . GC26characterCoordinates(c) = GC26characterCoordinates(d) => c = d
      %(GC26_source_generator_value_separation)%
  forall w : GC26CoordWord
  . GC26admissible(w) => GC26unitCoordinates(GC26unitFromCoordinates(w)) = w
      %(GC26_source_explicit_CRT_section)%
  . GC26admissible(w) =>
      GC26characterCoordinates(GC26characterFromCoordinates(w)) = w
      %(GC26_source_explicit_character_section)%
  forall c : GC26PresentedCharacter; u : GC26PresentedUnit
  . GC26presentedValue(c,u) =
      GC26coordEvaluate(GC26characterCoordinates(c),GC26unitCoordinates(u))
      %(GC26_presented_character_value_definition)%
then
  forall u : GC26PresentedUnit
  . GC26unitFromCoordinates(GC26unitCoordinates(u)) = u
      %(GC26_CRT_original_unit_return)% %implied
  forall c : GC26PresentedCharacter
  . GC26characterFromCoordinates(GC26characterCoordinates(c)) = c
      %(GC26_generator_original_character_return)% %implied
end
%% A fixed factorization q and fixed local generators determine admissible:
%% words with precisely the cyclic factor orders d_j and residues 0<=a_j<d_j.
%% For coprime local moduli m_j, put M_j=q/m_j and choose supplied Bezout
%% inverses t_j with M_j*t_j=1 mod m_j. UnitFromCoordinates is the least
%% residue of sum_j (g_j^a_j)*M_j*t_j mod q (combining the -1 and 5 factors
%% within the dyadic local component first). Reducing modulo each m_j proves
%% its section equation. CharacterFromCoordinates uses the displayed product
%% of root values, and evaluation on generators proves its section equation.
%% COMPLETE RETURN PROOF: encoded u is admissible, so encode(decode(encode(u)))
%% equals encode(u). Coordinate separation gives decode(encode(u))=u. Apply
%% the same argument to the character maps. Thus both actual original objects
%% return; an isomorphism predicate alone is not being used as their decoder.


%% A FIXED-MODULUS CONNECTION TO THE GLOBAL CHARACTER CARRIER.
%% The presentation is chosen from a factorization of q with local unit
%% generators. q is not the product of the cyclic orders (that is phi(q)).
%% Sources: the CRT and character-generator construction described above.
%% All reverse operations outside the displayed modulus guard are unused.
spec GC26_PresentedDirichletConnection = GC26_PresentedCRTCharacterReturn and
  GC26_DirichletValues then
  op GC26presentationModulus : GC26Nat
  ops GC26presentedToDirichlet : GC26PresentedCharacter -> GC26DirichletCharacter;
      GC26dirichletToPresented : GC26DirichletCharacter -> GC26PresentedCharacter;
      GC26integerToPresentedUnit : GC26Integer -> GC26PresentedUnit
  . GC26positive(GC26presentationModulus) %(GC26_fixed_presentation_positive_modulus)%
  forall p : GC26PresentedCharacter; a : GC26Integer
  . GC26modulus(GC26presentedToDirichlet(p))=GC26presentationModulus
      %(GC26_presented_global_modulus_definition)%
  . GC26isUnitAt(a,GC26presentationModulus) =>
      GC26evaluate(GC26presentedToDirichlet(p),a)=
      GC26presentedValue(p,GC26integerToPresentedUnit(a))
      %(GC26_presented_global_value_definition)%
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter; a : GC26Integer
  . GC26modulus(c)=GC26presentationModulus /\ GC26isUnitAt(a,GC26presentationModulus) =>
      GC26presentedValue(GC26dirichletToPresented(c),GC26integerToPresentedUnit(a))=
      GC26evaluate(c,a) %(GC26_source_generator_coordinates_of_global_character)%
  forall u : GC26PresentedUnit
  . exists a : GC26Integer . GC26isUnitAt(a,GC26presentationModulus) /\
      GC26integerToPresentedUnit(a)=u %(GC26_source_presented_unit_residue_coverage)%
  forall p,r : GC26PresentedCharacter
  . (forall u : GC26PresentedUnit . GC26presentedValue(p,u)=GC26presentedValue(r,u)) => p=r
      %(GC26_source_presented_value_separation)%
then
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter
  . GC26modulus(c)=GC26presentationModulus =>
      GC26presentedToDirichlet(GC26dirichletToPresented(c))=c
      %(GC26_global_character_from_presentation_return)% %implied
  forall p : GC26PresentedCharacter
  . GC26dirichletToPresented(GC26presentedToDirichlet(p))=p
      %(GC26_presented_character_from_global_return)% %implied
end
%% COMPLETE VALUE-TRANSPORT PROOF. The first composite has the original
%% modulus. At a unit, its two value equations give evaluate(c,a); at a
%% nonunit, both zero extensions vanish. Tagged extensionality returns c.
%% For the second composite, take any presented unit u. Residue coverage
%% supplies a unit integer a with that image. The same two value equations
%% show equality at u, and presented-value separation returns p. No value
%% at a different modulus is identified, and no arbitrary fibre is chosen.

%% DA26. DIRICHLET CLASSIFICATION AND EVERY NAMED CHARACTER APPLICATION
%% Source laws are explicitly marked source_*; %implied labels are goals.
%% K. Conrad, Gauss and Jacobi sums, Theorems 2.4, 3.12, 3.15:
%% https://kconrad.math.uconn.edu/blurbs/gradnumthy/Gauss-Jacobi-sums.pdf
%% W. Stein, Modular Forms: A Computational Approach, chapter 4:
%% https://wstein.org/books/modform/modform/dirichlet_characters.html
%% N. Koblitz, Introduction to Elliptic Curves and Modular Forms, III.3.17;
%% also Getz--Goresky, section 5.11: https://www.math.ias.edu/~goresky/pdf/Master.pdf
%% R. Evans, Fourier Coefficients of Hecke Eigenforms, introduction:
%% https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~revans/eigen.pdf (twisted Kloosterman convention).

%% Product values alone support coefficientwise twist composition.
spec DA26_CharacterProduct = GC26_DirichletValues then
  op DA26product : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26DirichletCharacter -> GC26DirichletCharacter
  forall c,d : GC26DirichletCharacter; a : GC26Integer
  . GC26modulus(c)=GC26modulus(d) => GC26modulus(DA26product(c,d))=GC26modulus(c)
      %(DA26_product_modulus_definition)%
  . GC26modulus(c)=GC26modulus(d) => GC26evaluate(DA26product(c,d),a)=
      GC26smul(GC26evaluate(c,a),GC26evaluate(d,a)) %(DA26_product_value_definition)%
end

spec DA26_CharacterKinds = DA26_CharacterProduct then
  ops DA26ineg : GC26Integer -> GC26Integer;
      DA26iabs : GC26Integer -> GC26Nat;
      DA26minusOne : GC26Integer;
      DA26scalarPower : GC26Scalar * GC26Nat -> GC26Scalar;
      DA26order : GC26DirichletCharacter -> GC26Nat
  preds DA26principal,DA26even,DA26odd,DA26real : GC26DirichletCharacter;
        DA26orderDivides : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26Nat;
        DA26prime : GC26Nat
  . DA26minusOne=DA26ineg(GC26naturalInteger(GC26one)) %(DA26_minus_one_definition)%
  forall x : GC26Scalar; k : GC26Nat
  . DA26scalarPower(x,GC26zero)=GC26so %(DA26_scalar_power_base)%
  . DA26scalarPower(x,GC26succ(k))=GC26smul(DA26scalarPower(x,k),x)
      %(DA26_scalar_power_step)%
  forall p : GC26Nat
  . DA26prime(p) <=> not p=GC26zero /\ not p=GC26one /\
      (forall d : GC26Nat . GC26divides(d,p) => d=GC26one \/ d=p)
      %(DA26_prime_definition)%
  forall c,d : GC26DirichletCharacter; a : GC26Integer; k : GC26Nat
  . DA26principal(c) <=> (forall n : GC26Integer .
      GC26isUnitAt(n,GC26modulus(c)) => GC26evaluate(c,n)=GC26so)
      %(DA26_principal_definition)%
  . DA26even(c) <=> GC26evaluate(c,DA26minusOne)=GC26so %(DA26_even_definition)%
  . DA26odd(c) <=> GC26evaluate(c,DA26minusOne)=GC26sneg(GC26so) %(DA26_odd_definition)%
  . DA26real(c) <=> (forall n : GC26Integer . GC26evaluate(c,n)=GC26sz \/
      GC26evaluate(c,n)=GC26so \/ GC26evaluate(c,n)=GC26sneg(GC26so))
      %(DA26_real_character_definition)%
  . DA26orderDivides(c,k) <=> GC26positive(k) /\ (forall n : GC26Integer .
      GC26isUnitAt(n,GC26modulus(c)) => DA26scalarPower(GC26evaluate(c,n),k)=GC26so)
      %(DA26_order_divisor_definition)%

end

%% Finite order and real-character classification are an optional source
%% extension. Their domain hypotheses do not enter twist composition.
spec DA26_CharacterOrderSource = DA26_CharacterKinds and GC26_CharacterScalarDomain then
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter; k : GC26Nat
  . GC26positive(DA26order(c)) /\
      (DA26orderDivides(c,k) <=> GC26positive(k) /\ GC26divides(DA26order(c),k))
      %(DA26_source_finite_character_order)%
  . DA26real(c) <=> DA26orderDivides(c,GC26two) %(DA26_source_real_order_at_most_two)%
end
%% Integer negation/absolute value retain their standard arithmetic meaning.
%% These are complex-valued characters: "real" means values in {0,1,-1}.
%% The order is the least positive exponent killing all unit values. Finite
%% cyclic coordinates construct it as the lcm of the local character orders.

spec DA26_FiniteCharacterSums = DA26_CharacterKinds and GC26_CyclotomicScalars then
  ops DA26phase,DA26unitInverse : GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat;
      DA26additivePhase : GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Scalar;
      DA26gaussPrefix : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26Nat -> GC26Scalar;
      DA26gauss : GC26DirichletCharacter -> GC26Scalar;
      DA26jacobiPrefix : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26Nat -> GC26Scalar;
      DA26jacobi : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26DirichletCharacter -> GC26Scalar;
      DA26kloostermanPrefix : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26Nat * GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Scalar;
      DA26kloosterman : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Scalar
  forall q,a : GC26Nat
  . DA26phase(q,a)=GC26modN(a,q) %(DA26_phase_residue_definition)%
  . DA26additivePhase(q,a)=GC26root(q,DA26phase(q,a)) %(DA26_additive_phase_definition)%
  . GC26positive(q) /\ GC26isUnitAt(GC26naturalInteger(a),q) =>
      GC26modN(GC26nmul(a,DA26unitInverse(a,q)),q)=GC26modN(GC26one,q)
      %(DA26_source_modular_inverse)%
  forall c,d : GC26DirichletCharacter; m,n,k : GC26Nat
  . DA26gaussPrefix(c,GC26zero)=GC26sz %(DA26_gauss_base)%
  . DA26gaussPrefix(c,GC26succ(k))=GC26sadd(DA26gaussPrefix(c,k),
      GC26smul(GC26evaluate(c,GC26naturalInteger(k)),DA26additivePhase(GC26modulus(c),k)))
      %(DA26_gauss_step)%
  . DA26gauss(c)=DA26gaussPrefix(c,GC26modulus(c)) %(DA26_gauss_definition)%
  . DA26jacobiPrefix(c,d,GC26zero)=GC26sz %(DA26_jacobi_base)%
  . DA26jacobiPrefix(c,d,GC26succ(k))=GC26sadd(DA26jacobiPrefix(c,d,k),
      GC26smul(GC26evaluate(c,GC26naturalInteger(k)),
        GC26evaluate(d,GC26iadd(GC26naturalInteger(GC26one),DA26ineg(GC26naturalInteger(k))))))
      %(DA26_jacobi_step)%
  . DA26jacobi(c,d)=DA26jacobiPrefix(c,d,GC26modulus(c)) %(DA26_jacobi_definition)%
  . DA26kloostermanPrefix(c,m,n,GC26zero)=GC26sz %(DA26_kloosterman_base)%
  . GC26isUnitAt(GC26naturalInteger(k),GC26modulus(c)) =>
      DA26kloostermanPrefix(c,m,n,GC26succ(k))=GC26sadd(DA26kloostermanPrefix(c,m,n,k),
        GC26smul(GC26evaluate(c,GC26naturalInteger(k)),
          DA26additivePhase(GC26modulus(c),GC26nadd(GC26nmul(m,k),
            GC26nmul(n,DA26unitInverse(k,GC26modulus(c)))))))
      %(DA26_kloosterman_unit_step)%
  . not GC26isUnitAt(GC26naturalInteger(k),GC26modulus(c)) =>
      DA26kloostermanPrefix(c,m,n,GC26succ(k))=DA26kloostermanPrefix(c,m,n,k)
      %(DA26_kloosterman_nonunit_step)%
  . DA26kloosterman(c,m,n)=DA26kloostermanPrefix(c,m,n,GC26modulus(c))
      %(DA26_kloosterman_definition)%
end
%% Prefix k is exactly the sum over 0<=a<k, proved by structural induction.
%% Thus G(c)=sum_a c(a)e(a/q), J(c,d)=sum_a c(a)d(1-a) for equal moduli q,
%% K_c(m,n;q)=sum_(a mod q)^* c(a)e((ma+n*a^{-1})/q). The twist is c(a),
%% not its conjugate; this fixes the convention. Natural m,n represent all
%% residue classes, so integer parameters are first reduced modulo q.
%% These recursions define the sums, rather than finite tests of identities.

spec DA26_GaussJacobiSource = DA26_FiniteCharacterSums then
  op DA26inverse : GC26Scalar -> GC26Scalar
  forall c,d : GC26DirichletCharacter
  . GC26primitive(c) => GC26smul(DA26gauss(c),DA26inverse(DA26gauss(c)))=GC26so
      %(DA26_source_primitive_gauss_unit)%
  . GC26modulus(c)=GC26modulus(d) /\ GC26primitive(DA26product(c,d)) /\
      not DA26principal(DA26product(c,d)) =>
      GC26smul(DA26gauss(c),DA26gauss(d))=
      GC26smul(DA26jacobi(c,d),DA26gauss(DA26product(c,d)))
      %(DA26_source_primitive_product_Gauss_Jacobi)%
  . GC26modulus(c)=GC26modulus(d) /\ DA26prime(GC26modulus(c)) /\
      not DA26principal(DA26product(c,d)) =>
      GC26smul(DA26gauss(c),DA26gauss(d))=
      GC26smul(DA26jacobi(c,d),DA26gauss(DA26product(c,d)))
      %(DA26_source_prime_nontrivial_product_Gauss_Jacobi)%
then
  forall c,d : GC26DirichletCharacter
  . GC26modulus(c)=GC26modulus(d) /\ GC26primitive(DA26product(c,d)) /\
      not DA26principal(DA26product(c,d)) => DA26jacobi(c,d)=
      GC26smul(GC26smul(DA26gauss(c),DA26gauss(d)),DA26inverse(DA26gauss(DA26product(c,d))))
      %(DA26_Jacobi_quotient_return)% %implied
end
%% COMPLETE RETURN PROOF: the product character is primitive, so its Gauss
%% sum has the displayed inverse. Multiply the imported cross-multiplied
%% factorization by that inverse and use associativity. Only this Gauss unit
%% is required; no field axiom or global inverse law is assumed. The nonprincipal-product
%% guard is retained; no degenerate product identity is inferred.

spec DA26_ModularTwistSource = DA26_CharacterProduct then
  sort DA26Form
  ops DA26coefficient : DA26Form * GC26Nat -> GC26Scalar;
      DA26twist : DA26Form * GC26DirichletCharacter -> DA26Form;
      DA26twistLevel : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26DirichletCharacter -> GC26Nat;
      DA26twistNebentypus : GC26DirichletCharacter * GC26DirichletCharacter -> GC26DirichletCharacter
  preds DA26modularAt,DA26cuspAt : DA26Form * GC26Nat * GC26Nat * GC26DirichletCharacter
  forall f : DA26Form; c,p : GC26DirichletCharacter; k,n,m : GC26Nat; a : GC26Integer
  . DA26coefficient(DA26twist(f,p),n)=
      GC26smul(GC26evaluate(p,GC26naturalInteger(n)),DA26coefficient(f,n))
      %(DA26_coefficient_twist_definition)%
  . DA26twistLevel(c,p)=GC26nmul(GC26modulus(c),GC26npow(GC26modulus(p),GC26two))
      %(DA26_twist_ambient_level_definition)%
  . DA26twistNebentypus(c,p)=DA26product(GC26induce(c,DA26twistLevel(c,p)),
      DA26product(GC26induce(p,DA26twistLevel(c,p)),GC26induce(p,DA26twistLevel(c,p))))
      %(DA26_twist_nebentypus_definition)%
  . GC26positive(m) /\ GC26divides(GC26modulus(c),m) =>
      GC26modulus(GC26induce(c,m))=m %(DA26_source_induced_modulus)%
  . GC26positive(m) /\ GC26divides(GC26modulus(c),m) /\ GC26isUnitAt(a,m) =>
      GC26evaluate(GC26induce(c,m),a)=GC26evaluate(c,a) %(DA26_source_induced_unit_values)%
  . DA26cuspAt(f,k,m,c) => DA26modularAt(f,k,m,c) %(DA26_source_cusp_is_modular)%
  . GC26positive(k) /\ GC26modulus(c)=m /\ GC26primitive(p) /\ DA26modularAt(f,k,m,c) =>
      DA26modularAt(DA26twist(f,p),k,DA26twistLevel(c,p),DA26twistNebentypus(c,p))
      %(DA26_source_twist_level_and_character)%
  . GC26positive(k) /\ GC26modulus(c)=m /\ GC26primitive(p) /\ DA26cuspAt(f,k,m,c) =>
      DA26cuspAt(DA26twist(f,p),k,DA26twistLevel(c,p),DA26twistNebentypus(c,p))
      %(DA26_source_twist_preserves_cusps)%
then
  forall f : DA26Form; c,d : GC26DirichletCharacter; n : GC26Nat
  . GC26modulus(c)=GC26modulus(d) =>
      DA26coefficient(DA26twist(DA26twist(f,c),d),n)=
      DA26coefficient(DA26twist(f,DA26product(c,d)),n)
      %(DA26_twist_composition_coefficient_return)% %implied
end
%% M=modulus(c), N=conductor(p)=modulus(p), k positive integral. MN^2 is an
%% allowed level, not necessarily the minimal level of the twisted form.
%% The new character is the induced c*p^2 at that SAME level. For primitive
%% p, f_p(z)=G(bar(p))^{-1} sum_a bar(p)(a)f(z+a/N). The finite Gauss identity
%% proves the coefficient formula, rational translations give the level law,
%% and every translated cusp form still vanishes at every rational cusp.
%% COMPLETE COMPOSITION PROOF: expand both twists; their coefficient is
%% d(n)*(c(n)*a_n)=(c(n)*d(n))*a_n by commutativity/associativity. The product
%% character definition identifies the last expression with the other side.

spec DA26_KroneckerClassificationSource = DA26_CharacterKinds then
  ops DA26kronecker : GC26Integer * GC26Integer -> GC26Scalar;
      DA26quadraticCharacter : GC26Integer -> GC26DirichletCharacter;
      DA26discriminant : GC26DirichletCharacter -> GC26Integer
  preds DA26squarefree : GC26Nat;
        DA26fundamentalDiscriminant : GC26Integer
  forall n : GC26Nat
  . DA26squarefree(n) <=> GC26positive(n) /\ (forall p : GC26Nat .
      DA26prime(p) => not GC26divides(GC26nmul(p,p),n)) %(DA26_squarefree_definition)%
  forall d : GC26Integer
  . DA26fundamentalDiscriminant(d) <=>
      (DA26squarefree(DA26iabs(d)) /\ GC26congruent(d,GC26naturalInteger(GC26one),GC26four)) \/
      (exists u : GC26Integer . d=GC26imul(GC26naturalInteger(GC26four),u) /\
        DA26squarefree(DA26iabs(u)) /\ (GC26congruent(u,GC26naturalInteger(GC26two),GC26four) \/
          GC26congruent(u,GC26naturalInteger(GC26three),GC26four)))
      %(DA26_fundamental_discriminant_definition)%
  . DA26fundamentalDiscriminant(d) => GC26primitive(DA26quadraticCharacter(d)) /\
      DA26real(DA26quadraticCharacter(d)) /\ GC26modulus(DA26quadraticCharacter(d))=DA26iabs(d)
      %(DA26_source_Kronecker_primitive_conductor)%
  forall d,n : GC26Integer
  . DA26fundamentalDiscriminant(d) =>
      GC26evaluate(DA26quadraticCharacter(d),n)=DA26kronecker(d,n)
      %(DA26_Kronecker_character_definition)%
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter; n : GC26Integer
  . GC26primitive(c) /\ DA26real(c) => DA26fundamentalDiscriminant(DA26discriminant(c)) /\
      DA26iabs(DA26discriminant(c))=GC26modulus(c) %(DA26_source_real_primitive_classification)%
  . GC26primitive(c) /\ DA26real(c) => GC26evaluate(c,n)=DA26kronecker(DA26discriminant(c),n)
      %(DA26_source_real_character_Kronecker_values)%
  forall d,e : GC26Integer
  . DA26fundamentalDiscriminant(d) /\ DA26fundamentalDiscriminant(e) /\
      (forall n : GC26Integer . DA26kronecker(d,n)=DA26kronecker(e,n)) => d=e
      %(DA26_source_fundamental_discriminant_uniqueness)%
then
  forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter
  . GC26primitive(c) /\ DA26real(c) => DA26quadraticCharacter(DA26discriminant(c))=c
      %(DA26_original_real_character_return)% %implied
end
%% Convention includes D=1, the conductor-one trivial character. Other D give
%% quadratic fields. Kronecker(D,n) is the standard symbol, with sign and
%% dyadic factors retained. Source: Stein chapter 4; official PARI's
%% znchartokronecker documentation also records the primitive/fundamental iff:
%% https://pari.math.u-bordeaux.fr/dochtml/html-stable/__backslashZslashNbackslashZ__star__and_Dirichlet_characters.html
%% COMPLETE RETURN PROOF: classification supplies fundamental D and |D|=q.
%% The constructed character therefore has modulus q and the same value at
%% every integer n. GC26 modulus-tagged extensionality returns the original c.

spec DA26_AllDirichletApplications = DA26_CharacterOrderSource and DA26_GaussJacobiSource and
  DA26_ModularTwistSource and DA26_KroneckerClassificationSource
end

%% MT26: MODULAR, THETA, WEIERSTRASS AND SIX-FRAME RETURN
%% 21 November 2026. Analytic source contracts and division-free algebra.
%% Sources, with conventions fixed rather than inferred from symbol names:
%% https://dlmf.nist.gov/20.7       (Jacobi identity and transformations)
%% https://dlmf.nist.gov/23.6       (periods 2*w1,2*w3 and theta/wp)
%% https://dlmf.nist.gov/23.15      (lambda and nome exp(pi*i*tau))
%% https://dlmf.nist.gov/23.18      (six lambda charts and mod-2 classes)
%% https://dlmf.nist.gov/23.19      (J, NOT j: j=1728*J)
%% https://www.math.ucla.edu/~wdduke/preprints/ramanujan.pdf
%% https://pure.hw.ac.uk/ws/portalfiles/portal/97077645/rnab376.pdf
%% https://dlmf.nist.gov/25.15
%% https://kskedlaya.org/18.785/funceq2.pdf
%% Status: SOURCE_* assertions are external analytic theorem contracts.
%% Algebraic %implied assertions have written constructive ring proofs.
%% Exact symbolic checks verify these polynomial expressions; their status
%% remains distinct from Hets/E proof receipts in the final receipt section.

spec MT26_CommutativeRing = H24_Ring with
  H24Scalar |-> MT26_R, h24Zero |-> MT26_z, h24One |-> MT26_o,
  h24Add |-> MT26_add, h24Neg |-> MT26_neg, h24Mul |-> MT26_mul
end

spec MT26_RingPolynomials = MT26_CommutativeRing then
  ops MT26_sub : MT26_R * MT26_R -> MT26_R;
      MT26_sq, MT26_cube : MT26_R -> MT26_R;
      MT26_two, MT26_three, MT26_four, MT26_five, MT26_seven,
      MT26_eight, MT26_nine, MT26_twelve, MT26_sixteen,
      MT26_twentyfour, MT26_twentyseven, MT26_twoforty,
      MT26_twofiftysix, MT26_fiveofour, MT26_seventeentwentyeight : MT26_R;
      MT26_f2, MT26_f3, MT26_h3 : MT26_R * MT26_R -> MT26_R
  forall x,y : MT26_R
  . MT26_sub(x,y) = MT26_add(x,MT26_neg(y)) %(n25s_scalar_subtraction)%
  . MT26_sq(x) = MT26_mul(x,x)
  . MT26_cube(x) = MT26_mul(MT26_sq(x),x)
  . MT26_two = MT26_add(MT26_o,MT26_o)
  . MT26_three = MT26_add(MT26_two,MT26_o)
  . MT26_four = MT26_sq(MT26_two)
  . MT26_five = MT26_add(MT26_two,MT26_three)
  . MT26_seven = MT26_add(MT26_three,MT26_four)
  . MT26_eight = MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_four)
  . MT26_nine = MT26_sq(MT26_three)
  . MT26_twelve = MT26_mul(MT26_three,MT26_four)
  . MT26_sixteen = MT26_sq(MT26_four)
  . MT26_twentyfour = MT26_mul(MT26_three,MT26_eight)
  . MT26_twentyseven = MT26_cube(MT26_three)
  . MT26_twoforty = MT26_mul(MT26_sixteen,MT26_mul(MT26_three,MT26_five))
  . MT26_twofiftysix = MT26_sq(MT26_sixteen)
  . MT26_fiveofour = MT26_mul(MT26_seven,MT26_mul(MT26_eight,MT26_nine))
  . MT26_seventeentwentyeight = MT26_cube(MT26_twelve)
  . MT26_f2(x,y) = MT26_add(MT26_sub(MT26_sq(x),MT26_mul(x,y)),MT26_sq(y))
  . MT26_f3(x,y) = MT26_mul(MT26_mul(x,y),MT26_sub(x,y))
  . MT26_h3(x,y) = MT26_mul(MT26_add(x,y),
      MT26_mul(MT26_sub(x,MT26_mul(MT26_two,y)),MT26_sub(MT26_mul(MT26_two,x),y)))
end

%% Proof: distribute f2^3 and h3^2 and cancel equal monomials. The remainder
%% is 27*x^2*y^2*(x-y)^2. This is a polynomial identity over Z and therefore
%% holds over every commutative ring, including characteristic 2 or 3.
%% Substitution (x,y)->(y-x,y) or (x,x-y) fixes f2 and f3 and negates h3.
%% For example f3(y-x,y)=(y-x)*y*(-x)=x*y*(x-y).
%% The f2 assertions follow by two distributive expansions; h3's three
%% factors are permuted with exactly one sign change. No field or choice.
spec MT26_UniversalModularAlgebra = MT26_RingPolynomials then
  forall x,y : MT26_R
  . MT26_sub(MT26_mul(MT26_four,MT26_cube(MT26_f2(x,y))),MT26_sq(MT26_h3(x,y))) =
      MT26_mul(MT26_twentyseven,MT26_sq(MT26_f3(x,y)))
      %(MT26_discriminant_polynomial)% %implied
  . MT26_f2(MT26_sub(y,x),y) = MT26_f2(x,y) %(MT26_f2_S)% %implied
  . MT26_f2(x,MT26_sub(x,y)) = MT26_f2(x,y) %(MT26_f2_T)% %implied
  . MT26_f3(MT26_sub(y,x),y) = MT26_f3(x,y) %(MT26_f3_S)% %implied
  . MT26_f3(x,MT26_sub(x,y)) = MT26_f3(x,y) %(MT26_f3_T)% %implied
  . MT26_h3(MT26_sub(y,x),y) = MT26_neg(MT26_h3(x,y)) %(MT26_h3_S)% %implied
  . MT26_h3(x,MT26_sub(x,y)) = MT26_neg(MT26_h3(x,y)) %(MT26_h3_T)% %implied
end

spec MT26_MatrixCarrier = MT26_RingPolynomials then
  free type MT26_V4 ::= MT26_v4(MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R)
  free type MT26_M4 ::= MT26_mat4(
    MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;
    MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R)
  ops MT26_dot4 : MT26_R * MT26_R * MT26_R * MT26_R *
                  MT26_R * MT26_R * MT26_R * MT26_R -> MT26_R;
      MT26_apply : MT26_M4 * MT26_V4 -> MT26_V4;
      MT26_comp : MT26_M4 * MT26_M4 -> MT26_M4;
      MT26_transpose : MT26_M4 -> MT26_M4;
      MT26_scale : MT26_R * MT26_M4 -> MT26_M4;
      MT26_vscale : MT26_R * MT26_V4 -> MT26_V4;
      MT26_I4, MT26_S4, MT26_T4, MT26_Q4, MT26_Qadj4 : MT26_M4
  forall a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p,w,x,y,z,r : MT26_R;
         M,N : MT26_M4; v : MT26_V4
  . MT26_dot4(a,b,c,d,w,x,y,z) = MT26_add(MT26_add(MT26_mul(a,w),MT26_mul(b,x)),
      MT26_add(MT26_mul(c,y),MT26_mul(d,z)))
  . MT26_apply(MT26_mat4(a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p),MT26_v4(w,x,y,z)) =
      MT26_v4(MT26_dot4(a,b,c,d,w,x,y,z),MT26_dot4(e,f,g,h,w,x,y,z),
              MT26_dot4(i,j,k,l,w,x,y,z),MT26_dot4(m,n,o,p,w,x,y,z))
  . MT26_apply(MT26_comp(M,N),v) = MT26_apply(M,MT26_apply(N,v))
  . MT26_transpose(MT26_mat4(a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p)) =
      MT26_mat4(a,e,i,m,b,f,j,n,c,g,k,o,d,h,l,p)
  . MT26_vscale(r,MT26_v4(w,x,y,z)) =
      MT26_v4(MT26_mul(r,w),MT26_mul(r,x),MT26_mul(r,y),MT26_mul(r,z))
  . MT26_apply(MT26_scale(r,M),v) = MT26_vscale(r,MT26_apply(M,v))
  . (forall u : MT26_V4 . MT26_apply(M,u) = MT26_apply(N,u)) => M=N
  . MT26_I4 = MT26_mat4(MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,
      MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,
      MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o)
  . MT26_S4 = MT26_mat4(MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,
      MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_neg(MT26_o),MT26_o,
      MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o)
  . MT26_T4 = MT26_mat4(MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,
      MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,
      MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_neg(MT26_o))
  . MT26_Q4 = MT26_mat4(MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,
      MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,
      MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_two,MT26_neg(MT26_o),
      MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_neg(MT26_o),MT26_two)
  . MT26_Qadj4 = MT26_mat4(MT26_three,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,
      MT26_z,MT26_three,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_two,MT26_o,
      MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_two)
end

%% S=[[-1,1],[0,1]], T=[[1,0],[1,-1]], Q=[[2,-1],[-1,2]].
%% Put P=[[0,1],[1,0]]. The programme matrices are S4=diag(P,S),
%% T4=diag(P,T), Q4=diag(I2,Q), Qadj4=diag(3*I2,adj(Q)). Both generators
%% have five nonzero entries when 1 != 0. S^2=T^2=I, ST=[[0,-1],[1,-1]],
%% (ST)^2=[[-1,1],[-1,0]], (ST)^3=I. Qadj*Q=3I and
%% S^t*Q*S=T^t*Q*T=Q by matrix multiplication. The first block retains
%% the regular C2 quotient; the second retains the standard S3 block.
%% These are universal equations, not a table-based enumeration proof.
spec MT26_SixFrameMatrices = MT26_MatrixCarrier then
  . MT26_comp(MT26_S4,MT26_S4) = MT26_I4 %(MT26_S_involution)% %implied
  . MT26_comp(MT26_T4,MT26_T4) = MT26_I4 %(MT26_T_involution)% %implied
  . MT26_comp(MT26_comp(MT26_S4,MT26_T4),
      MT26_comp(MT26_comp(MT26_S4,MT26_T4),MT26_comp(MT26_S4,MT26_T4))) = MT26_I4
      %(MT26_ST_order_three)% %implied
  . MT26_comp(MT26_Qadj4,MT26_Q4) = MT26_scale(MT26_three,MT26_I4)
      %(MT26_Q_inverse_numerator)% %implied
  . MT26_comp(MT26_transpose(MT26_S4),MT26_comp(MT26_Q4,MT26_S4)) = MT26_Q4
      %(MT26_S_preserves_Q)% %implied
  . MT26_comp(MT26_transpose(MT26_T4),MT26_comp(MT26_Q4,MT26_T4)) = MT26_Q4
      %(MT26_T_preserves_Q)% %implied
end

spec MT26_DaggerWithThird = MT26_SixFrameMatrices then
  op MT26_third : MT26_R
  op MT26_dagger : MT26_M4 -> MT26_M4
  . MT26_mul(MT26_three,MT26_third) = MT26_o
  forall M : MT26_M4
  . MT26_dagger(M) = MT26_scale(MT26_third,
      MT26_comp(MT26_Qadj4,MT26_comp(MT26_transpose(M),MT26_Q4)))
end

%% Put Qinv=(1/3)Qadj. Symmetry of Q,Qinv and Qinv*Q=Q*Qinv=I imply
%% (Qinv*M^t*Q)^dagger=M and (MN)^dagger=N^dagger*M^dagger.
%% Q-isometry and S^2=T^2=I then give S^dagger=S, T^dagger=T.
%% Only 3 invertible is required; this includes Z_2. No 1/2 is used.
spec MT26_DaggerReturn = MT26_DaggerWithThird then
  forall M,N : MT26_M4
  . MT26_dagger(MT26_dagger(M)) = M %(MT26_dagger_involutive)% %implied
  . MT26_dagger(MT26_comp(M,N)) = MT26_comp(MT26_dagger(N),MT26_dagger(M))
      %(MT26_dagger_contravariant)% %implied
  . MT26_dagger(MT26_S4) = MT26_S4 %(MT26_S_self_adjoint)% %implied
  . MT26_dagger(MT26_T4) = MT26_T4 %(MT26_T_self_adjoint)% %implied
end

spec MT26_NaturalScalarEmbedding = MT26_RingPolynomials and H24_Naturals then
  ops MT26_nat : GC26Nat -> MT26_R;
      MT26_powN : MT26_R * GC26Nat -> MT26_R
  forall n,m : GC26Nat; x,y : MT26_R
  . MT26_nat(GC26zero)=MT26_z %(nt26_complex_nat_zero)%
  . MT26_nat(GC26succ(n))=MT26_add(MT26_nat(n),MT26_o) %(nt26_complex_nat_step)%
  . MT26_powN(x,GC26zero)=MT26_o %(nt26_scalar_power_zero)%
  . MT26_powN(x,GC26succ(n))=MT26_mul(x,MT26_powN(x,n)) %(nt26_scalar_power_step)%
then
  forall n,m,k : GC26Nat
  . MT26_nat(GC26nadd(n,m))=MT26_add(MT26_nat(n),MT26_nat(m))
      %(nt26_natural_embedding_preserves_addition)% %implied
  . MT26_nat(GC26nmul(n,m))=MT26_mul(MT26_nat(n),MT26_nat(m))
      %(nt26_natural_embedding_preserves_multiplication)% %implied
  . MT26_powN(MT26_nat(n),k)=MT26_nat(GC26npow(n,k))
      %(nt26_natural_embedding_preserves_powers)% %implied
end
%% Induct on m for addition, then multiplication; induct on k for powers.
%% Distributivity and the additive result prove the multiplication step;
%% commutativity aligns the two power recursion conventions. No field needed.

spec MT26_CharacteristicZeroField = MT26_NaturalScalarEmbedding then
  ops MT26_inv : MT26_R ->? MT26_R;
      MT26_div : MT26_R * MT26_R ->? MT26_R
  forall n,m : GC26Nat; x,y : MT26_R
  . MT26_nat(n)=MT26_nat(m) => n=m %(nt26_characteristic_zero)%
  . def MT26_inv(x) <=> not x=MT26_z %(nt26_inverse_domain)%
  . not x=MT26_z => MT26_mul(x,MT26_inv(x))=MT26_o %(nt26_inverse_law)%
  . def MT26_div(x,y) <=> not y=MT26_z %(nt26_division_domain)%
  . not y=MT26_z => MT26_div(x,y)=MT26_mul(x,MT26_inv(y)) %(nt26_division_definition)%
end
spec MT26_DivisorCoefficients = MT26_NaturalScalarEmbedding and H24_DivisorSums then
  ops MT26_divisorPrefix : GC26Nat * GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> MT26_R;
      MT26_sigma : GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> MT26_R
  forall k,n,b : GC26Nat
  . MT26_divisorPrefix(k,n,b)=MT26_nat(h24DivisorPrefix(k,n,b))
      %(nt26_scalar_image_of_the_divisor_fold)%
  . MT26_sigma(k,n)=MT26_nat(h24DivisorSum(k,n)) %(nt26_scalar_divisor_sum)%
end
%% ANALYTIC INTERFACE: R is now interpreted as C. UHP is the actual upper
%% half-plane, tau its inclusion; exp is the complex exponential. ASeq is
%% a coefficient sequence, PS its convergent sum. No completeness or
%% convergence theorem is claimed to follow from the preceding ring axioms.
%% The zero coefficient of each normalized Eisenstein series is 1.
%% theta nome q=exp(pi*i*tau), Fourier nome Q=q^2=exp(2*pi*i*tau).
%% One complex analytic signature: character theta imports these constants
%% without importing Eisenstein, elliptic, modular or lambda source theorems.
%% The commutative-ring and 3-invertible matrix theories above remain separate.
spec MT26_AnalyticConstants = MT26_CharacteristicZeroField then
  ops MT26_pi,MT26_i : MT26_R;
      MT26_exp : MT26_R -> MT26_R
  . MT26_sq(MT26_i)=MT26_neg(MT26_o)
  . not MT26_pi=MT26_z
end

spec MT26_EisensteinSignature = MT26_DivisorCoefficients and MT26_AnalyticConstants then
  sorts MT26_UHP, MT26_ASeq, MT26_Modular
  ops MT26_tau : MT26_UHP -> MT26_R;
      MT26_qtheta, MT26_qfourier : MT26_UHP -> MT26_R;
      MT26_coeff : MT26_ASeq * GC26Nat -> MT26_R;
      MT26_PS : MT26_ASeq * MT26_R ->? MT26_R;
      MT26_Bernoulli : GC26Nat -> MT26_R;
      MT26_Ecoeff : GC26Nat -> MT26_ASeq;
      MT26_E,MT26_G : GC26Nat * MT26_UHP -> MT26_R;
      MT26_zetaEven : GC26Nat -> MT26_R;
      MT26_a,MT26_b,MT26_c,MT26_d : MT26_Modular -> MT26_R;
      MT26_act : MT26_Modular * MT26_UHP -> MT26_UHP;
      MT26_DerivativeE : GC26Nat * MT26_UHP -> MT26_R
  forall u : MT26_UHP; k,n : GC26Nat; g : MT26_Modular
  . MT26_qtheta(u)=MT26_exp(MT26_mul(MT26_mul(MT26_pi,MT26_i),MT26_tau(u)))
  . MT26_qfourier(u)=MT26_sq(MT26_qtheta(u))
  . MT26_coeff(MT26_Ecoeff(k),GC26zero)=MT26_o
  . not k=GC26zero => MT26_coeff(MT26_Ecoeff(k),GC26succ(n)) =
      MT26_neg(MT26_mul(MT26_div(MT26_mul(MT26_four,MT26_nat(k)),
        MT26_Bernoulli(GC26nadd(k,k))),MT26_sigma(GC26pred(GC26nadd(k,k)),GC26succ(n))))
  . MT26_sub(MT26_mul(MT26_a(g),MT26_d(g)),MT26_mul(MT26_b(g),MT26_c(g)))=MT26_o
  . MT26_tau(MT26_act(g,u)) = MT26_div(MT26_add(MT26_mul(MT26_a(g),MT26_tau(u)),MT26_b(g)),
      MT26_add(MT26_mul(MT26_c(g),MT26_tau(u)),MT26_d(g)))
end

%% SOURCE: G(k,tau)=sum_{(m,n)!=0}(m+n*tau)^(-2k), k>=2, with
%% absolute convergence. For k=1 the lattice sum needs a summation order;
%% the normalized Fourier series E(1,tau)=E2 is used instead.
%% D=(2*pi*i)^(-1)d/dtau. The following facts are imported analytic
%% theorems; they are not deductions from uninterpreted series operators.
spec MT26_SOURCE_EisensteinTheorems = MT26_EisensteinSignature then
  forall u : MT26_UHP; k,n : GC26Nat; g : MT26_Modular
  . not k=GC26zero => not MT26_Bernoulli(GC26nadd(k,k))=MT26_z
  . not k=GC26zero => MT26_E(k,u)=MT26_PS(MT26_Ecoeff(k),MT26_qfourier(u))
  . MT26_G(GC26nadd(GC26two,k),u)=MT26_mul(MT26_two,
      MT26_mul(MT26_zetaEven(GC26nadd(GC26two,k)),MT26_E(GC26nadd(GC26two,k),u)))
  . MT26_E(GC26nadd(GC26two,k),MT26_act(g,u)) =
      MT26_mul(MT26_powN(MT26_add(MT26_mul(MT26_c(g),MT26_tau(u)),MT26_d(g)),
        GC26nmul(GC26two,GC26nadd(GC26two,k))),MT26_E(GC26nadd(GC26two,k),u))
  . MT26_E(GC26one,MT26_act(g,u)) = MT26_add(
      MT26_mul(MT26_sq(MT26_add(MT26_mul(MT26_c(g),MT26_tau(u)),MT26_d(g))),MT26_E(GC26one,u)),
      MT26_mul(MT26_div(MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_three),MT26_mul(MT26_pi,MT26_i)),
        MT26_mul(MT26_c(g),MT26_add(MT26_mul(MT26_c(g),MT26_tau(u)),MT26_d(g)))))
  . MT26_coeff(MT26_Ecoeff(GC26one),GC26succ(n)) =
      MT26_neg(MT26_mul(MT26_twentyfour,MT26_sigma(GC26one,GC26succ(n))))
  . MT26_coeff(MT26_Ecoeff(GC26two),GC26succ(n)) =
      MT26_mul(MT26_twoforty,MT26_sigma(GC26three,GC26succ(n)))
  . MT26_coeff(MT26_Ecoeff(GC26three),GC26succ(n)) =
      MT26_neg(MT26_mul(MT26_fiveofour,MT26_sigma(h24Five,GC26succ(n))))
  . MT26_mul(MT26_twelve,MT26_DerivativeE(GC26one,u)) =
      MT26_sub(MT26_sq(MT26_E(GC26one,u)),MT26_E(GC26two,u))
  . MT26_mul(MT26_three,MT26_DerivativeE(GC26two,u)) =
      MT26_sub(MT26_mul(MT26_E(GC26one,u),MT26_E(GC26two,u)),MT26_E(GC26three,u))
  . MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_DerivativeE(GC26three,u)) =
      MT26_sub(MT26_mul(MT26_E(GC26one,u),MT26_E(GC26three,u)),MT26_sq(MT26_E(GC26two,u)))
end

%% theta2=sum_n exp(pi*i*tau*(n+1/2)^2), theta3=sum_n exp(pi*i*tau*n^2),
%% theta4=sum_n (-1)^n exp(pi*i*tau*n^2). Here all are theta constants.
%% theta1(z),theta2z(z) below use the DLMF angular argument, not z/pi.
%% Fixed full lattice generators are 1,tau (thus DLMF w1=1/2,w3=tau/2).
spec MT26_ThetaWeierstrassSignature = MT26_EisensteinSignature then
  ops MT26_theta2,MT26_theta3,MT26_theta4,MT26_eta : MT26_UHP -> MT26_R;
      MT26_tauHalf,MT26_tauDouble : MT26_UHP -> MT26_UHP;
      MT26_theta1z,MT26_theta2z : MT26_UHP * MT26_R -> MT26_R;
      MT26_x,MT26_y,MT26_lambda,MT26_Delta,MT26_j,MT26_J,
      MT26_e1,MT26_e2,MT26_e3,MT26_g2,MT26_g3 : MT26_UHP -> MT26_R;
      MT26_wp,MT26_wpPrime,MT26_LegendreX,MT26_LegendreY : MT26_UHP * MT26_R ->? MT26_R;
      MT26_Smod,MT26_Tmod : MT26_Modular
  pred MT26_offLattice : MT26_UHP * MT26_R
  forall u : MT26_UHP; z : MT26_R
  . MT26_tau(MT26_tauHalf(u))=MT26_div(MT26_tau(u),MT26_two)
  . MT26_tau(MT26_tauDouble(u))=MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_tau(u))
  . MT26_x(u)=MT26_sq(MT26_sq(MT26_theta2(u)))
  . MT26_y(u)=MT26_sq(MT26_sq(MT26_theta3(u)))
  . MT26_lambda(u)=MT26_div(MT26_x(u),MT26_y(u))
  . MT26_Delta(u)=MT26_div(MT26_sub(MT26_cube(MT26_E(GC26two,u)),
      MT26_sq(MT26_E(GC26three,u))),MT26_seventeentwentyeight)
  . MT26_j(u)=MT26_div(MT26_cube(MT26_E(GC26two,u)),MT26_Delta(u))
  . MT26_J(u)=MT26_div(MT26_j(u),MT26_seventeentwentyeight)
  . def MT26_wp(u,z) <=> MT26_offLattice(u,z)
  . def MT26_wpPrime(u,z) <=> MT26_offLattice(u,z)
  . def MT26_LegendreX(u,z) <=> MT26_offLattice(u,z)
  . def MT26_LegendreY(u,z) <=> MT26_offLattice(u,z)
  . MT26_offLattice(u,z) => MT26_LegendreX(u,z)=
      MT26_div(MT26_sub(MT26_wp(u,z),MT26_e3(u)),MT26_mul(MT26_sq(MT26_pi),MT26_y(u)))
  . MT26_offLattice(u,z) => MT26_LegendreY(u,z)=MT26_div(MT26_wpPrime(u,z),
      MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_mul(MT26_cube(MT26_pi),MT26_mul(MT26_sq(MT26_theta3(u)),MT26_y(u)))))
end

spec MT26_SOURCE_ThetaWeierstrassTheorems = MT26_ThetaWeierstrassSignature and MT26_SOURCE_EisensteinTheorems then
  forall u : MT26_UHP; z : MT26_R
  . not MT26_eta(u)=MT26_z
  . MT26_mul(MT26_theta2(u),MT26_mul(MT26_theta3(u),MT26_theta4(u)))=
      MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_cube(MT26_eta(u)))
  . MT26_Delta(u)=MT26_sq(MT26_cube(MT26_sq(MT26_sq(MT26_eta(u)))))
  . MT26_lambda(u)=MT26_mul(MT26_sixteen,MT26_powN(MT26_div(
      MT26_mul(MT26_sq(MT26_eta(MT26_tauDouble(u))),MT26_eta(MT26_tauHalf(u))),
      MT26_cube(MT26_eta(u))),GC26nadd(GC26four,GC26four)))
  . MT26_y(u)=MT26_add(MT26_x(u),MT26_sq(MT26_sq(MT26_theta4(u))))
  . not MT26_x(u)=MT26_z /\ not MT26_y(u)=MT26_z /\ not MT26_x(u)=MT26_y(u)
  . not MT26_Delta(u)=MT26_z
  . MT26_E(GC26two,u)=MT26_f2(MT26_x(u),MT26_y(u))
  . MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_E(GC26three,u))=MT26_h3(MT26_x(u),MT26_y(u))
  . MT26_e1(u)=MT26_mul(MT26_div(MT26_sq(MT26_pi),MT26_three),
      MT26_sub(MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_y(u)),MT26_x(u)))
  . MT26_e2(u)=MT26_mul(MT26_div(MT26_sq(MT26_pi),MT26_three),
      MT26_sub(MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_x(u)),MT26_y(u)))
  . MT26_e3(u)=MT26_neg(MT26_mul(MT26_div(MT26_sq(MT26_pi),MT26_three),
      MT26_add(MT26_x(u),MT26_y(u))))
  . MT26_g2(u)=MT26_mul(MT26_div(MT26_mul(MT26_four,MT26_sq(MT26_sq(MT26_pi))),MT26_three),
      MT26_E(GC26two,u))
  . MT26_g3(u)=MT26_mul(MT26_div(MT26_mul(MT26_eight,MT26_sq(MT26_cube(MT26_pi))),MT26_twentyseven),
      MT26_E(GC26three,u))
  . MT26_offLattice(u,z) => MT26_sq(MT26_wpPrime(u,z)) =
      MT26_sub(MT26_sub(MT26_mul(MT26_four,MT26_cube(MT26_wp(u,z))),
        MT26_mul(MT26_g2(u),MT26_wp(u,z))),MT26_g3(u))
  . MT26_offLattice(u,z) => not MT26_theta1z(u,MT26_mul(MT26_pi,z))=MT26_z
  . MT26_offLattice(u,z) => MT26_sub(MT26_wp(u,z),MT26_e1(u)) = MT26_sq(
      MT26_div(MT26_mul(MT26_pi,MT26_mul(MT26_theta3(u),
        MT26_mul(MT26_theta4(u),MT26_theta2z(u,MT26_mul(MT26_pi,z))))),
        MT26_theta1z(u,MT26_mul(MT26_pi,z))))
  . MT26_tau(MT26_act(MT26_Smod,u))=MT26_neg(MT26_inv(MT26_tau(u)))
  . MT26_tau(MT26_act(MT26_Tmod,u))=MT26_add(MT26_tau(u),MT26_o)
  . MT26_lambda(MT26_act(MT26_Smod,u))=MT26_sub(MT26_o,MT26_lambda(u))
  . MT26_lambda(MT26_act(MT26_Tmod,u))=MT26_div(MT26_lambda(u),MT26_sub(MT26_lambda(u),MT26_o))
end

%% Complete algebraic return: E4=f2, 2E6=h3 and 4f2^3-h3^2=27f3^2
%% imply 4*1728*Delta=27*f3^2. Characteristic zero permits cancellation
%% of 27, giving 256*Delta=f3^2. Multiply j=E4^3/Delta to obtain
%% j*f3^2=256*f2^3. Homogeneity and x=lambda*y then cancel y^6, yielding
%% j*lambda^2*(1-lambda)^2=256*(1-lambda+lambda^2)^3.
%% For the Legendre return, e1+e2+e3=0 and g2,g3 give
%% (wp')^2=4*(wp-e1)*(wp-e2)*(wp-e3). Put d=pi^2*theta3^4,
%% X=(wp-e3)/d, Y=wp'/(2*pi^3*theta3^6). Then Y^2=X*(X-1)*(X-lambda).
%% All denominators are nonzero; theta3^2 supplies a specified square root
%% of theta3^4, so no branch choice is needed.
%% Neither lambda=0 nor lambda=1 occurs on UHP, so every division here is
%% defined. S,T invariance of f2,f3 proves invariance of this ratio by word
%% induction. The modular action factors through SL2(Z)/Gamma(2)=S3.
%% This quotient is the frame label return; analytic values stay in C.
%% Over Z_2 the integral matrices and Q-dagger survive. Use homogeneous
%% charts: lambda and 1-lambda cannot both be 2-adic units.
spec MT26_ModularReturn = MT26_SOURCE_ThetaWeierstrassTheorems and MT26_UniversalModularAlgebra then
  forall u : MT26_UHP; z : MT26_R
  . MT26_mul(MT26_twofiftysix,MT26_Delta(u))=MT26_sq(MT26_f3(MT26_x(u),MT26_y(u)))
      %(MT26_theta_discriminant_return)% %implied
  . MT26_mul(MT26_j(u),MT26_sq(MT26_f3(MT26_x(u),MT26_y(u)))) =
      MT26_mul(MT26_twofiftysix,MT26_cube(MT26_f2(MT26_x(u),MT26_y(u))))
      %(MT26_j_homogeneous_return)% %implied
  . MT26_mul(MT26_j(u),MT26_mul(MT26_sq(MT26_lambda(u)),MT26_sq(MT26_sub(MT26_o,MT26_lambda(u))))) =
      MT26_mul(MT26_twofiftysix,MT26_cube(MT26_f2(MT26_lambda(u),MT26_o)))
      %(MT26_j_lambda_return)% %implied
  . MT26_j(MT26_act(MT26_Smod,u))=MT26_j(u) %(MT26_j_S_frame_return)% %implied
  . MT26_j(MT26_act(MT26_Tmod,u))=MT26_j(u) %(MT26_j_T_frame_return)% %implied
  . MT26_add(MT26_add(MT26_e1(u),MT26_e2(u)),MT26_e3(u))=MT26_z
      %(MT26_half_period_sum_return)% %implied
  . MT26_sub(MT26_e2(u),MT26_e3(u))=MT26_mul(MT26_sq(MT26_pi),MT26_x(u))
      %(MT26_half_period_numerator_return)% %implied
  . MT26_sub(MT26_e1(u),MT26_e3(u))=MT26_mul(MT26_sq(MT26_pi),MT26_y(u))
      %(MT26_half_period_denominator_return)% %implied
  . MT26_lambda(u)=MT26_div(MT26_sub(MT26_e2(u),MT26_e3(u)),MT26_sub(MT26_e1(u),MT26_e3(u)))
      %(MT26_weierstrass_cross_ratio_return)% %implied
  . MT26_offLattice(u,z) => MT26_sq(MT26_LegendreY(u,z))=MT26_mul(MT26_LegendreX(u,z),
      MT26_mul(MT26_sub(MT26_LegendreX(u,z),MT26_o),MT26_sub(MT26_LegendreX(u,z),MT26_lambda(u))))
      %(MT26_legendre_curve_return)% %implied
end

%% CHI IS THE SORT OF NONTRIVIAL PRIMITIVE DIRICHLET CHARACTERS, conductor
%% N>1. The trivial conductor-one character has a theta zero mode and zeta
%% poles; it belongs to the separate RH/principal-character module.
%% Define a in {0,1} by chi(-1)=(-1)^a and, for real t>0,
%% theta_chi(t)=sum_{n in Z} n^a chi(n) exp(-pi*n^2*t/N).
%% Mellin_chi(s)=integral_0^infty theta_chi(t)*t^((s+a)/2) dt/t.
%% The sums and integrals here are named analytic ports; source theorems
%% below identify their completed-L interpretation and exact constants.
%% The scalar comparison is a typed ring map. Character labels are shared;
%% the theta carrier is the actual nontrivial-primitive subsort below.
spec MT26_CharacterRealization = MT26_AnalyticConstants and DA26_FiniteCharacterSums then
  ops MT26_scalar : GC26Scalar -> MT26_R;
      MT26_conj : MT26_R -> MT26_R;
      MT26_integerPoint : GC26Integer -> MT26_R
  forall a,b : GC26Scalar; n : GC26Nat
  . MT26_scalar(GC26sz)=MT26_z %(nt26_scalar_zero)%
  . MT26_scalar(GC26so)=MT26_o %(nt26_scalar_one)%
  . MT26_scalar(GC26sadd(a,b))=MT26_add(MT26_scalar(a),MT26_scalar(b)) %(nt26_scalar_add)%
  . MT26_scalar(GC26smul(a,b))=MT26_mul(MT26_scalar(a),MT26_scalar(b)) %(nt26_scalar_mul)%
  . MT26_scalar(GC26sneg(a))=MT26_neg(MT26_scalar(a)) %(nt26_scalar_neg)%
  . MT26_scalar(GC26conjugate(a))=MT26_conj(MT26_scalar(a)) %(nt26_scalar_conjugation)%
  . MT26_scalar(a)=MT26_scalar(b) => a=b %(nt26_scalar_comparison_injective)%
  . MT26_integerPoint(GC26naturalInteger(n))=MT26_nat(n) %(nt26_same_integer_embedding)%
  forall a,b : GC26Integer
  . MT26_integerPoint(GC26iadd(a,b))=MT26_add(MT26_integerPoint(a),MT26_integerPoint(b))
      %(nt26_integer_embedding_additive)%
  . MT26_integerPoint(GC26imul(a,b))=MT26_mul(MT26_integerPoint(a),MT26_integerPoint(b))
      %(nt26_integer_embedding_multiplicative)%
  . MT26_integerPoint(DA26ineg(a))=MT26_neg(MT26_integerPoint(a))
      %(nt26_integer_embedding_negation)%
  . MT26_integerPoint(a)=MT26_integerPoint(b) => a=b %(nt26_integer_embedding_injective)%
  . exists n : GC26Nat . a=GC26naturalInteger(n) \/ a=DA26ineg(GC26naturalInteger(n))
      %(nt26_integer_signed_generation)%
  forall q,a : GC26Nat
  . GC26positive(q) => MT26_scalar(DA26additivePhase(q,a))=
      MT26_exp(MT26_div(MT26_mul(MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_pi),
        MT26_mul(MT26_i,MT26_nat(a))),MT26_nat(q))) %(nt26_same_additive_exponential_phase)%
end

spec MT26_CharacterThetaSignature = MT26_CharacterRealization then
  sort MT26_Chi < GC26DirichletCharacter
  ops GC26conjugateCharacter : MT26_Chi -> MT26_Chi;
      MT26_parity : MT26_Chi -> GC26Nat;
      MT26_chiMinusOne,MT26_gauss,MT26_rootConductor,MT26_rootNumber : MT26_Chi -> MT26_R;
      MT26_cLog : MT26_R -> MT26_R;
      MT26_cGamma : MT26_R ->? MT26_R;
      MT26_L,MT26_Lambda,MT26_mellin : MT26_Chi * MT26_R -> MT26_R;
      MT26_charTheta : MT26_Chi * MT26_R ->? MT26_R;
      MT26_rpow : MT26_R * MT26_R ->? MT26_R;
      MT26_halfShift : MT26_Chi * MT26_R -> MT26_R
  preds MT26_positive,MT26_rightHalf : MT26_R
  forall chi : GC26DirichletCharacter
  . chi in MT26_Chi <=> GC26primitive(chi) /\ not GC26conductor(chi)=GC26one
      %(nt26_nontrivial_primitive_character_subsort)%
  forall chi : MT26_Chi; s,t : MT26_R
  . MT26_gauss(chi)=MT26_scalar(DA26gauss(chi)) %(nt26_same_finite_Gauss_sum)%
  . MT26_chiMinusOne(chi)=MT26_scalar(GC26evaluate(chi,DA26minusOne)) %(nt26_same_character_parity_value)%
  . MT26_parity(chi)=GC26zero \/ MT26_parity(chi)=GC26one
  . MT26_chiMinusOne(chi)=MT26_powN(MT26_neg(MT26_o),MT26_parity(chi))
  . MT26_halfShift(chi,s)=MT26_div(MT26_add(s,MT26_nat(MT26_parity(chi))),MT26_two) %(bridge_half_shift_source)%
  . def MT26_charTheta(chi,t) <=> MT26_positive(t)
  . def MT26_rpow(t,s) <=> MT26_positive(t)
  . MT26_positive(t) => MT26_rpow(t,s)=MT26_exp(MT26_mul(s,MT26_cLog(t)))
end

%% SOURCE: finite Fourier identity sum_r chi(r)*exp(2*pi*i*m*r/N)
%% =conj(chi(m))*tau(chi), including nonunits because chi is primitive.
%% Poisson summation of x^a*exp(-pi*t*x^2/N) gives Fourier transform
%% (-i)^a*(N/t)^(1/2)*(N*xi/t)^a*exp(-pi*N*xi^2/t).
%% Substitute xi=m/N: theta_chi(t)=epsilon_chi*t^(-a-1/2)*theta_bar(1/t).
%% epsilon=tau/(i^a*sqrt(N)), |epsilon|=1, epsilon_chi*epsilon_bar=1.
%% Mellin integration first for Re(s)>1 gives 2*Lambda(s,chi).
%% Decay at both ends extends this identity to every s. Changing t->1/t
%% in the integral gives Lambda(s,chi)=epsilon*Lambda(1-s,bar(chi)).
%% The functional equation supplies a reflection of zeros; no assertion
%% that every zero is a reflection fixed point is added here.
spec MT26_SOURCE_CharacterThetaTheorems = MT26_CharacterThetaSignature then
  forall chi : MT26_Chi; s,t : MT26_R
  . GC26conductor(GC26conjugateCharacter(chi))=GC26conductor(chi)
  . MT26_parity(GC26conjugateCharacter(chi))=MT26_parity(chi)
  . MT26_positive(MT26_pi)
  . MT26_positive(MT26_nat(GC26conductor(chi)))
  . MT26_positive(t) => not t=MT26_z /\ MT26_positive(MT26_inv(t))
  . MT26_positive(MT26_div(MT26_nat(GC26conductor(chi)),MT26_pi))
  . MT26_sq(MT26_rootConductor(chi))=MT26_nat(GC26conductor(chi))
  . MT26_positive(MT26_rootConductor(chi))
  . MT26_rootNumber(chi)=MT26_div(MT26_gauss(chi),
      MT26_mul(MT26_powN(MT26_i,MT26_parity(chi)),MT26_rootConductor(chi)))
  . MT26_mul(MT26_rootNumber(chi),MT26_conj(MT26_rootNumber(chi)))=MT26_o
  . MT26_mul(MT26_rootNumber(chi),MT26_rootNumber(GC26conjugateCharacter(chi)))=MT26_o
  . MT26_positive(t) => MT26_charTheta(chi,t)=MT26_mul(MT26_rootNumber(chi),
      MT26_mul(MT26_rpow(t,MT26_neg(MT26_add(MT26_nat(MT26_parity(chi)),MT26_div(MT26_o,MT26_two)))),
        MT26_charTheta(GC26conjugateCharacter(chi),MT26_inv(t))))
  . MT26_rightHalf(s) => MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_mul(
      MT26_rpow(MT26_div(MT26_nat(GC26conductor(chi)),MT26_pi),MT26_halfShift(chi,s)),
      MT26_mul(MT26_cGamma(MT26_halfShift(chi,s)),MT26_L(chi,s)))
  . MT26_mellin(chi,s)=MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_Lambda(chi,s)) %(bridge_mellin_source)%
  . MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_mul(MT26_rootNumber(chi),MT26_Lambda(GC26conjugateCharacter(chi),MT26_sub(MT26_o,s)))
end

%% Root-number product and chibar^2=id make the functional-equation
%% transport involutive: apply it twice and cancel epsilon*epsilon_bar=1.
%% To reflect zeros, multiply by the inverse root number; existence of
%% that inverse is explicitly witnessed by the conjugate-character root.
spec MT26_CharacterThetaReturn = MT26_SOURCE_CharacterThetaTheorems then
  forall chi : MT26_Chi; s : MT26_R
  . MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_z <=> MT26_Lambda(GC26conjugateCharacter(chi),MT26_sub(MT26_o,s))=MT26_z
      %(MT26_functional_equation_zero_return)% %implied
  . MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_mul(MT26_rootNumber(chi),MT26_mul(MT26_rootNumber(GC26conjugateCharacter(chi)),
      MT26_Lambda(chi,s))) %(MT26_functional_equation_double_return)% %implied
end

%% THE FULL PROGRAMME GROUP ALGEBRA, INCLUDING ITS C2 FACTOR.
%% For coefficient vector (a,b,c,d,e,f) on (I,S,T,ST,TS,STS), the image is
%% [[u,v],[v,u]] direct-sum [[w,x],[y,z]], with the six coordinates below.
%% The coefficient-to-coordinate determinant is 27. More constructively,
%% the displayed inverse uses only 1/3; substitution cancels every mixed
%% term and leaves each original coefficient. Thus the map is an algebra
%% isomorphism R[S3] -> R[C2] x M2(R) whenever 3 is invertible. Products
%% are preserved because S,T satisfy the group presentation. This covers
%% every word: cancel SS and TT, use STST=TS and TSTS=ST to shorten an
%% alternating word, and identify STS=TST. This is induction on word
%% length. The explicit inverse proves independence of the resulting six
%% normal forms without enumerating any state of an infinite carrier.
%% The construction covers
%% Z_2 and every dyadic cut. It is stronger than a doubled standard block,
%% whose group representation is faithful but whose group-algebra map is not.
spec MT26_BlockCarrier = MT26_MatrixCarrier then
  op MT26_block : MT26_R * MT26_R * MT26_R * MT26_R * MT26_R * MT26_R -> MT26_M4
  forall a,b,c,d,e,f : MT26_R
  . MT26_block(a,b,c,d,e,f)=MT26_mat4(a,b,MT26_z,MT26_z,
      b,a,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,c,d,MT26_z,MT26_z,e,f) %(n25s_block_coordinates)%
end

spec MT26_GroupAlgebraCoordinates = MT26_DaggerWithThird and MT26_BlockCarrier then
  free type MT26_V6 ::= MT26_v6(MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R;MT26_R)
  ops MT26_encode6 : MT26_V6 -> MT26_M4;
      MT26_decode6 : MT26_M4 -> MT26_V6
  forall a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p : MT26_R
  . MT26_encode6(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))=MT26_block(
      MT26_add(a,MT26_add(d,e)), MT26_add(b,MT26_add(c,f)),
      MT26_sub(MT26_add(a,c),MT26_add(b,e)),
      MT26_sub(MT26_add(b,e),MT26_add(d,f)),
      MT26_sub(MT26_add(c,d),MT26_add(e,f)),
      MT26_sub(MT26_add(a,b),MT26_add(c,d)))
  . MT26_decode6(MT26_mat4(a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p))=MT26_v6(
      MT26_mul(MT26_third,MT26_add(a,MT26_add(k,p))),
      MT26_mul(MT26_third,MT26_add(MT26_sub(b,k),MT26_add(o,p))),
      MT26_mul(MT26_third,MT26_sub(MT26_add(b,MT26_add(k,l)),p)),
      MT26_mul(MT26_third,MT26_sub(MT26_add(a,o),MT26_add(k,l))),
      MT26_mul(MT26_third,MT26_sub(MT26_add(a,l),MT26_add(o,p))),
      MT26_mul(MT26_third,MT26_sub(b,MT26_add(l,o))))
end

spec MT26_GroupAlgebraReturn = MT26_GroupAlgebraCoordinates then
  forall v : MT26_V6; a,b,c,d,e,f : MT26_R
  . MT26_decode6(MT26_encode6(v))=v %(MT26_algebra_coefficients_return)% %implied
  . MT26_encode6(MT26_decode6(MT26_block(a,b,c,d,e,f)))=MT26_block(a,b,c,d,e,f)
      %(MT26_algebra_blocks_return)% %implied
end

%% FINITE GALOIS BRIDGE: let L be transcendental over C, and
%% j(L)=256*(1-L+L^2)^3/(L^2*(1-L)^2). The numerator and denominator
%% are coprime: the only denominator roots are 0,1 and the numerator is
%% nonzero there. The rational map therefore has degree max(6,4)=6.
%% The two field automorphisms L->1-L and L->L/(L-1) fix j. Their action
%% on {0,1,infinity} is by adjacent transpositions, hence is faithful S3.
%% A separable degree-six extension with six automorphisms is Galois and
%% its fixed field is C(j). This uses the ordinary rational-function degree
%% and finite Galois theorems, made explicit as SOURCE contracts below.
%% On SPECIALIZED complex values, the orbit may have fewer than six points:
%% {-1,1/2,2} has stabilizer C2 (j=1728); L^2-L+1=0 has stabilizer C3
%% (j=0). A frame remains a labelled basis of E[2], a genuine S3 torsor;
%% equality of evaluated lambda values does not identify those frame tags.
%% References: DLMF 23.18,23.19; Milne, Modular Functions and Modular Forms,
%% https://www.jmilne.org/math/CourseNotes/mf.html , sections 2,4,8.
spec MT26_SOURCE_LambdaGaloisInterface = MT26_CharacteristicZeroField then
  sorts MT26_RationalFunction,MT26_FrameTag
  ops MT26_genericLambda,MT26_genericJ : MT26_RationalFunction;
      MT26_rfS,MT26_rfT : MT26_RationalFunction -> MT26_RationalFunction;
      MT26_extensionDegree : GC26Nat;
      MT26_orbitSize,MT26_stabilizerSize : MT26_R -> GC26Nat
  preds MT26_inCj,MT26_fixedBySix : MT26_RationalFunction;
        MT26_harmonic,MT26_equianharmonic,MT26_validLambda : MT26_R
  forall f : MT26_RationalFunction; x : MT26_R
  . MT26_extensionDegree=h24Six
  . MT26_rfS(MT26_rfS(f))=f
  . MT26_rfT(MT26_rfT(f))=f
  . MT26_rfS(MT26_rfT(MT26_rfS(MT26_rfT(MT26_rfS(MT26_rfT(f))))))=f
  . MT26_rfS(MT26_genericJ)=MT26_genericJ
  . MT26_rfT(MT26_genericJ)=MT26_genericJ
  . MT26_fixedBySix(f) <=> (MT26_rfS(f)=f /\ MT26_rfT(f)=f)
  . MT26_fixedBySix(f) <=> MT26_inCj(f)
  . MT26_validLambda(x) <=> (not x=MT26_z /\ not x=MT26_o)
  . MT26_harmonic(x) <=> (x=MT26_neg(MT26_o) \/ x=MT26_div(MT26_o,MT26_two) \/ x=MT26_two)
  . MT26_equianharmonic(x) <=> MT26_f2(x,MT26_o)=MT26_z
  . MT26_validLambda(x) /\ MT26_harmonic(x) =>
      MT26_orbitSize(x)=GC26three /\ MT26_stabilizerSize(x)=GC26two
  . MT26_validLambda(x) /\ MT26_equianharmonic(x) =>
      MT26_orbitSize(x)=GC26two /\ MT26_stabilizerSize(x)=GC26three
  . MT26_validLambda(x) /\ not MT26_harmonic(x) /\ not MT26_equianharmonic(x) =>
      MT26_orbitSize(x)=h24Six /\ MT26_stabilizerSize(x)=GC26one
end

%% MX26: SECTION-SPECIFIC HOLOMORPHIC THETA AND LAMBDA RELATIONS.
%% Analytic limits, modularity
%% and elliptic inversion remain named source contracts; algebraic returns
%% are %implied goals. Analytic-source constructions remain explicit imports.
%% Primary sources: Allen--Beckwith--Sharma, section 2.2,
%% https://doi.org/10.1007/s11139-024-01020-3 ; DLMF 20.5,23.15,23.19,19.2.

%% h_chi(z)=(1/2)*sum_{n in Z} n^a*chi(n)*exp(2*pi*i*n^2*z),
%% a=(1-chi(-1))/2 in {0,1}. Our MT26_Chi excludes the principal modulus-one
%% character, so chi(0)=0. Pairing n and -n gives the positive-n series.
%% MX26_hSumLimit names convergence of the displayed partial sums as n->infty.
%% Theta(z)=sum_n exp(2*pi*i*n^2*z)=theta3(2z), NOT the odd Jacobi theta1.
%% jTheta(g,z)=Theta(gz)/Theta(z) fixes the usual theta multiplier globally
%% without choosing a square root of c*z+d in a separate convention.
spec MX26_HolomorphicThetaSignature = MT26_SOURCE_ThetaWeierstrassTheorems and MT26_SOURCE_CharacterThetaTheorems then
  ops MX26_level,MX26_twiceWeight : MT26_Chi -> GC26Nat;
      MX26_characterValue : MT26_Chi * MT26_R ->? MT26_R;
      MX26_minus4 : MT26_R -> MT26_R;
      MX26_hSum : MT26_Chi * MT26_UHP * GC26Nat -> MT26_R;
      MX26_holoTheta : MT26_Chi * MT26_UHP -> MT26_R;
      MX26_Theta : MT26_UHP -> MT26_R;
      MX26_jTheta : MT26_Modular * MT26_UHP -> MT26_R;
      MX26_thermalChart : MT26_Chi * MT26_R ->? MT26_UHP
  preds MX26_integer : MT26_R;
        MX26_inGamma0 : MT26_Chi * MT26_Modular;
        MX26_hSumLimit : MT26_Chi * MT26_UHP * MT26_R;
        MX26_holomorphicAtCusps,MX26_cuspidal : MT26_Chi
  forall r : MT26_R
  . MX26_integer(r) <=> (exists a : GC26Integer . MT26_integerPoint(a)=r)
      %(nt26_integer_image_definition)%
  forall chi : MT26_Chi; a : GC26Integer
  . MX26_characterValue(chi,MT26_integerPoint(a))=MT26_scalar(GC26evaluate(chi,a))
      %(nt26_holomorphic_theta_uses_same_character_values)%
  forall chi : MT26_Chi; u : MT26_UHP; n : GC26Nat; t,r : MT26_R; g : MT26_Modular
  . def MX26_characterValue(chi,r) <=> MX26_integer(r)
  . MX26_level(chi)=GC26nmul(GC26four,GC26nmul(GC26conductor(chi),GC26conductor(chi)))
  . MX26_twiceWeight(chi)=GC26succ(GC26nmul(GC26two,MT26_parity(chi)))
  . MX26_Theta(u)=MT26_theta3(MT26_tauDouble(u))
  . MX26_jTheta(g,u)=MT26_div(MX26_Theta(MT26_act(g,u)),MX26_Theta(u))
  . MX26_hSum(chi,u,GC26zero)=MT26_z
  . MX26_hSum(chi,u,GC26succ(n))=MT26_add(MX26_hSum(chi,u,n),
      MT26_mul(MX26_characterValue(chi,MT26_nat(GC26succ(n))),MT26_mul(
        MT26_powN(MT26_nat(GC26succ(n)),MT26_parity(chi)),MT26_exp(MT26_mul(
          MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_mul(MT26_pi,MT26_i)),
          MT26_mul(MT26_sq(MT26_nat(GC26succ(n))),MT26_tau(u)))))))
  . MX26_inGamma0(chi,g) <=> (MX26_integer(MT26_a(g)) /\ MX26_integer(MT26_b(g)) /\
      MX26_integer(MT26_c(g)) /\ MX26_integer(MT26_d(g)) /\
      (exists q : MT26_R . MX26_integer(q) /\ MT26_c(g)=MT26_mul(MT26_nat(MX26_level(chi)),q)))
  . MX26_integer(r) => MX26_minus4(MT26_add(MT26_mul(MT26_four,r),MT26_o))=MT26_o
  . MX26_integer(r) => MX26_minus4(MT26_add(MT26_mul(MT26_four,r),MT26_three))=MT26_neg(MT26_o)
  . def MX26_thermalChart(chi,t) <=> MT26_positive(t)
  . MT26_positive(t) => MT26_tau(MX26_thermalChart(chi,t))=
      MT26_mul(MT26_i,MT26_div(t,MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_nat(GC26conductor(chi)))))
end

%% SOURCE: weight a+1/2, level 4*N^2 (not necessarily minimal level),
%% nebentypus chi(d)*((-1)/d)^a. The ratio multiplier power is 2*a+1.
%% Under z=i*t/(2*N), exp(2*pi*i*n^2*z)=exp(-pi*n^2*t/N);
%% hence the SAME thermal series MT26_charTheta is exactly twice h_chi.
spec MX26_SOURCE_HolomorphicThetaTheorems = MX26_HolomorphicThetaSignature then
  forall chi : MT26_Chi; u : MT26_UHP; t : MT26_R; g : MT26_Modular
  . MX26_hSumLimit(chi,u,MX26_holoTheta(chi,u))
  . MX26_holomorphicAtCusps(chi)
  . MT26_parity(chi)=GC26one => MX26_cuspidal(chi)
  . MX26_inGamma0(chi,g) => MX26_holoTheta(chi,MT26_act(g,u))=MT26_mul(
      MX26_characterValue(chi,MT26_d(g)),MT26_mul(
        MT26_powN(MX26_minus4(MT26_d(g)),MT26_parity(chi)),
        MT26_mul(MT26_powN(MX26_jTheta(g,u),MX26_twiceWeight(chi)),MX26_holoTheta(chi,u))))
  . MT26_positive(t) => MT26_charTheta(chi,t)=
      MT26_mul(MT26_two,MX26_holoTheta(chi,MX26_thermalChart(chi,t)))
end

%% k=theta2^2/theta3^2 specifies the modulus itself; lambda=k^2 follows.
%% Eta quotients below follow from the convergent Jacobi/eta products:
%% theta2=2*eta(2tau)^2/eta(tau), theta4=eta(tau/2)^2/eta(tau).
%% Thus r^8=16*theta4^4/theta2^4 for r=eta(tau/2)/eta(2tau).
%% Jacobi gives lambda*(r^8+16)=16; lambda!=0 then returns the quotient.
%% No unmarked fourth roots or global inverse branches are introduced.
spec MX26_LambdaRelationsSignature = MT26_ModularReturn then
  ops MX26_k,MX26_kComplement,MX26_etaRatio,MX26_etaDen : MT26_UHP -> MT26_R
  forall u : MT26_UHP
  . MX26_k(u)=MT26_div(MT26_sq(MT26_theta2(u)),MT26_sq(MT26_theta3(u)))
  . MX26_kComplement(u)=MT26_div(MT26_sq(MT26_theta4(u)),MT26_sq(MT26_theta3(u)))
  . MX26_etaRatio(u)=MT26_div(MT26_eta(MT26_tauHalf(u)),MT26_eta(MT26_tauDouble(u)))
  . MX26_etaDen(u)=MT26_add(MT26_powN(MX26_etaRatio(u),GC26nadd(GC26four,GC26four)),MT26_sixteen)
end

spec MX26_SOURCE_EtaProducts = MX26_LambdaRelationsSignature then
  forall u : MT26_UHP
  . MT26_theta2(u)=MT26_div(MT26_mul(MT26_two,MT26_sq(MT26_eta(MT26_tauDouble(u)))),MT26_eta(u))
  . MT26_theta4(u)=MT26_div(MT26_sq(MT26_eta(MT26_tauHalf(u))),MT26_eta(u))
end

spec MX26_LambdaRelationReturns = MX26_SOURCE_EtaProducts then
  forall u : MT26_UHP
  . MT26_sq(MX26_k(u))=MT26_lambda(u) %(MX26_lambda_is_k_squared)% %implied
  . MT26_sq(MX26_kComplement(u))=MT26_sub(MT26_o,MT26_lambda(u)) %(MX26_complement_squared)% %implied
  . MT26_mul(MT26_lambda(u),MX26_etaDen(u))=MT26_sixteen %(MX26_eta_polynomial_return)% %implied
  . not MX26_etaDen(u)=MT26_z %(MX26_eta_denominator_nonzero)% %implied
  . MT26_lambda(u)=MT26_div(MT26_sixteen,MX26_etaDen(u)) %(MX26_second_eta_quotient)% %implied
end

%% POSITIVE-AXIS INVERSE ONLY. Kpar uses the PARAMETER m, not the modulus:
%% Kpar(m)=integral_0^(pi/2) (1-m*sin(theta)^2)^(-1/2) dtheta=K_DLMF(sqrt(m)).
%% For 0<m<1 use the positive real integrand and positive Kpar values.
%% The inverse point is tau=i*Kpar(1-m)/Kpar(m) on the positive imaginary axis.
%% Primary source: DLMF 19.2 and 23.15.1-2, with m=k^2 explicitly substituted.
spec MX26_SOURCE_PositiveAxisInverse = MX26_LambdaRelationReturns and MT26_SOURCE_CharacterThetaTheorems then
  ops MX26_Kpar : MT26_R ->? MT26_R;
      MX26_inverseLambda : MT26_R ->? MT26_UHP
  pred MX26_unitInterval : MT26_R
  forall m : MT26_R
  . MX26_unitInterval(m) <=> (MT26_positive(m) /\ MT26_positive(MT26_sub(MT26_o,m)))
  . def MX26_Kpar(m) <=> MX26_unitInterval(m)
  . def MX26_inverseLambda(m) <=> MX26_unitInterval(m)
  . MX26_unitInterval(m) => MT26_positive(MX26_Kpar(m))
  . MX26_unitInterval(m) => MT26_tau(MX26_inverseLambda(m))=MT26_mul(MT26_i,
      MT26_div(MX26_Kpar(MT26_sub(MT26_o,m)),MX26_Kpar(m)))
  . MX26_unitInterval(m) => MT26_lambda(MX26_inverseLambda(m))=m
end

%% RH26: Robin, Lagarias, RH, Dirichlet GRH, and a sparse dagger return.
%% This is a CASL development fragment; the enclosing file supplies `logic CASL`.
%% No unqualified RH or GRH assertion occurs in the base specifications.
%% Source identities and proof status are retained in this single file.
%% Analytical operations below have their standard real/complex interpretation.
%% Their signature is an interface, not a construction of complete analysis.

spec RH26_ARITHMETIC = H24_DivisorSums and
  { H24_Ring with H24Scalar |-> RH26Real, h24Zero |-> rh26R0, h24One |-> rh26R1,
      h24Add |-> rh26Radd, h24Neg |-> rh26Rneg, h24Mul |-> rh26Rmul } then
  ops rh26Cut5040 : GC26Nat;
      rh26SigmaUp : GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat;
      rh26Sigma : GC26Nat -> GC26Nat;
      rh26Half,rh26Gamma : RH26Real;
      rh26Rsub : RH26Real * RH26Real -> RH26Real;
      rh26Rinv,rh26Exp,rh26Log,rh26Abs : RH26Real -> RH26Real;
      rh26NatR,rh26H,rh26RobinBound,rh26LagariasBound : GC26Nat -> RH26Real
  preds rh26LeR,rh26LtR : RH26Real * RH26Real
  . rh26Cut5040=GC26nadd(GC26nmul(h24Five,GC26nmul(h24Ten,GC26nmul(h24Ten,h24Ten))),
      GC26nmul(GC26four,h24Ten)) %(rh26_cut_5040)%
  . forall n,k : GC26Nat . rh26SigmaUp(n,k)=h24DivisorPrefix(GC26one,n,k)
      %(rh26_same_divisor_prefix)%
  . forall n : GC26Nat . rh26Sigma(n)=h24DivisorSum(GC26one,n)
      %(rh26_same_divisor_sum)%
  %% sigmaUp(n,k) sums precisely the positive divisors d<=k.
  . forall x : RH26Real . not x=rh26R0 =>
      rh26Rmul(x,rh26Rinv(x))=rh26R1 %(rh26_inverse_nonzero)%
  . not rh26R0=rh26R1 %(rh26_nontrivial_scalar)%
  . forall x,y : RH26Real . rh26Rsub(x,y)=rh26Radd(x,rh26Rneg(y)) %(rh26_sub)%
  . rh26Radd(rh26Half,rh26Half)=rh26R1 %(rh26_half)%
  . forall x : RH26Real . rh26LeR(x,x) %(rh26_order_refl)%
  . forall x,y,z : RH26Real . rh26LeR(x,y) /\ rh26LeR(y,z) =>
      rh26LeR(x,z) %(rh26_order_trans)%
  . forall x,y : RH26Real . rh26LeR(x,y) /\ rh26LeR(y,x) => x=y %(rh26_order_antisym)%
  . forall x,y : RH26Real . rh26LeR(x,y) \/ rh26LeR(y,x) %(rh26_order_total)%
  . forall x,y : RH26Real . rh26LtR(x,y) <=>
      rh26LeR(x,y) /\ not x=y %(rh26_order_strict)%
  . forall x,y,z : RH26Real . rh26LeR(x,y) =>
      rh26LeR(rh26Radd(x,z),rh26Radd(y,z)) %(rh26_order_add)%
  . forall x,y : RH26Real . rh26LeR(rh26R0,x) /\ rh26LeR(rh26R0,y) =>
      rh26LeR(rh26R0,rh26Rmul(x,y)) %(rh26_order_mul)%
  . rh26LtR(rh26R0,rh26R1) %(rh26_positive_one)%
  . forall x : RH26Real . rh26LeR(rh26R0,x) => rh26Abs(x)=x %(rh26_abs_pos)%
  . forall x : RH26Real . rh26LeR(x,rh26R0) => rh26Abs(x)=rh26Rneg(x) %(rh26_abs_neg)%
  . rh26NatR(GC26zero)=rh26R0 %(rh26_embed_zero)%
  . forall n : GC26Nat . rh26NatR(GC26succ(n))=
      rh26Radd(rh26NatR(n),rh26R1) %(rh26_embed_succ)%
  . rh26H(GC26zero)=rh26R0 %(rh26_harmonic_zero)%
  . forall n : GC26Nat . rh26H(GC26succ(n))=
      rh26Radd(rh26H(n),rh26Rinv(rh26NatR(GC26succ(n)))) %(rh26_harmonic_succ)%
  . rh26Log(rh26R1)=rh26R0 %(rh26_log_one)%
  . forall x : RH26Real . rh26LtR(rh26R0,rh26Exp(x)) %(rh26_exp_positive)%
  . forall x,y : RH26Real . rh26Exp(rh26Radd(x,y))=
      rh26Rmul(rh26Exp(x),rh26Exp(y)) %(rh26_exp_add)%
  . forall x,y : RH26Real . rh26LtR(x,y) =>
      rh26LtR(rh26Exp(x),rh26Exp(y)) %(rh26_exp_monotone)%
  . forall x : RH26Real . rh26Log(rh26Exp(x))=x %(rh26_log_exp)%
  . forall x : RH26Real . rh26LtR(rh26R0,x) =>
      rh26Exp(rh26Log(x))=x %(rh26_exp_log)%
  %% Euler--Mascheroni constant: lim_n (H_n-log n)=gamma.
  . forall eps : RH26Real . rh26LtR(rh26R0,eps) =>
      (exists m : GC26Nat . forall n : GC26Nat . h24LtN(m,n) =>
        rh26LtR(rh26Abs(rh26Rsub(
          rh26Rsub(rh26H(n),rh26Log(rh26NatR(n))),rh26Gamma)),eps))
      %(rh26_euler_mascheroni_limit)%
  . forall n : GC26Nat . rh26RobinBound(n)=
      rh26Rmul(rh26Rmul(rh26Exp(rh26Gamma),rh26NatR(n)),
        rh26Log(rh26Log(rh26NatR(n)))) %(rh26_robin_bound)%
  . forall n : GC26Nat . rh26LagariasBound(n)=
      rh26Radd(rh26H(n),rh26Rmul(rh26Exp(rh26H(n)),rh26Log(rh26H(n))))
      %(rh26_lagarias_bound)%
end
spec RH26_TARGET_DEFINITIONS = RH26_ARITHMETIC and GC26_CharacterSignature then
  sort RH26Complex
  ops rh26C0, rh26C1 : RH26Complex;
      rh26COfN : GC26Nat -> RH26Complex;
      rh26Cneg, rh26Conj, rh26OneMinus, rh26Zeta : RH26Complex -> RH26Complex;
      rh26Re : RH26Complex -> RH26Real;
      rh26TrivialChi : GC26DirichletCharacter;
      rh26L : GC26DirichletCharacter * RH26Complex -> RH26Complex
  preds rh26Strip, rh26OnLine, rh26TrivialZetaZero,
        rh26NontrivialZetaZero : RH26Complex;
        rh26RobinAll, rh26LagariasAll, rh26LagariasStrict,
        rh26RH, rh26RHStrip, rh26GRHPrimitive, rh26GRHAll : ()
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26Strip(s) <=>
      rh26LtR(rh26R0,rh26Re(s)) /\ rh26LtR(rh26Re(s),rh26R1)
      %(rh26_strip_definition)%
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26OnLine(s) <=> rh26Re(s)=rh26Half
      %(rh26_line_definition)%
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26TrivialZetaZero(s) <=>
      (exists n : GC26Nat .
        s=rh26Cneg(rh26COfN(GC26nmul(GC26two,GC26succ(n)))))
      %(rh26_trivial_zeta_zero_definition)%
  %% This excludes s=1 explicitly, matching the audited Mathlib statement.
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26NontrivialZetaZero(s) <=>
      rh26Zeta(s)=rh26C0 /\ not rh26TrivialZetaZero(s) /\ not s=rh26C1
      %(rh26_nontrivial_zeta_zero_definition)%
  . rh26RH <=> (forall s : RH26Complex .
      rh26NontrivialZetaZero(s) => rh26OnLine(s)) %(rh26_RH_definition)%
  . rh26RHStrip <=> (forall s : RH26Complex .
      rh26Strip(s) /\ rh26Zeta(s)=rh26C0 => rh26OnLine(s)) %(rh26_RH_strip_definition)%
  %% GC26DirichletCharacter denotes all Dirichlet characters of positive moduli, including 1.
  . rh26GRHPrimitive <=> (forall chi : GC26DirichletCharacter; s : RH26Complex .
      GC26primitive(chi) /\ rh26Strip(s) /\ rh26L(chi,s)=rh26C0 => rh26OnLine(s))
      %(rh26_GRH_primitive_definition)%
  . rh26GRHAll <=> (forall chi : GC26DirichletCharacter; s : RH26Complex .
      rh26Strip(s) /\ rh26L(chi,s)=rh26C0 => rh26OnLine(s))
      %(rh26_GRH_all_definition)%
  . rh26RobinAll <=> (forall n : GC26Nat . h24LtN(rh26Cut5040,n) =>
      rh26LtR(rh26NatR(rh26Sigma(n)),rh26RobinBound(n))) %(rh26_Robin_definition)%
  . rh26LagariasAll <=> (forall n : GC26Nat . h24LtN(GC26zero,n) =>
      rh26LeR(rh26NatR(rh26Sigma(n)),rh26LagariasBound(n))) %(rh26_Lagarias_definition)%
  . rh26LagariasStrict <=> (forall n : GC26Nat . h24LtN(GC26one,n) =>
      rh26LtR(rh26NatR(rh26Sigma(n)),rh26LagariasBound(n)))
      %(rh26_Lagarias_strict_definition)%
  %% H_1=sigma(1)=1 and log(1)=0 give this equality by arithmetic reduction.
  . rh26NatR(rh26Sigma(GC26one))=rh26LagariasBound(GC26one)
      %(rh26_lagarias_at_one)% %implied
  %% Direct universal specialization, requiring no analytic theorem.
  . rh26GRHAll => rh26GRHPrimitive %(rh26_all_implies_primitive)% %implied
end
spec RH26_STANDARD_ANALYTIC_IMPORTS = RH26_TARGET_DEFINITIONS then
  %% Imported standard analytic facts, not consequences of an arbitrary L symbol.
  %% DLMF 25.10(i), 25.15.4. Imprimitive extra Euler-factor zeros have Re(s)=0,
  %% so primitive and induced L-functions have the same zeros in the open strip.
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26NontrivialZetaZero(s) <=>
      rh26Strip(s) /\ rh26Zeta(s)=rh26C0 %(rh26_import_zeta_zero_classification)%
  . GC26primitive(rh26TrivialChi) %(rh26_import_trivial_is_primitive)%
  . GC26conductor(rh26TrivialChi)=GC26one %(rh26_import_trivial_conductor)%
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26L(rh26TrivialChi,s)=rh26Zeta(s)
      %(rh26_import_conductor_one_zeta)%
  . forall chi : GC26DirichletCharacter . GC26primitive(GC26primitiveAncestor(chi))
      %(rh26_import_primitive_source)%
  . forall chi : GC26DirichletCharacter; s : RH26Complex . rh26Strip(s) =>
      (rh26L(chi,s)=rh26C0 <=> rh26L(GC26primitiveAncestor(chi),s)=rh26C0)
      %(rh26_import_induction_preserves_strip_zeros)%
  . rh26RH <=> rh26RHStrip %(rh26_full_strip_equivalence)% %implied
  . rh26GRHPrimitive <=> rh26GRHAll %(rh26_primitive_all_equivalence)% %implied
  . rh26GRHPrimitive => rh26RH %(rh26_GRH_returns_RH)% %implied
end

spec RH26_IMPORTED_ROBIN_LAGARIAS = RH26_STANDARD_ANALYTIC_IMPORTS then
  %% Classical source theorems: Robin (1984); Lagarias (2002), Thm 1.1.
  %% Independently formalized theorem names in kimihiro64/Robin1984:
  %% Robin1984.robin_inequality_iff_riemannHypothesis
  %% Robin1984.riemann_hypothesis_iff_lagarias_elementary_criterion
  %% Importing the equivalences does not assert RH.
  . rh26RobinAll <=> rh26RH %(rh26_import_Robin_1984_equivalence)%
  . rh26RH <=> rh26LagariasAll %(rh26_import_Lagarias_2002_equivalence)%
  %% Lagarias Problem E additionally gives strictness for every n>1 under RH.
  . rh26RH => rh26LagariasStrict %(rh26_import_Lagarias_strictness)%
  %% Proof: compose the indicated directions of the two imported equivalences.
  . rh26RobinAll <=> rh26LagariasAll %(rh26_robin_lagarias_return)% %implied
  . rh26GRHPrimitive => rh26RobinAll /\ rh26LagariasAll /\ rh26LagariasStrict
      %(rh26_GRH_arithmetic_returns)% %implied
  . rh26LagariasAll => rh26LagariasStrict %(rh26_weak_returns_strict)% %implied
  %% These are pointwise contradiction returns, with an explicit violating n.
  . forall n : GC26Nat . h24LtN(rh26Cut5040,n) /\
      not rh26LtR(rh26NatR(rh26Sigma(n)),rh26RobinBound(n)) => not rh26RH
      %(rh26_robin_counterexample_return)% %implied
  . forall n : GC26Nat . h24LtN(GC26zero,n) /\
      not rh26LeR(rh26NatR(rh26Sigma(n)),rh26LagariasBound(n)) => not rh26RH
      %(rh26_lagarias_counterexample_return)% %implied
end

spec RH26_GRH_PREMISE_BRANCH = RH26_IMPORTED_ROBIN_LAGARIAS then
  %% Explicit scenario assumption; its label is retained by every return.
  . rh26GRHPrimitive %(rh26_assume_Dirichlet_GRH)%
  . rh26RH %(rh26_scenario_RH)% %implied
  . rh26RobinAll %(rh26_scenario_Robin)% %implied
  . rh26LagariasAll %(rh26_scenario_Lagarias)% %implied
  . rh26LagariasStrict %(rh26_scenario_Lagarias_strict)% %implied
end

spec RH26_EXTERNAL_2026_DIRICHLET_GRH = RH26_IMPORTED_ROBIN_LAGARIAS then
  %% External-source theorem interface, separate from the scenario branch.
  %% Andrew Sutherland's MIT verification page, accessed 2026-11-21:
  %% https://math.mit.edu/~drew/grh/
  %% Audited source theorem: `LFunction_eq_zero_re` in module Main
  %% (all Dirichlet characters; the declaration is unnamespaced).
  %% Page reports independent Lean replays; those were not repeated by this file.
  %% Exact audited dependencies: propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.
  %% The institution translation preserves these dependencies; it does not erase
  %% Classical.choice or certify an axiom-independent constructive replacement.
  %% Scope is Dirichlet GRH. General Hecke/automorphic/Dedekind GRH is not imported.
  . rh26GRHAll %(rh26_external_2026_LFunction_eq_zero_re)%
  . rh26GRHPrimitive %(rh26_external_primitive_return)% %implied
  . rh26RH %(rh26_external_RH_return)% %implied
  . rh26RobinAll %(rh26_external_Robin_return)% %implied
  . rh26LagariasAll %(rh26_external_Lagarias_return)% %implied
end

spec RH26_DAGGER_ZERO_RETURN = RH26_STANDARD_ANALYTIC_IMPORTS then
  ops rh26Reflect : RH26Complex -> RH26Complex
  %% Complex conjugation and affine reflection are two commuting involutions.
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26Conj(rh26Conj(s))=s %(rh26_conj_involution)%
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26OneMinus(rh26OneMinus(s))=s %(rh26_flip_involution)%
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26Conj(rh26OneMinus(s))=
      rh26OneMinus(rh26Conj(s)) %(rh26_commuting_involutions)%
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26Reflect(s)=rh26OneMinus(rh26Conj(s))
      %(rh26_reflection_definition)%
  %% Ordinary complex-coordinate identity: s=1-conj(s) iff Re(s)=1/2.
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26Reflect(s)=s <=> rh26OnLine(s)
      %(rh26_complex_fixed_point_identity)%
  . forall s : RH26Complex . rh26Reflect(rh26Reflect(s))=s
      %(rh26_reflection_involution)% %implied
  %% This is the exact RH/GRH fixed-point target, on the original zero carrier.
  . rh26RH <=> (forall s : RH26Complex .
      rh26NontrivialZetaZero(s) => rh26Reflect(s)=s)
      %(rh26_RH_fixed_point_return)% %implied
  . rh26GRHAll <=> (forall chi : GC26DirichletCharacter; s : RH26Complex .
      rh26Strip(s) /\ rh26L(chi,s)=rh26C0 => rh26Reflect(s)=s)
      %(rh26_GRH_fixed_point_return)% %implied
end

%% ================================================================
%% TR26. CONSTRUCTIVE TORSER, COLLATZ AND INSTITUTION RETURN INTERFACES
%% ================================================================
%% Archive provenance: Tesseract_Moonshine_Theta_Return.het version 17;
%% Hexaholographic_Full_Goals_RH_GRH.het version 23 (APDyadicAddressData,
%% APCollatzAffineReturn, HPPAdjunctionReturns); Native_Shortest_Proof.het
%% version 9 (CollatzSpectralReturn). Their branch convention is
%% T(x)=x/2 for even x, T(x)=(3*x+1)/2 for odd x.
%% The following is a small independent extraction with new symbol names.
%% It retains the original source model through every representation.

%% A Boolean group, specialized by an explicit two-point carrier.
%% The proofs below use its group equations, not enumeration of 16 states.
spec TR26BitCarrier =
  free type TR26Bit ::= tr26Off | tr26On
end
spec TR26BitGroup = TR26BitCarrier and
  { H24_BooleanGroup with H24Bit |-> TR26Bit, h24Off |-> tr26Off, h24Xor |-> tr26Xor }
end
spec TR26PointCarrier = TR26BitCarrier then
  free type TR26Point ::= tr26Point(tr26Input:TR26Bit;tr26Carry:TR26Bit;
                                   tr26N:TR26Bit;tr26B:TR26Bit)
  forall p : TR26Point
  %% Explicit product eta preserves free-record coverage in first-order exports.
  . tr26Point(tr26Input(p),tr26Carry(p),tr26N(p),tr26B(p))=p
      %(tr26_point_product_eta)%
end

spec TR26TesseractData = TR26PointCarrier and TR26BitGroup then
  ops tr26Origin : TR26Point;
      tr26Translate,tr26Difference : TR26Point * TR26Point -> TR26Point
  . tr26Origin=tr26Point(tr26Off,tr26Off,tr26Off,tr26Off)
  forall p,q : TR26Point
  . tr26Translate(p,q)=tr26Point(
      tr26Xor(tr26Input(p),tr26Input(q)),
      tr26Xor(tr26Carry(p),tr26Carry(q)),
      tr26Xor(tr26N(p),tr26N(q)),tr26Xor(tr26B(p),tr26B(q)))
      %(tr26_tesseract_translation_definition)%
  . tr26Difference(p,q)=tr26Translate(p,q)
      %(tr26_torsor_difference_definition)%
end

%% In each coordinate, (u+w)+w=u+(w+w)=u. Hence difference(p,q)
%% sends p to q. If translate(p,w)=q, translate p on both sides;
%% the same cancellation gives w=difference(p,q). This constructs the
%% unique transporter and proves free/transitive action in one argument.
spec TR26TesseractReturns = TR26TesseractData then
  forall p,q,w : TR26Point
  . tr26Translate(tr26Translate(p,w),w)=p
      %(tr26_tesseract_involution_return)% %implied
  . tr26Translate(p,tr26Difference(p,q))=q
      %(tr26_constructed_transporter)% %implied
  . tr26Translate(p,w)=q => w=tr26Difference(p,q)
      %(tr26_unique_transporter)% %implied
  . tr26Translate(p,tr26Origin)=p
      %(tr26_tesseract_identity)% %implied
end

%% Bitstream/coinductive interface. The intended TR26Stream is 2^N.
%% Every stream is exposed by one head and one tail. This carrier is
%% distinct from the four-bit controller and from its finite quotients.
spec TR26StreamData = TR26BitCarrier then
  sort TR26Stream
  ops tr26Head : TR26Stream -> TR26Bit;
      tr26Tail : TR26Stream -> TR26Stream;
      tr26ConsStream : TR26Bit * TR26Stream -> TR26Stream
  forall b : TR26Bit; s : TR26Stream
  . tr26Head(tr26ConsStream(b,s))=b %(tr26_stream_head_beta)%
  . tr26Tail(tr26ConsStream(b,s))=s %(tr26_stream_tail_beta)%
  . tr26ConsStream(tr26Head(s),tr26Tail(s))=s %(tr26_stream_eta)%
end

%% This algebraic interface is interpreted in Z_2 using the displayed
%% inverse branches B_0(y)=2y and B_1(y)=(2y-1)/3. Three is an odd unit.
%% The beta/eta laws follow by substituting into the correct parity branch:
%% T(2y)=y and T((2y-1)/3)=y; B_parity(x)(T(x))=x.
%% These are source laws with written proofs, not a Collatz-termination axiom.
spec TR26CollatzBranches = TR26BitCarrier then
  sort TR26Z2
  ops tr26Parity : TR26Z2 -> TR26Bit;
      tr26Collatz : TR26Z2 -> TR26Z2;
      tr26Branch : TR26Bit * TR26Z2 -> TR26Z2
  forall b : TR26Bit; x : TR26Z2
  . tr26Parity(tr26Branch(b,x))=b %(tr26_branch_parity)%
  . tr26Collatz(tr26Branch(b,x))=x %(tr26_branch_beta)%
  . tr26Branch(tr26Parity(x),tr26Collatz(x))=x %(tr26_branch_eta)%
end

spec TR26CollatzWords = TR26CollatzBranches and
  { H24_WordCarrier with H24Letter |-> TR26Bit, H24Word |-> TR26Word,
    h24Nil |-> tr26Nil, h24Cons |-> tr26Cons } then
  ops tr26Build,tr26Run : TR26Word * TR26Z2 -> TR26Z2
  pred tr26Matches : TR26Word * TR26Z2
  forall x : TR26Z2; b : TR26Bit; w : TR26Word
  . tr26Build(tr26Nil,x)=x %(tr26_build_base)%
  . tr26Build(tr26Cons(b,w),x)=tr26Branch(b,tr26Build(w,x))
      %(tr26_build_step)%
  . tr26Run(tr26Nil,x)=x %(tr26_run_base)%
  . tr26Run(tr26Cons(b,w),x)=tr26Run(w,tr26Collatz(x))
      %(tr26_run_step)%
  . tr26Matches(tr26Nil,x) %(tr26_matches_base)%
  . tr26Matches(tr26Cons(b,w),x) <=>
      tr26Parity(x)=b /\ tr26Matches(w,tr26Collatz(x))
      %(tr26_matches_step)%
end
%% Explicit base and induction-step receivers, independently available to
%% first-order proof replay. Free-word induction then proves all words.
spec TR26WordInductionData = TR26CollatzWords then
  preds tr26TailReturnAt,tr26PointReturnAt,tr26MembershipAt : TR26Word
  forall w : TR26Word
  . tr26TailReturnAt(w) <=> (forall x : TR26Z2 .
      tr26Run(w,tr26Build(w,x))=x) %(tr26_tail_induction_property)%
  . tr26PointReturnAt(w) <=> (forall x : TR26Z2 . tr26Matches(w,x) =>
      tr26Build(w,tr26Run(w,x))=x) %(tr26_point_induction_property)%
  . tr26MembershipAt(w) <=> (forall x : TR26Z2 .
      tr26Matches(w,tr26Build(w,x))) %(tr26_membership_induction_property)%
end

spec TR26WordInductionReturns = TR26WordInductionData then
  . tr26TailReturnAt(tr26Nil) %(tr26_tail_return_base)% %implied
  . tr26PointReturnAt(tr26Nil) %(tr26_point_return_base)% %implied
  . tr26MembershipAt(tr26Nil) %(tr26_membership_base)% %implied
  forall b : TR26Bit; w : TR26Word
  . tr26TailReturnAt(w) => tr26TailReturnAt(tr26Cons(b,w))
      %(tr26_tail_return_induction_step)% %implied
  . tr26PointReturnAt(w) => tr26PointReturnAt(tr26Cons(b,w))
      %(tr26_point_return_induction_step)% %implied
  . tr26MembershipAt(w) => tr26MembershipAt(tr26Cons(b,w))
      %(tr26_membership_induction_step)% %implied
end

%% Structural induction on w. Base is identity. At Cons(b,w), beta
%% cancels B_b before the induction hypothesis; eta cancels it in the
%% opposite direction once Matches supplies parity(x)=b. This is the
%% original tail return for every word, with no search over prefixes.
spec TR26CollatzWordReturns = TR26CollatzWords then
  forall w : TR26Word; x : TR26Z2
  . tr26Run(w,tr26Build(w,x))=x
      %(tr26_word_original_tail_return)% %implied
  . tr26Matches(w,tr26Build(w,x))
      %(tr26_word_cylinder_membership)% %implied
  . tr26Matches(w,x) => tr26Build(w,tr26Run(w,x))=x
      %(tr26_word_original_point_return)% %implied
end

%% Compatible parity coding is supplied as a coalgebra morphism.
%% The inverse-limit realization is the unique compatible Z_2 point;
%% Q^{-1}(s)=-sum_{n>=0} s_n*2^n / 3^{s_0+...+s_n}.
%% Each term has valuation at least n; terms at zero bits vanish. Finite
%% prefixes therefore determine residues without deciding whether the stream
%% contains finitely many ones. This construction needs no selection from
%% nonempty fibres. Raw binary digits and parity digits
%% remain different coordinate systems.
spec TR26ParityCoding = TR26CollatzBranches and TR26StreamData then
  ops tr26ParityCode : TR26Z2 -> TR26Stream;
      tr26ParityDecode : TR26Stream -> TR26Z2
  forall x : TR26Z2; s : TR26Stream
  . tr26Head(tr26ParityCode(x))=tr26Parity(x)
      %(tr26_parity_code_head)%
  . tr26Tail(tr26ParityCode(x))=tr26ParityCode(tr26Collatz(x))
      %(tr26_parity_code_shift)%
  . tr26ParityDecode(tr26ParityCode(x))=x
      %(tr26_import_constructed_parity_inverse_limit_return)%
  . tr26ParityCode(tr26ParityDecode(s))=s
      %(tr26_import_constructed_stream_inverse_limit_return)%
end

spec TR26ParityCodingReturns = TR26ParityCoding then
  forall x,y : TR26Z2; s : TR26Stream
  . tr26Collatz(tr26ParityDecode(s))=tr26ParityDecode(tr26Tail(s))
      %(tr26_collatz_shift_conjugacy_return)% %implied
end

%% Predicate-fibre version of Sigma_q |- q* |- Pi_q. This is an exact
%% logical schema with definable predicate operations. Its proof uses
%% witness introduction/elimination and universal evaluation only.
spec TR26FibreData =
  sorts TR26X, TR26Y, TR26PredX, TR26PredY
  ops tr26Q : TR26X -> TR26Y;
      tr26Pull : TR26PredY -> TR26PredX;
      tr26Sigma,tr26Pi : TR26PredX -> TR26PredY
  preds tr26HoldsX : TR26PredX * TR26X;
        tr26HoldsY : TR26PredY * TR26Y;
        tr26BelowX : TR26PredX * TR26PredX;
        tr26BelowY : TR26PredY * TR26PredY
  forall a,b : TR26PredX; c,d : TR26PredY; x : TR26X; y : TR26Y
  . tr26HoldsX(tr26Pull(c),x) <=> tr26HoldsY(c,tr26Q(x))
      %(tr26_fibre_pull_definition)%
  . tr26HoldsY(tr26Sigma(a),y) <=>
      (exists z : TR26X . tr26Q(z)=y /\ tr26HoldsX(a,z))
      %(tr26_fibre_sigma_definition)%
  . tr26HoldsY(tr26Pi(a),y) <=>
      (forall z : TR26X . tr26Q(z)=y => tr26HoldsX(a,z))
      %(tr26_fibre_pi_definition)%
  . tr26BelowX(a,b) <=> (forall z : TR26X . tr26HoldsX(a,z) => tr26HoldsX(b,z))
      %(tr26_predicate_order_x)%
  . tr26BelowY(c,d) <=> (forall z : TR26Y . tr26HoldsY(c,z) => tr26HoldsY(d,z))
      %(tr26_predicate_order_y)%
end

%% Sigma: send (x,qx=y,a(x)) to c(qx), and in reverse insert x as witness.
%% Pi: evaluate its supplied function at x; in reverse abstract over every
%% x with qx=y. Both equivalences are intuitionistically valid.
spec TR26FibreReturns = TR26FibreData then
  forall a : TR26PredX; c : TR26PredY
  . tr26BelowY(tr26Sigma(a),c) <=> tr26BelowX(a,tr26Pull(c))
      %(tr26_sigma_pull_adjunction)% %implied
  . tr26BelowX(tr26Pull(c),a) <=> tr26BelowY(c,tr26Pi(a))
      %(tr26_pull_pi_adjunction)% %implied
  . tr26BelowX(a,tr26Pull(tr26Sigma(a)))
      %(tr26_sigma_unit)% %implied
  . tr26BelowY(tr26Sigma(tr26Pull(c)),c)
      %(tr26_sigma_counit)% %implied
  . tr26BelowY(c,tr26Pi(tr26Pull(c)))
      %(tr26_pi_unit)% %implied
  . tr26BelowX(tr26Pull(tr26Pi(a)),a)
      %(tr26_pi_counit)% %implied
end

%% Pullback is a subtype predicate on the full pair carrier, so the actual
%% pullback may be empty. It is never assumed to have a selected element.
spec TR26BaseChangeData = TR26FibreData then
  sort TR26YPrime
  free type TR26Pair ::= tr26Pair(tr26PX:TR26X;tr26PY:TR26YPrime)
  op tr26G : TR26YPrime -> TR26Y
  preds tr26ValidPair : TR26Pair;
        tr26SigmaBaseChange,tr26PiBaseChange : TR26PredX * TR26YPrime
  forall w : TR26Pair; a : TR26PredX; y : TR26YPrime
  . tr26ValidPair(w) <=> tr26Q(tr26PX(w))=tr26G(tr26PY(w))
      %(tr26_pullback_pair_definition)%
  . tr26SigmaBaseChange(a,y) <=>
      (exists v : TR26Pair . tr26ValidPair(v) /\ tr26PY(v)=y /\
        tr26HoldsX(a,tr26PX(v))) %(tr26_sigma_base_change_definition)%
  . tr26PiBaseChange(a,y) <=>
      (forall v : TR26Pair . tr26ValidPair(v) /\ tr26PY(v)=y =>
        tr26HoldsX(a,tr26PX(v))) %(tr26_pi_base_change_definition)%
end

%% Pair x with the supplied y in one direction; project x in the other.
%% The two maps are inverse by the pair beta/eta laws. Universals are
%% transported by precomposition with these same inverse maps.
spec TR26BaseChangeReturns = TR26BaseChangeData then
  forall a : TR26PredX; y : TR26YPrime
  . tr26SigmaBaseChange(a,y) <=> tr26HoldsY(tr26Sigma(a),tr26G(y))
      %(tr26_beck_chevalley_sigma)% %implied
  . tr26PiBaseChange(a,y) <=> tr26HoldsY(tr26Pi(a),tr26G(y))
      %(tr26_beck_chevalley_pi)% %implied
end

%% Signature-indexed satisfaction fragment of an institution comorphism.
%% Full signature-arrow functoriality/naturality is supplied by a chosen
%% logic translation; these schemas do not manufacture that translation.
%% Two evidence rails are independent predicates. Neither is defined as
%% the Boolean negation of the other. CASL is the metatheory for the rails.
spec TR26InstitutionData =
  sorts TR26SourceSig, TR26TargetSig, TR26SourceSentence,
        TR26TargetSentence, TR26SourceModel, TR26TargetModel
  free type TR26Rail ::= tr26Support | tr26Opposition
  ops tr26SentenceSig : TR26SourceSentence -> TR26SourceSig;
      tr26ModelSig : TR26SourceModel -> TR26SourceSig;
      tr26TargetSentenceSig : TR26TargetSentence -> TR26TargetSig;
      tr26TargetModelSig : TR26TargetModel -> TR26TargetSig;
      tr26TranslateSig : TR26SourceSig -> TR26TargetSig;
      tr26TranslateSentence : TR26SourceSentence -> TR26TargetSentence;
      tr26Reduct : TR26SourceSig * TR26TargetModel -> TR26SourceModel
  preds tr26SourceHolds : TR26Rail * TR26SourceModel * TR26SourceSentence;
        tr26TargetHolds : TR26Rail * TR26TargetModel * TR26TargetSentence
  forall sig : TR26SourceSig; n : TR26TargetModel
  . tr26TargetModelSig(n)=tr26TranslateSig(sig) =>
      tr26ModelSig(tr26Reduct(sig,n))=sig
      %(tr26_reduct_typing)%
  forall f : TR26SourceSentence; n : TR26TargetModel; r : TR26Rail
  . tr26TargetSentenceSig(tr26TranslateSentence(f))=tr26TranslateSig(tr26SentenceSig(f))
      %(tr26_sentence_translation_typing)%
  . tr26TargetModelSig(n)=tr26TranslateSig(tr26SentenceSig(f)) =>
      (tr26TargetHolds(r,n,tr26TranslateSentence(f)) <=>
       tr26SourceHolds(r,tr26Reduct(tr26SentenceSig(f),n),f))
      %(tr26_signed_satisfaction_law)%
end

%% A supplied expansion is an actual model witness. Its section equation
%% is precisely the extra data required to reflect universal source truth.
spec TR26ModelExpansionData = TR26InstitutionData then
  op tr26Expand : TR26SourceModel -> TR26TargetModel
  forall m : TR26SourceModel
  . tr26TargetModelSig(tr26Expand(m))=tr26TranslateSig(tr26ModelSig(m))
      %(tr26_expansion_typing)%
  . tr26Reduct(tr26ModelSig(m),tr26Expand(m))=m
      %(tr26_original_model_section)%
end

spec TR26InstitutionReturns = TR26ModelExpansionData then
  forall m : TR26SourceModel; f : TR26SourceSentence; r : TR26Rail
  . tr26ModelSig(m)=tr26SentenceSig(f) =>
      (tr26TargetHolds(r,tr26Expand(m),tr26TranslateSentence(f)) <=>
       tr26SourceHolds(r,m,f))
      %(tr26_same_source_model_return)% %implied
  forall f : TR26SourceSentence; r : TR26Rail
  . (forall n : TR26TargetModel .
       tr26TargetModelSig(n)=tr26TranslateSig(tr26SentenceSig(f)) =>
       tr26TargetHolds(r,n,tr26TranslateSentence(f))) =>
    (forall m : TR26SourceModel . tr26ModelSig(m)=tr26SentenceSig(f) =>
       tr26SourceHolds(r,m,f))
      %(tr26_universal_source_reflection_with_expansion)% %implied
end

%% Proof: instantiate target validity at Expand(m), use its signature
%% equation, apply the satisfaction law, and rewrite the supplied section.
%% No selection, classical contraposition, or erased source hypothesis
%% occurs. This proof is portable across any interpretation satisfying
%% the displayed interface. It is not an unconditional source RH proof.

%% MBR26. ONE FAITHFUL FOUR-BY-FOUR RETURN FOR COEFFICIENTS AND MARKED POINTS.
%% Required algebra: a commutative ring with a specified inverse of 3.
%% No field, characteristic-zero, analytic, RH or GRH premise is imported.
%% The scalar involution is a parameter, later instantiated at 1-conj(s).
%% Symbolic matrix verification accompanies the written ring proofs below.

spec MBR26_CoefficientStar = MT26_GroupAlgebraReturn and MT26_DaggerReturn then
  ops MBR_star6 : MT26_V6 -> MT26_V6;
      MBR_fourth6 : MT26_V6 -> MT26_R
  forall a,b,c,d,e,f : MT26_R
  . MBR_star6(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))=MT26_v6(a,b,c,e,d,f)
      %(mbr26_star_definition)%
  . MBR_fourth6(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))=d %(mbr26_fourth_coefficient_definition)%
end

%% In the basis (I,S,T,ST,TS,STS), inversion fixes I,S,T,STS and swaps
%% ST with TS. Each generator is Q-self-adjoint; (UV)^dagger=V^dagger U^dagger.
%% The dagger is scalar-linear, so the same assertion holds for the complete
%% linear extension. Apply the already constructed coefficient decoder to
%% recover the original inverted coefficients. This is word induction and
%% linearity, not an enumeration of states in the two-adic inverse limit.
spec MBR26_CoefficientStarReturns = MBR26_CoefficientStar then
  forall a,b,c,d,e,f : MT26_R
  . MBR_star6(MBR_star6(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f)))=MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f)
      %(mbr26_coefficient_star_involution)% %implied
  . MT26_encode6(MBR_star6(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f)))=
      MT26_dagger(MT26_encode6(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f)))
      %(mbr26_encode_intertwines_dagger)% %implied
  . MT26_decode6(MT26_dagger(MT26_encode6(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))))=
      MBR_star6(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))
      %(mbr26_dagger_coefficient_return)% %implied
  . MT26_dagger(MT26_encode6(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f)))=
      MT26_encode6(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f)) <=> d=e
      %(mbr26_self_adjoint_coefficients)% %implied
end

spec MBR26_InvolutionEncoding = MBR26_CoefficientStarReturns then
  op MBR_reflect : MT26_R -> MT26_R
  ops MBR_E6 : MT26_R -> MT26_V6;
      MBR_E4 : MT26_R -> MT26_M4;
      MBR_decode : MT26_M4 -> MT26_R
  forall s : MT26_R; M : MT26_M4
  . MBR_reflect(MBR_reflect(s))=s %(mbr26_supplied_scalar_involution)%
  . MBR_E6(s)=MT26_v6(MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,s,MBR_reflect(s),MT26_z)
      %(mbr26_marked_coefficient_encoding)%
  . MBR_E4(s)=MT26_encode6(MBR_E6(s)) %(mbr26_faithful_matrix_encoding)%
  . MBR_decode(M)=MBR_fourth6(MT26_decode6(M)) %(mbr26_original_scalar_decoder)%
end

%% Let e=encode6, d=decode6. The first return is proj4(d(e(E6(s))))=s.
%% star(E6(s))=E6(reflect(s)) follows by reflect^2=id. Consequently
%% dagger(E4(s))=e(star(E6(s)))=E4(reflect(s)). If this equals E4(s),
%% apply the retained decoder to obtain reflect(s)=s; the converse is
%% substitution. These steps are intuitionistic equalities and compose
%% through the same retraction theorem used by Galois and Collatz charts.
%% The closed matrix is diag((s+r)I2,[[-r,r-s],[s-r,-s]]), r=reflect(s).
%% It is the faithful C2-plus-standard representation, not a new matrix lens.
spec MBR26_InvolutionEncodingReturns = MBR26_InvolutionEncoding then
  forall s,t : MT26_R
  . MBR_star6(MBR_E6(s))=MBR_E6(MBR_reflect(s))
      %(mbr26_scalar_involution_coefficient_equivariance)% %implied
  . MBR_decode(MBR_E4(s))=s %(mbr26_exact_original_scalar_return)% %implied
  . MBR_E4(s)=MBR_E4(t) => s=t %(mbr26_matrix_encoding_reflects_source_equality)% %implied
  . MT26_dagger(MBR_E4(s))=MBR_E4(MBR_reflect(s))
      %(mbr26_marked_matrix_dagger_equivariance)% %implied
  . MT26_dagger(MBR_E4(s))=MBR_E4(s) <=> MBR_reflect(s)=s
      %(mbr26_faithful_dagger_fixed_point_return)% %implied
  . MBR_E4(s)=MT26_block(MT26_add(s,MBR_reflect(s)),MT26_z,
      MT26_neg(MBR_reflect(s)),MT26_sub(MBR_reflect(s),s),
      MT26_sub(s,MBR_reflect(s)),MT26_neg(s))
      %(mbr26_marked_matrix_closed_formula)% %implied
end

%% INDEPENDENT LOW-ASSUMPTION INTERPRETATION ON THE ORIGINAL FOUR BIT TORSOR.
%% Reduction of the same integer generators modulo 2 acts on the retained
%% order (input,carry,N,B). Both actions preserve the unique transporter.
%% This subtheory requires only the existing Boolean group and product;
%% it does not import the preceding scalar ring or its inverse of 3.
spec MBR26_TorserFrameData = TR26TesseractData then
  ops MBR_Sbit,MBR_Tbit : TR26Point -> TR26Point
  forall p : TR26Point
  . MBR_Sbit(p)=tr26Point(tr26Carry(p),tr26Input(p),
      tr26Xor(tr26N(p),tr26B(p)),tr26B(p)) %(mbr26_S_mod_two_definition)%
  . MBR_Tbit(p)=tr26Point(tr26Carry(p),tr26Input(p),
      tr26N(p),tr26Xor(tr26N(p),tr26B(p))) %(mbr26_T_mod_two_definition)%
end

%% Each output is a sum of input coordinates; distributivity over XOR
%% therefore proves covariance of translation and of its constructed inverse.
%% S^2 and T^2 cancel a repeated bit. In ST the upper swap occurs twice,
%% while the lower block is [[0,1],[1,1]], whose cube is the identity.
spec MBR26_TorserFrameReturns = MBR26_TorserFrameData then
  forall p,q,w : TR26Point
  . MBR_Sbit(MBR_Sbit(p))=p %(mbr26_Sbit_involution)% %implied
  . MBR_Tbit(MBR_Tbit(p))=p %(mbr26_Tbit_involution)% %implied
  . MBR_Sbit(MBR_Tbit(MBR_Sbit(MBR_Tbit(MBR_Sbit(MBR_Tbit(p))))))=p
      %(mbr26_STbit_cube)% %implied
  . MBR_Sbit(tr26Translate(p,w))=tr26Translate(MBR_Sbit(p),MBR_Sbit(w))
      %(mbr26_Sbit_translation_covariance)% %implied
  . MBR_Tbit(tr26Translate(p,w))=tr26Translate(MBR_Tbit(p),MBR_Tbit(w))
      %(mbr26_Tbit_translation_covariance)% %implied
  . MBR_Sbit(tr26Difference(p,q))=tr26Difference(MBR_Sbit(p),MBR_Sbit(q))
      %(mbr26_Sbit_transporter_covariance)% %implied
  . MBR_Tbit(tr26Difference(p,q))=tr26Difference(MBR_Tbit(p),MBR_Tbit(q))
      %(mbr26_Tbit_transporter_covariance)% %implied
end

%% Root joining map to RH uses the SAME marked scalar and reflection:
%% Source=MT26_R, Image=MT26_M4, Encode=MBR_E4, Decode=MBR_decode.
%% If a comparison j:MT26_R->RH26Complex is injective and satisfies
%% j(MBR_reflect(s))=rh26Reflect(j(s)), then the fixed-point result returns
%% rh26OnLine(j(s)). A source assertion placing each original L-zero in this
%% fixed fibre remains a separate hypothesis/theorem with its original scope.

%% THE TORSER IS THE CHART BASE; THE WHOLE SOURCE REMAINS IN ITS FIBRE.
%% This construction is over the fixed (input,carry,N,B) controller.
%% Source/image maps are actual data, with image membership explicitly kept.
spec H24_FrameInterface =
  sort TR26Point
  op tr26Translate : TR26Point * TR26Point -> TR26Point
end
%% The full atlas binds this interface to the original tesseract. No frame
%% group equation is a premise of chart/packet cancellation or naturality.
spec H24_TorserCharts = H24_FrameInterface then
  sorts H24Payload,H24ChartValue
  ops h24ChartEncode : TR26Point * H24Payload -> H24ChartValue;
      h24ChartDecode : TR26Point * H24ChartValue -> H24Payload;
      h24Transition : TR26Point * TR26Point * H24ChartValue -> H24ChartValue
  pred h24InChart : TR26Point * H24ChartValue
  forall p,q : TR26Point; x : H24Payload; u : H24ChartValue
  . h24ChartDecode(p,h24ChartEncode(p,x))=x %(h24_chart_source_return)%
  . h24InChart(p,u) <=> h24ChartEncode(p,h24ChartDecode(p,u))=u
      %(h24_chart_image_definition)%
  . h24Transition(p,q,u)=h24ChartEncode(q,h24ChartDecode(p,u))
      %(h24_chart_transition_definition)%
then
  forall p,q,r,w : TR26Point; x : H24Payload; u : H24ChartValue
  . h24InChart(p,h24ChartEncode(p,x)) %(h24_encoding_lands_in_chart)% %implied
  . h24Transition(q,r,h24Transition(p,q,u))=h24Transition(p,r,u)
      %(h24_chart_triangle_composes)% %implied
  . h24InChart(p,u) => h24Transition(q,p,h24Transition(p,q,u))=u
      %(h24_chart_round_trip)% %implied
  . h24ChartDecode(q,h24Transition(p,q,u))=h24ChartDecode(p,u)
      %(h24_transition_preserves_original_payload)% %implied
  . h24Transition(p,tr26Translate(p,w),h24ChartEncode(p,x))=
      h24ChartEncode(tr26Translate(p,w),x) %(h24_torser_translation_lift)% %implied
end
%% The middle cancellation D_q E_q=id proves the triangle. The reverse
%% return uses image membership only at the original chart p. No choice.

%% A constructed universal payload chart, used by the actual matrix packet.
spec H24_PacketCharts = H24_FrameInterface then
  sort H24PacketPayload
  free type H24Packet ::= h24Packet(h24PacketFrame:TR26Point;h24PacketPayload:H24PacketPayload)
  ops h24PacketEncode : TR26Point * H24PacketPayload -> H24Packet;
      h24PacketDecode : TR26Point * H24Packet -> H24PacketPayload;
      h24PacketTransition : TR26Point * TR26Point * H24Packet -> H24Packet;
      h24PayloadMove : H24PacketPayload -> H24PacketPayload;
      h24LiftMove : H24Packet -> H24Packet
  pred h24PacketAt : TR26Point * H24Packet
  forall p,q : TR26Point; x : H24PacketPayload; u : H24Packet
  . h24Packet(h24PacketFrame(u),h24PacketPayload(u))=u %(h24_packet_eta)%
  . h24PacketEncode(p,x)=h24Packet(p,x) %(h24_packet_encoding)%
  . h24PacketDecode(p,u)=h24PacketPayload(u) %(h24_packet_decoding)%
  . h24PacketAt(p,u) <=> h24PacketFrame(u)=p %(h24_packet_chart_membership)%
  . h24PacketTransition(p,q,u)=h24Packet(q,h24PacketPayload(u)) %(h24_packet_transition)%
  . h24LiftMove(u)=h24Packet(h24PacketFrame(u),h24PayloadMove(h24PacketPayload(u)))
      %(h24_packet_lift_definition)%
then
  forall p,q : TR26Point; x : H24PacketPayload; u : H24Packet
  . h24PacketDecode(p,h24PacketEncode(p,x))=x %(h24_constructed_packet_source_return)% %implied
  . h24PacketAt(p,u) => h24PacketEncode(p,h24PacketDecode(p,u))=u
      %(h24_constructed_packet_image_return)% %implied
  . h24LiftMove(h24PacketTransition(p,q,u))=h24PacketTransition(p,q,h24LiftMove(u))
      %(h24_payload_move_commutes_with_chart_translation)% %implied
end

view H24ConstructedPacketCharts : H24_TorserCharts to H24_PacketCharts =
  H24Payload |-> H24PacketPayload, H24ChartValue |-> H24Packet,
  h24ChartEncode |-> h24PacketEncode, h24ChartDecode |-> h24PacketDecode,
  h24Transition |-> h24PacketTransition, h24InChart |-> h24PacketAt
end

%% Actual specialization: the payload is the faithful programme matrix,
%% and its operation is the already constructed bilinear Q-dagger.
spec H24_MatrixPackets = MT26_DaggerReturn and
  { H24_PacketCharts with H24PacketPayload |-> MT26_M4, h24PayloadMove |-> MT26_dagger }
end

%% HILBERT 24: LOCAL CALIBRATION, WHOLE PROOFS, AND COMPOSABLE PROOF PATHS.
%% Recovered from Hexaholographic_Full_Goals_RH_GRH.het: OrderedChargeGroup /
%% CalibrationSteps (23131ff), HCProofData / HCWholeProofReturns /
%% HCMinimumReturn / HCNativeProofData (25230ff), HPOPath / HPONative (46448ff).
%% Rule tags additionally retain the actual assumptions used at each step.
%% Charges are a partial order; no total order, minimum selector, or optimizer.
spec HC24_OrderedChargeGroup = H24_AdditiveGroup with
  H24Scalar |-> HC24Charge, h24Zero |-> hc24Zero,
  h24Add |-> hc24Plus, h24Neg |-> hc24Minus
then
  pred hc24Nonnegative : HC24Charge
  forall a,b : HC24Charge
  . hc24Nonnegative(hc24Zero) %(hc24_cone_zero)%
  . hc24Nonnegative(a) /\ hc24Nonnegative(b) => hc24Nonnegative(hc24Plus(a,b))
      %(hc24_cone_addition)%
  . hc24Nonnegative(a) /\ hc24Nonnegative(b) /\ hc24Plus(a,b)=hc24Zero =>
      a=hc24Zero /\ b=hc24Zero %(hc24_cone_pointed)%
end

spec HC24_ProofData = HC24_OrderedChargeGroup then
  sorts HC24Judgement,HC24Rule,HC24Assumption
  free type HC24Slots ::= hc24EmptySlots | hc24ConsSlot(HC24Judgement;HC24Slots)
  free types HC24Proof ::= hc24Step(hc24Conclusion:HC24Judgement;hc24Charge:HC24Charge;
               hc24RuleTag:HC24Rule;hc24Children:HC24Forest);
             HC24Forest ::= hc24Empty | hc24Cons(HC24Proof;HC24Forest)
  ops hc24Potential : HC24Judgement -> HC24Charge;
      hc24LocalExcess : HC24Charge * HC24Charge * HC24Judgement -> HC24Charge;
      hc24PremisePotential : HC24Slots -> HC24Charge;
      hc24Premises : HC24Forest -> HC24Slots;
      hc24Work,hc24Excess : HC24Proof -> HC24Charge;
      hc24ForestWork,hc24ForestExcess : HC24Forest -> HC24Charge
  preds hc24Rule : HC24Rule * HC24Charge * HC24Slots * HC24Judgement;
        hc24Valid,hc24Tight : HC24Proof; hc24ForestValid,hc24ForestTight : HC24Forest;
        hc24NoMore : HC24Charge * HC24Charge;
        hc24RuleUses : HC24Rule * HC24Assumption;
        hc24Uses : HC24Proof * HC24Assumption; hc24ForestUses : HC24Forest * HC24Assumption
  forall p : HC24Proof; ps : HC24Forest; q : HC24Judgement; r : HC24Rule;
         w,a,b : HC24Charge; slots : HC24Slots; h : HC24Assumption
  . hc24LocalExcess(w,a,q)=hc24Plus(hc24Plus(w,a),hc24Minus(hc24Potential(q)))
  . hc24PremisePotential(hc24EmptySlots)=hc24Zero
  . hc24PremisePotential(hc24ConsSlot(q,slots))=hc24Plus(hc24Potential(q),hc24PremisePotential(slots))
  . hc24Premises(hc24Empty)=hc24EmptySlots
  . hc24Premises(hc24Cons(p,ps))=hc24ConsSlot(hc24Conclusion(p),hc24Premises(ps))
  . hc24ForestWork(hc24Empty)=hc24Zero
  . hc24ForestWork(hc24Cons(p,ps))=hc24Plus(hc24Work(p),hc24ForestWork(ps))
  . hc24Work(hc24Step(q,w,r,ps))=hc24Plus(w,hc24ForestWork(ps))
  . hc24ForestExcess(hc24Empty)=hc24Zero
  . hc24ForestExcess(hc24Cons(p,ps))=hc24Plus(hc24Excess(p),hc24ForestExcess(ps))
  . hc24Excess(hc24Step(q,w,r,ps))=hc24Plus(
      hc24LocalExcess(w,hc24PremisePotential(hc24Premises(ps)),q),hc24ForestExcess(ps))
  . hc24ForestValid(hc24Empty)
  . hc24ForestValid(hc24Cons(p,ps)) <=> hc24Valid(p) /\ hc24ForestValid(ps)
  . hc24Valid(hc24Step(q,w,r,ps)) <=> hc24Rule(r,w,hc24Premises(ps),q) /\ hc24ForestValid(ps)
  . hc24ForestTight(hc24Empty)
  . hc24ForestTight(hc24Cons(p,ps)) <=> hc24Tight(p) /\ hc24ForestTight(ps)
  . hc24Tight(hc24Step(q,w,r,ps)) <=>
      hc24LocalExcess(w,hc24PremisePotential(hc24Premises(ps)),q)=hc24Zero /\ hc24ForestTight(ps)
  . hc24NoMore(a,b) <=> hc24Nonnegative(hc24Plus(b,hc24Minus(a)))
  . hc24Rule(r,w,slots,q) => hc24Nonnegative(hc24LocalExcess(w,hc24PremisePotential(slots),q))
      %(hc24_local_calibration_of_each_admitted_rule)%
  . not hc24ForestUses(hc24Empty,h)
  . hc24ForestUses(hc24Cons(p,ps),h) <=> hc24Uses(p,h) \/ hc24ForestUses(ps,h)
  . hc24Uses(hc24Step(q,w,r,ps),h) <=> hc24RuleUses(r,h) \/ hc24ForestUses(ps,h)
end

spec HC24_WholeProofReturns = HC24_ProofData then
  forall p : HC24Proof; ps : HC24Forest
  . hc24ForestExcess(ps)=hc24Plus(hc24ForestWork(ps),hc24Minus(hc24PremisePotential(hc24Premises(ps))))
      %(hc24_all_premise_occurrences_telescope)% %implied
  . hc24Excess(p)=hc24Plus(hc24Work(p),hc24Minus(hc24Potential(hc24Conclusion(p))))
      %(hc24_whole_proof_charge_identity)% %implied
  . hc24ForestValid(ps) => hc24Nonnegative(hc24ForestExcess(ps))
      %(hc24_forest_nonnegative_excess)% %implied
  . hc24Valid(p) => hc24Nonnegative(hc24Excess(p))
      %(hc24_all_valid_proofs_calibrated)% %implied
  . hc24Valid(p) => hc24NoMore(hc24Potential(hc24Conclusion(p)),hc24Work(p))
      %(hc24_same_judgement_lower_bound)% %implied
  . hc24Valid(p) => (hc24Excess(p)=hc24Zero <=> hc24Tight(p))
      %(hc24_zero_excess_exactly_local_tightness)% %implied
end

spec HC24_MinimumReturn = HC24_WholeProofReturns then
  pred hc24Minimum : HC24Proof
  forall p : HC24Proof
  . hc24Minimum(p) <=> hc24Valid(p) /\ (forall q : HC24Proof .
      hc24Valid(q) /\ hc24Conclusion(q)=hc24Conclusion(p) => hc24NoMore(hc24Work(p),hc24Work(q)))
  . hc24Valid(p) /\ hc24Excess(p)=hc24Zero => hc24Minimum(p)
      %(hc24_zero_excess_proves_universal_same_judgement_minimum)% %implied
  . hc24Valid(p) /\ hc24Tight(p) => hc24Minimum(p)
      %(hc24_locally_tight_proof_is_minimum)% %implied
end
%% PROOF: simultaneous structural induction on the actual proof and forest.
%% Empty forest gives 0=0-0; Cons adds both induction hypotheses. At Step,
%% the sum of child potentials occurs once with each sign and cancels.
%% Hence Excess=Work-Potential(conclusion), with every premise occurrence.
%% Local calibration plus cone closure proves positivity for valid trees.
%% Pointedness makes a zero sum of positive local/child excesses tight;
%% the converse adds zeros. A zero-excess p has Work(p)=Potential(goal).
%% Every valid competitor of that identical goal has work above that value.
%% Whole-proof calibration and universal minimum are goals, not source axioms.

spec HC24_PathData = HC24_ProofData and
  { H24_WordAction with H24Letter |-> HC24Move, H24Word |-> HC24Path,
    h24Nil |-> hc24Stay, h24Cons |-> hc24Next, h24Append |-> hc24Append,
    H24FoldValue |-> HC24Proof, h24Step |-> hc24Apply, h24Fold |-> hc24Run } then
  ops hc24Gain,hc24Saved : HC24Path * HC24Proof -> HC24Charge
  preds hc24ImprovesAt : HC24Move * HC24Proof;
        hc24Admitted,hc24Attains : HC24Path * HC24Proof;
        hc24SupportIncluded : HC24Proof * HC24Proof
  forall a : HC24Move; u,v : HC24Path; p,q : HC24Proof
  . hc24SupportIncluded(p,q) <=> (forall h : HC24Assumption . hc24Uses(p,h) => hc24Uses(q,h))
  . hc24ImprovesAt(a,p) <=> hc24Valid(p) /\ hc24Valid(hc24Apply(a,p)) /\
      hc24Conclusion(hc24Apply(a,p))=hc24Conclusion(p) /\ hc24NoMore(hc24Work(hc24Apply(a,p)),hc24Work(p)) /\
      hc24SupportIncluded(hc24Apply(a,p),p)
  . hc24Admitted(hc24Stay,p) <=> hc24Valid(p)
  . hc24Admitted(hc24Next(a,u),p) <=> hc24ImprovesAt(a,p) /\ hc24Admitted(u,hc24Apply(a,p))
  . hc24Gain(u,p)=hc24Plus(hc24Work(p),hc24Minus(hc24Work(hc24Run(u,p))))
  . hc24Saved(hc24Stay,p)=hc24Zero
  . hc24Saved(hc24Next(a,u),p)=hc24Plus(
      hc24Plus(hc24Work(p),hc24Minus(hc24Work(hc24Apply(a,p)))),hc24Saved(u,hc24Apply(a,p)))
  . hc24Attains(u,p) <=> hc24Admitted(u,p) /\ hc24Excess(hc24Run(u,p))=hc24Zero
end
spec HC24_PathReturns = HC24_PathData and HC24_MinimumReturn then
  forall u,v,w : HC24Path; p : HC24Proof
  . hc24Admitted(hc24Append(u,v),p) <=> hc24Admitted(u,p) /\ hc24Admitted(v,hc24Run(u,p))
      %(hc24_composite_admissibility)% %implied
  . hc24Admitted(u,p) => hc24Valid(hc24Run(u,p)) /\ hc24Conclusion(hc24Run(u,p))=hc24Conclusion(p) /\
      hc24NoMore(hc24Work(hc24Run(u,p)),hc24Work(p)) %(hc24_valid_same_goal_improvement)% %implied
  . hc24Admitted(u,p) => hc24SupportIncluded(hc24Run(u,p),p)
      %(hc24_admitted_paths_do_not_add_assumptions)% %implied
  . hc24Saved(u,p)=hc24Gain(u,p) %(hc24_all_local_savings_telescope)% %implied
  . hc24Gain(hc24Append(u,v),p)=hc24Plus(hc24Gain(u,p),hc24Gain(v,hc24Run(u,p)))
      %(hc24_exact_saved_charge_cocycle)% %implied
  . hc24Admitted(u,p) => hc24Gain(u,p)=hc24Plus(hc24Excess(p),hc24Minus(hc24Excess(hc24Run(u,p))))
      %(hc24_same_goal_gain_is_removed_excess)% %implied
  . hc24Attains(u,p) => hc24Minimum(hc24Run(u,p)) /\ hc24Gain(u,p)=hc24Excess(p)
      %(hc24_supplied_zero_excess_endpoint_attains_the_full_minimum)% %implied
end
%% Induct on the first path with an arbitrary starting proof. Next uses the
%% SAME intermediate Apply(a,p); append/run recursion composes, and admitted
%% conditions preserve validity and goal. Cone closure composes work bounds.
%% Adjacent internal works cancel in Saved; the identical conclusion then
%% cancels its potential. A supplied tight endpoint invokes MinimumReturn.
%% Support induction composes membership implications at that same middle
%% proof: every assumption in the endpoint already occurs in the source.

spec HC24_ProofChart = H24_Retraction with
  H24Source |-> HC24Proof, H24Target |-> HC24NativeProof,
  h24Encode |-> hc24Encode, h24Decode |-> hc24Decode
end

spec HC24_NativeReturns = HC24_PathReturns and HC24_ProofChart then
  ops hc24NativeWork : HC24NativeProof -> HC24Charge;
      hc24NativeConclusion : HC24NativeProof -> HC24Judgement;
      hc24NativeRun : HC24Path * HC24NativeProof -> HC24NativeProof;
      hc24FirstPath,hc24SecondPath : HC24Path;
      hc24First,hc24Second : HC24Proof -> HC24Proof;
      hc24FirstNative,hc24SecondNative,hc24CompositeNative : HC24NativeProof -> HC24NativeProof
  preds hc24NativeValid,hc24NativeMinimum : HC24NativeProof;
        hc24NativeUses : HC24NativeProof * HC24Assumption
  forall x : HC24NativeProof; u : HC24Path; p : HC24Proof; h : HC24Assumption
  . hc24NativeWork(x)=hc24Work(hc24Decode(x))
  . hc24NativeConclusion(x)=hc24Conclusion(hc24Decode(x))
  . hc24NativeValid(x) <=> hc24Valid(hc24Decode(x))
  . hc24NativeUses(x,h) <=> hc24Uses(hc24Decode(x),h)
  . hc24NativeMinimum(x) <=> hc24NativeValid(x) /\ (forall y : HC24NativeProof .
      hc24NativeValid(y) /\ hc24NativeConclusion(y)=hc24NativeConclusion(x) =>
      hc24NoMore(hc24NativeWork(x),hc24NativeWork(y)))
  . hc24NativeRun(u,x)=hc24Encode(hc24Run(u,hc24Decode(x)))
  . hc24First(p)=hc24Run(hc24FirstPath,p)
  . hc24Second(p)=hc24Run(hc24SecondPath,p)
  . hc24FirstNative(x)=hc24NativeRun(hc24FirstPath,x)
  . hc24SecondNative(x)=hc24NativeRun(hc24SecondPath,x)
  . hc24CompositeNative(x)=hc24NativeRun(hc24Append(hc24FirstPath,hc24SecondPath),x)
then
  forall p : HC24Proof; x : HC24NativeProof; u,v : HC24Path; h : HC24Assumption
  . hc24NativeMinimum(hc24Encode(p)) <=> hc24Minimum(p)
      %(hc24_minimum_returns_through_the_actual_proof_chart)% %implied
  . hc24Attains(u,p) => hc24NativeMinimum(hc24NativeRun(u,hc24Encode(p)))
      %(hc24_calibrated_endpoint_returns_a_native_minimum)% %implied
  . hc24NativeRun(hc24Stay,hc24Encode(p))=hc24Encode(p)
      %(hc24_native_identity_on_encoded_proofs)% %implied
  . hc24NativeRun(hc24Append(u,v),x)=hc24NativeRun(v,hc24NativeRun(u,x))
      %(hc24_native_path_composition_cancels_the_middle_chart)% %implied
  . hc24Decode(hc24NativeRun(u,x))=hc24Run(u,hc24Decode(x))
      %(hc24_native_output_returns_the_whole_original_proof)% %implied
  . hc24NativeUses(hc24Encode(p),h) <=> hc24Uses(p,h)
      %(hc24_native_original_support_return)% %implied
  . hc24NativeUses(hc24NativeRun(u,x),h) <=> hc24Uses(hc24Run(u,hc24Decode(x)),h)
      %(hc24_native_path_support_return)% %implied
  . hc24Admitted(u,hc24Decode(x)) /\ hc24NativeUses(hc24NativeRun(u,x),h) => hc24NativeUses(x,h)
      %(hc24_native_path_assumption_nonincrease)% %implied
  . hc24Admitted(u,hc24Decode(x)) => hc24NativeConclusion(hc24NativeRun(u,x))=hc24NativeConclusion(x) /\
      hc24Plus(hc24NativeWork(x),hc24Minus(hc24NativeWork(hc24NativeRun(u,x))))=hc24Gain(u,hc24Decode(x))
      %(hc24_native_same_goal_and_complete_charge_return)% %implied
end
%% Decode every native competitor, apply the source minimum, and read its
%% unchanged goal/work. Reverse by encoding every source competitor. For
%% path composition expand E Run_v D E Run_u D and cancel only D E=id,
%% exactly the middle cancellation of H24_TranslationComposition. No E D=id
%% on arbitrary native objects is required; identity is claimed on the image.

%% Two supplied paths instantiate the generic composition; neither is optimized.
view HC24NativePathTranslation : H24_TranslationComposition to HC24_NativeReturns =
  H24X |-> HC24Proof, H24Y |-> HC24Proof, H24Z |-> HC24Proof,
  H24NX |-> HC24NativeProof, H24NY |-> HC24NativeProof, H24NZ |-> HC24NativeProof,
  h24EX |-> hc24Encode, h24EY |-> hc24Encode, h24EZ |-> hc24Encode,
  h24DX |-> hc24Decode, h24DY |-> hc24Decode, h24DZ |-> hc24Decode,
  h24F |-> hc24First, h24G |-> hc24Second,
  h24Fsharp |-> hc24FirstNative, h24Gsharp |-> hc24SecondNative,
  h24GFsharp |-> hc24CompositeNative
end
%% The charge minimum is conditional on the supplied rule calibration and
%% zero-excess proof. It asserts neither existence nor cheap discovery of
%% such a proof. Uses records actual tagged-rule assumptions separately;
%% equal/minimum charge does not identify proofs or minimize assumption sets.
%% Every premise occurrence is charged. Shared DAG costs need their own model.

%% CONSTRUCTED PROOF PACKETS: the entire tagged proof is the chart payload.
%% Packet names are distinct from matrix packets; the common controller is
%% shared. A supplied frame gives a section, while paths retain every frame.
spec HC24_ProofPackets = HC24_PathReturns and
  { H24_PacketCharts with H24PacketPayload |-> HC24Proof, H24Packet |-> HC24ProofPacket,
    h24Packet |-> hc24ProofPacket, h24PacketFrame |-> hc24ProofPacketFrame,
    h24PacketPayload |-> hc24ProofPacketPayload, h24PacketEncode |-> hc24ProofPacketEncode,
    h24PacketDecode |-> hc24ProofPacketDecode, h24PacketTransition |-> hc24ProofPacketTransition,
    h24PacketAt |-> hc24ProofPacketAt, h24PayloadMove |-> hc24PacketProofMove,
    h24LiftMove |-> hc24LiftProofPacketMove }
then
  ops hc24PacketFrame : TR26Point; hc24PacketPath : HC24Path;
      hc24EncodePacket : HC24Proof -> HC24ProofPacket;
      hc24DecodePacket : HC24ProofPacket -> HC24Proof;
      hc24PacketRun : HC24Path * HC24ProofPacket -> HC24ProofPacket;
      hc24PacketWork : HC24ProofPacket -> HC24Charge
  pred hc24PacketUses : HC24ProofPacket * HC24Assumption
  forall p : HC24Proof; x : HC24ProofPacket; u : HC24Path; h : HC24Assumption
  . hc24EncodePacket(p)=hc24ProofPacketEncode(hc24PacketFrame,p)
  . hc24DecodePacket(x)=hc24ProofPacketPayload(x) %(n25s_packet_decode)%
  . hc24PacketProofMove(p)=hc24Run(hc24PacketPath,p)
  . hc24PacketRun(u,x)=hc24ProofPacket(hc24ProofPacketFrame(x),hc24Run(u,hc24DecodePacket(x)))
  . hc24PacketWork(x)=hc24Work(hc24DecodePacket(x))
  . hc24PacketUses(x,h) <=> hc24Uses(hc24DecodePacket(x),h) %(n25s_packet_uses)%
then
  forall p : HC24Proof; x : HC24ProofPacket; u,v : HC24Path; f,g : TR26Point; h : HC24Assumption
  . hc24DecodePacket(hc24EncodePacket(p))=p /\ hc24PacketWork(hc24EncodePacket(p))=hc24Work(p) /\
      (hc24PacketUses(hc24EncodePacket(p),h) <=> hc24Uses(p,h)) %(hc24_packet_original_proof_work_support)% %implied
  . hc24ProofPacketFrame(hc24PacketRun(u,x))=hc24ProofPacketFrame(x)
      %(hc24_proof_path_keeps_the_original_chart)% %implied
  . hc24DecodePacket(hc24PacketRun(u,x))=hc24Run(u,hc24DecodePacket(x))
      %(hc24_packet_path_returns_the_actual_output_proof)% %implied
  . hc24PacketRun(hc24Append(u,v),x)=hc24PacketRun(v,hc24PacketRun(u,x))
      %(hc24_all_chart_proof_path_composition)% %implied
  . hc24PacketRun(hc24Stay,x)=x %(hc24_constructed_packet_path_identity)% %implied
  . hc24PacketRun(hc24PacketPath,x)=hc24LiftProofPacketMove(x) %(hc24_selected_path_is_the_generic_packet_lift)% %implied
  . hc24PacketRun(u,hc24ProofPacketTransition(f,g,x))=hc24ProofPacketTransition(f,g,hc24PacketRun(u,x))
      %(hc24_proof_paths_commute_with_torser_recharting)% %implied
  . hc24PacketWork(hc24PacketRun(u,x))=hc24Work(hc24Run(u,hc24DecodePacket(x))) /\
      (hc24PacketUses(hc24PacketRun(u,x),h) <=> hc24Uses(hc24Run(u,hc24DecodePacket(x)),h))
      %(hc24_packet_output_work_and_support_are_exact)% %implied
  . hc24Admitted(u,hc24DecodePacket(x)) => hc24NoMore(hc24PacketWork(hc24PacketRun(u,x)),hc24PacketWork(x)) /\
      (hc24PacketUses(hc24PacketRun(u,x),h) => hc24PacketUses(x,h)) %(hc24_packet_improvement_adds_neither_cost_nor_assumptions)% %implied
end
%% Constructor beta/eta returns the exact proof and frame. Expanding Run
%% twice and using source-path composition proves the lifted composition.
%% Recharting changes only the frame; Run changes only the proof. Work and
%% support are their exact decoded readings, so the admitted-path bounds apply.
view HC24ConstructedProofChart : HC24_ProofChart to HC24_ProofPackets =
  HC24Proof |-> HC24Proof, HC24NativeProof |-> HC24ProofPacket,
  hc24Encode |-> hc24EncodePacket, hc24Decode |-> hc24DecodePacket
end

%% Instantiate the calibrated native theory at the CONSTRUCTED proof packet.
%% Its canonical NativeRun re-encodes in the supplied frame; PacketRun above
%% preserves every input frame. Their decoded proof/work/support agree.
spec HC24_CalibratedProofPackets = HC24_ProofPackets and
  { HC24_NativeReturns with HC24NativeProof |-> HC24ProofPacket,
    hc24Encode |-> hc24EncodePacket, hc24Decode |-> hc24DecodePacket,
    hc24NativeWork |-> hc24PacketWork, hc24NativeUses |-> hc24PacketUses }
then
  forall x : HC24ProofPacket; u : HC24Path
  . hc24NativeMinimum(x) <=> hc24Minimum(hc24DecodePacket(x))
      %(hc24_actual_packet_minimum_returns_the_original_proof)% %implied
  . hc24Attains(u,hc24DecodePacket(x)) => hc24NativeMinimum(hc24PacketRun(u,x))
      %(hc24_calibrated_minimum_in_every_retained_torser_frame)% %implied
end
%% Decode every competitor; in the reverse direction encode each source
%% competitor using the constructed section. PacketRun's exact output then
%% transports the attained minimum without changing the retained chart.

%% COMPOSED SOURCE RETURNS. Naturals and character labels are shared.
%% MT26_Chi is the actual nontrivial-primitive subsort; conductor one is
%% retained in GC26DirichletCharacter and in the full RH/GRH targets.
%% Only the complex scalar presentations require a comparison map.
spec AT26_DivisorCoefficientReturn = MT26_DivisorCoefficients and RH26_ARITHMETIC then
  forall n : GC26Nat
  . MT26_sigma(GC26one,n)=MT26_nat(rh26Sigma(n))
      %(at26_Eisenstein_and_Robin_use_the_same_divisor_sum)% %implied
end
%% Both sides reduce to the image of h24DivisorSum(1,n). This identifies
%% the arithmetic coefficient itself, before any analytic convergence claim.

spec AT26_CharacterBridge = MT26_SOURCE_CharacterThetaTheorems and
  RH26_STANDARD_ANALYTIC_IMPORTS then
  op at26ComplexToRH : MT26_R -> RH26Complex
  . at26ComplexToRH(MT26_z)=rh26C0 %(at26_zero_comparison)%
  forall s,t : MT26_R
  . at26ComplexToRH(s)=at26ComplexToRH(t) => s=t
      %(at26_complex_comparison_injective)%
  forall chi : MT26_Chi; s : MT26_R
  . rh26L(chi,at26ComplexToRH(s))=at26ComplexToRH(MT26_L(chi,s))
      %(at26_same_Dirichlet_L_comparison)%
end

%% The completion factor is defined precisely on the open-strip domain.
%% Its formula uses the SAME conductor, parity, power and Gamma operations.
%% Analytic inputs: positive-base complex powers never vanish; Gamma has
%% neither a pole nor a zero at (s+a)/2 when 0<Re(s)<1 and a in {0,1};
%% analytic continuation extends the completed-L identity to this strip.
%% Sources: DLMF 5.2(i), 25.15; the gamma/power sources are not RH premises.
spec AT26_CompletionStripImports = AT26_CharacterBridge then
  op at26CompletionFactor : MT26_Chi * MT26_R ->? MT26_R
  forall chi : MT26_Chi; s : MT26_R
  . def at26CompletionFactor(chi,s) <=> rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s))
      %(at26_completion_factor_domain)%
  . rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) =>
      def MT26_rpow(MT26_div(MT26_nat(GC26conductor(chi)),MT26_pi),MT26_halfShift(chi,s)) /\
      not MT26_rpow(MT26_div(MT26_nat(GC26conductor(chi)),MT26_pi),MT26_halfShift(chi,s))=MT26_z
      %(at26_import_positive_base_power_defined_nonzero)%
  . rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) =>
      def MT26_cGamma(MT26_halfShift(chi,s)) /\
      not MT26_cGamma(MT26_halfShift(chi,s))=MT26_z
      %(at26_import_strip_gamma_defined_nonzero)%
  . rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) => at26CompletionFactor(chi,s)=MT26_mul(
      MT26_rpow(MT26_div(MT26_nat(GC26conductor(chi)),MT26_pi),MT26_halfShift(chi,s)),
      MT26_cGamma(MT26_halfShift(chi,s))) %(at26_completion_factor_definition)%
  . rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) =>
      MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_mul(at26CompletionFactor(chi,s),MT26_L(chi,s))
      %(at26_import_continued_completion_identity)%
end

%% Proof: for defined nonzero p,g, the exhibited inverse inv(g)*inv(p)
%% satisfies (inv(g)*inv(p))*(p*g)=1. Apply H24_ZeroMonoid's proved
%% cancellation theorem, first to the completion factor, then to 2 in the
%% Mellin identity. Injectivity of the scalar comparison reflects the SAME
%% L-zero. No zero-equivalence or zero-location conclusion is an input.
%% More explicitly, write p=power, g=Gamma and F=p*g on the strip. If F=0,
%% g=inv(p)*(p*g)=0, contradicting g!=0; hence inv(F) exists. The completion
%% identity gives L=inv(F)*Lambda and Lambda=F*L, proving both directions.
%% Finally 2=nat(2)!=nat(0)=0 by characteristic zero, so inv(2)*Mellin=Lambda.
spec AT26_CharacterBridgeReturns = AT26_CompletionStripImports then
  forall chi : MT26_Chi; s : MT26_R
  . rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) => not at26CompletionFactor(chi,s)=MT26_z
      %(at26_completion_factor_nonzero)% %implied
  . rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) =>
      (MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_z <=> MT26_L(chi,s)=MT26_z)
      %(at26_completion_zero_equivalence_derived)% %implied
  . MT26_mellin(chi,s)=MT26_z <=> MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_z
      %(at26_mellin_zero_equivalence_derived)% %implied
  . rh26GRHPrimitive /\ rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) /\
      MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_z => rh26OnLine(at26ComplexToRH(s))
      %(at26_primitive_theta_completed_zero_line_return)% %implied
  . rh26GRHAll /\ rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) /\
      MT26_mellin(chi,s)=MT26_z => rh26OnLine(at26ComplexToRH(s))
      %(at26_mellin_original_zero_line_return)% %implied
end

%% This complex instance is checked before adjoining the generic scalar
%% involution contract. Hence its involution goal does not assume itself.
spec AT26_ComplexReflectionData = AT26_CharacterBridge and RH26_DAGGER_ZERO_RETURN then
  op MBR_reflect : MT26_R -> MT26_R
  forall s : MT26_R
  . MBR_reflect(s)=MT26_sub(MT26_o,MT26_conj(s)) %(at26_complex_reflection_formula)%
  . at26ComplexToRH(MBR_reflect(s))=rh26Reflect(at26ComplexToRH(s))
      %(at26_same_original_complex_reflection)%
end
spec AT26_ComplexReflectionReturns = AT26_ComplexReflectionData then
  forall s : MT26_R
  . MBR_reflect(MBR_reflect(s))=s %(at26_complex_instance_involution)% %implied
  . MBR_reflect(s)=s <=> rh26OnLine(at26ComplexToRH(s))
      %(at26_complex_instance_fixed_line)% %implied
end
%% Apply j to r(r(s)), use the RH scalar involution, then reflect equality
%% through j. For the fixed line, use the same square and j's injectivity.

spec AT26_FaithfulMatrixReturns = AT26_ComplexReflectionReturns and
  AT26_CharacterBridgeReturns and MBR26_InvolutionEncodingReturns then
  forall chi : MT26_Chi; s : MT26_R
  . MT26_dagger(MBR_E4(s))=MBR_E4(s) <=> rh26OnLine(at26ComplexToRH(s))
      %(at26_same_zero_faithful_matrix_line_return)% %implied
  . rh26GRHPrimitive /\ rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) /\ MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_z =>
      MT26_dagger(MBR_E4(s))=MBR_E4(s)
      %(at26_completed_theta_zero_original_matrix_return)% %implied
end

%% For complete original-carrier matrix targets, supply the inverse between
%% the two presentations of C. This extra source contract is unnecessary for
%% the preceding injective-comparison returns and is isolated here.
spec AT26_OriginalComplexPresentation = AT26_FaithfulMatrixReturns then
  ops at26RHScalar : RH26Complex -> MT26_R;
      at26EncodeOriginalZero : RH26Complex -> MT26_M4;
      at26DecodeOriginalZero : MT26_M4 -> RH26Complex
  forall s : RH26Complex; M : MT26_M4
  . at26ComplexToRH(at26RHScalar(s))=s %(at26_supplied_same_complex_inverse)%
  . at26EncodeOriginalZero(s)=MBR_E4(at26RHScalar(s)) %(at26_full_original_matrix_encoding)%
  . at26DecodeOriginalZero(M)=at26ComplexToRH(MBR_decode(M)) %(at26_full_original_matrix_decoder)%
then
  forall s : RH26Complex; t : MT26_R
  . at26RHScalar(at26ComplexToRH(t))=t %(at26_complex_presentation_two_sided_return)% %implied
  . at26DecodeOriginalZero(at26EncodeOriginalZero(s))=s
      %(at26_every_original_zero_matrix_return)% %implied
  . MT26_dagger(at26EncodeOriginalZero(s))=at26EncodeOriginalZero(rh26Reflect(s))
      %(at26_full_original_reflection_matrix_equivariance)% %implied
  . MT26_dagger(at26EncodeOriginalZero(s))=at26EncodeOriginalZero(s) <=> rh26OnLine(s)
      %(at26_full_original_carrier_matrix_fixed_line)% %implied
  . rh26RH <=> (forall z : RH26Complex . rh26NontrivialZetaZero(z) =>
      MT26_dagger(at26EncodeOriginalZero(z))=at26EncodeOriginalZero(z))
      %(at26_full_RH_faithful_matrix_equivalence)% %implied
  . rh26GRHAll <=> (forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter; z : RH26Complex .
      rh26Strip(z) /\ rh26L(c,z)=rh26C0 =>
      MT26_dagger(at26EncodeOriginalZero(z))=at26EncodeOriginalZero(z))
      %(at26_full_Dirichlet_GRH_faithful_matrix_equivalence)% %implied
end
%% Injectivity returns the other composite; DE4=id returns each original
%% zero, then the proved line equivalence is substituted under the original
%% RH/GRH quantifiers. Every character and every original point are retained.

%% Keep the complete source in the fibre of the four-bit controller.
%% Chart translation changes the frame and retains the SAME faithful matrix.
spec AT26_ZeroPackets = AT26_OriginalComplexPresentation and H24_MatrixPackets then
  ops at26ZeroPacket : TR26Point * MT26_R -> H24Packet;
      at26ReadZero : H24Packet -> MT26_R;
      at26OriginalZeroPacket : TR26Point * RH26Complex -> H24Packet
  forall p : TR26Point; s : MT26_R; z : RH26Complex; u : H24Packet
  . at26ZeroPacket(p,s)=h24Packet(p,MBR_E4(s)) %(at26_zero_packet_definition)%
  . at26ReadZero(u)=MBR_decode(h24PacketPayload(u)) %(at26_original_zero_decoder)%
  . at26OriginalZeroPacket(p,z)=h24Packet(p,at26EncodeOriginalZero(z))
      %(at26_full_original_zero_packet_definition)%
then
  forall p,q : TR26Point; chi : MT26_Chi; s : MT26_R; u : H24Packet
  . at26ReadZero(at26ZeroPacket(p,s))=s %(at26_packet_returns_original_zero)% %implied
  . h24PacketTransition(p,q,at26ZeroPacket(p,s))=at26ZeroPacket(q,s)
      %(at26_same_zero_chart_translation)% %implied
  . at26ReadZero(h24PacketTransition(p,q,u))=at26ReadZero(u)
      %(at26_chart_translation_retains_original_zero)% %implied
  . h24LiftMove(at26ZeroPacket(p,s))=at26ZeroPacket(p,s) <=> rh26OnLine(at26ComplexToRH(s))
      %(at26_torser_packet_dagger_returns_original_line)% %implied
  . rh26GRHPrimitive /\ rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) /\ MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_z =>
      h24LiftMove(h24PacketTransition(p,q,at26ZeroPacket(p,s)))=at26ZeroPacket(q,s)
      %(at26_completed_zero_all_torser_charts_return)% %implied
  forall p,q : TR26Point; z : RH26Complex
  . at26DecodeOriginalZero(h24PacketPayload(at26OriginalZeroPacket(p,z)))=z
      %(at26_full_original_zero_packet_return)% %implied
  . h24PacketTransition(p,q,at26OriginalZeroPacket(p,z))=at26OriginalZeroPacket(q,z)
      %(at26_full_original_zero_packet_chart_return)% %implied
  . h24LiftMove(at26OriginalZeroPacket(p,z))=at26OriginalZeroPacket(p,z) <=> rh26OnLine(z)
      %(at26_full_original_zero_packet_line_return)% %implied
  . rh26GRHAll <=> (forall c : GC26DirichletCharacter; z : RH26Complex; p : TR26Point .
      rh26Strip(z) /\ rh26L(c,z)=rh26C0 =>
      h24LiftMove(at26OriginalZeroPacket(p,z))=at26OriginalZeroPacket(p,z))
      %(at26_full_Dirichlet_GRH_all_torser_charts_equivalence)% %implied
end

%% The independent base contains all requested foundations and conditional
%% returns. Its imports do not include either unqualified GRH branch below.
spec AT26_NumberTheoryAtlas =
  GC26_InfiniteGaloisSource and GC26_CompatibleAutomorphismFamilies and
  GC26_UnitClassificationSource and GC26_FiniteCharacterFourierSource and GC26_PresentedDirichletConnection and DA26_AllDirichletApplications and
  MX26_SOURCE_HolomorphicThetaTheorems and MX26_SOURCE_PositiveAxisInverse and
  MT26_SOURCE_LambdaGaloisInterface and MT26_CharacterThetaReturn and
  RH26_IMPORTED_ROBIN_LAGARIAS and AT26_DivisorCoefficientReturn and
  AT26_ZeroPackets and MBR26_TorserFrameReturns and
  TR26CollatzWordReturns and TR26WordInductionReturns and TR26ParityCodingReturns and
  TR26FibreReturns and TR26BaseChangeReturns and TR26InstitutionReturns and
  H24_TorserCharts and HC24_CalibratedProofPackets
end
spec AT26_WithGRHPremise = AT26_NumberTheoryAtlas and RH26_GRH_PREMISE_BRANCH then
  . rh26RH /\ rh26RobinAll /\ rh26LagariasAll
      %(at26_named_GRH_premise_arithmetic_bundle)% %implied
end

spec AT26_WithExternal2026DirichletTheorem =
  AT26_NumberTheoryAtlas and RH26_EXTERNAL_2026_DIRICHLET_GRH then
  . rh26GRHAll /\ rh26RH /\ rh26RobinAll /\ rh26LagariasAll
      %(at26_external_Dirichlet_arithmetic_bundle)% %implied
  forall p,q : TR26Point; chi : MT26_Chi; s : MT26_R
  . rh26Strip(at26ComplexToRH(s)) /\ MT26_Lambda(chi,s)=MT26_z =>
      h24LiftMove(h24PacketTransition(p,q,at26ZeroPacket(p,s)))=at26ZeroPacket(q,s)
      %(at26_external_completed_zero_original_packet_return)% %implied
end

%% ACTUAL CASL VIEWS: PORTABLE LAWS AT THEIR CONCRETE SOURCE THEORIES.
%% These are signature morphisms with sentence-preservation obligations.
%% Static analysis checks their typing; target return proofs supply the
%% indicated mathematics. No equality between unrelated carriers is asserted.

view H24GaloisInverseLimitRetraction : H24_Retraction to GC26_CompatibleAutomorphismFamilies =
  H24Source |-> GC26GlobalAuto, H24Target |-> GC26Family,
  h24Encode |-> GC26encode, h24Decode |-> GC26decode
end
%% The source section is the coordinate-wise reconstructed automorphism
%% return, GC26_inverse_limit_global_return, not a chosen point in a fibre.

view H24CollatzParityRetraction : H24_Retraction to TR26ParityCoding =
  H24Source |-> TR26Z2, H24Target |-> TR26Stream,
  h24Encode |-> tr26ParityCode, h24Decode |-> tr26ParityDecode
end
%% Only the supplied original-point section is used; no natural-number
%% Collatz termination statement is introduced by this interpretation.

view H24GroupAlgebraRetraction : H24_Retraction to MT26_GroupAlgebraReturn =
  H24Source |-> MT26_V6, H24Target |-> MT26_M4,
  h24Encode |-> MT26_encode6, h24Decode |-> MT26_decode6
end
%% The specified inverse of 3 reconstructs all six original coefficients.
%% This view does not claim that the image contains every four-by-four matrix.

view H24InvolutionMatrixEquivariance : H24_EquivariantReturn to MBR26_InvolutionEncodingReturns =
  H24Source |-> MT26_R, H24Target |-> MT26_M4,
  h24Encode |-> MBR_E4, h24Decode |-> MBR_decode,
  h24SourceMove |-> MBR_reflect, h24TargetMove |-> MT26_dagger
end
%% The two obligations are mbr26_exact_original_scalar_return and
%% mbr26_marked_matrix_dagger_equivariance; their concrete encoding
%% identifies fixed points without dropping the original scalar.

view H24DirichletTaggedDagger : H24_TaggedInvolution to GC26_DirichletValues =
  H24Object |-> GC26DirichletCharacter, H24Index |-> GC26Integer,
  H24Tag |-> GC26Nat, H24Value |-> GC26Scalar,
  h24Tag |-> GC26modulus, h24Evaluate |-> GC26evaluate,
  h24ObjectDagger |-> GC26conjugateCharacter, h24ValueDagger |-> GC26conjugate
end
%% Modulus-tagged extensionality is essential: zero extensions at distinct
%% declared moduli are not identified by a change of character presentation.

view H24FibreSigmaPullAdjunction : H24_PreorderAdjunction to TR26FibreReturns =
  H24A |-> TR26PredX, H24B |-> TR26PredY,
  h24Left |-> tr26Sigma, h24Right |-> tr26Pull,
  h24BelowA |-> tr26BelowX, h24BelowB |-> tr26BelowY
end

view H24FibrePullPiAdjunction : H24_PreorderAdjunction to TR26FibreReturns =
  H24A |-> TR26PredY, H24B |-> TR26PredX,
  h24Left |-> tr26Pull, h24Right |-> tr26Pi,
  h24BelowA |-> tr26BelowY, h24BelowB |-> tr26BelowX
end
%% Pointwise implication supplies reflexivity/transitivity. Existential
%% witnesses and universal evaluation prove the two concrete adjunctions.
%% These views require no predicate extensionality or antisymmetry.

view H24GaloisOppositePosetAdjunction : H24_PosetAdjunction to GC26_GaloisConnection =
  H24A |-> GC26Subgroup, H24B |-> GC26Subfield,
  h24Left |-> GC26fixed, h24Right |-> GC26stabilizer,
  h24BelowA |-> GC26groupIncluded, h24BelowB |-> GC26fieldOppositeIncluded
end
%% fieldOppositeIncluded(m,n) means fieldIncluded(n,m). The generic law is
%% therefore exactly h<=stabilizer(m) iff m<=fixed(h). Set extensionality
%% supplies antisymmetry; no infinite-Galois closure theorem is imported.

view H24AnalyticMultiplicativeZeroMonoid : H24_ZeroMonoid to MT26_CharacteristicZeroField =
  H24M |-> MT26_R, h24M0 |-> MT26_z, h24M1 |-> MT26_o,
  h24Times |-> MT26_mul
end
%% Associativity, the left unit, and multiplication by zero are ring
%% consequences. The transported zero-reflection theorem still requests
%% only a specified left inverse; it does not assert that every factor is a unit.

%% CANONICITY WITH THE ORIGINAL MARKS, AND SPLIT-OBSERVATION CALCULUS.
%% Recovered origins: Tesseract_Metatheorems.md, sections Retained comparison,
%% Split observations, Normal forms from generators; native concordance
%% SourceReturnFusion/HTExactChartReturns; Torser_Canonical_Readings.het and
%% Torser_Canonical_Marks.het. Exact archive/version locators are in the notes.
%% Every generic conclusion below uses only the displayed section equation.
spec CN26_Normalization = H24_Retraction with
  H24Source |-> CN26Source, H24Target |-> CN26Code,
  h24Encode |-> CN26encode, h24Decode |-> CN26decode
then
  op CN26normalize : CN26Code -> CN26Code
  pred CN26canonical : CN26Code
  forall u : CN26Code
  . CN26normalize(u)=CN26encode(CN26decode(u)) %(cn26_normalize_definition)%
  . CN26canonical(u) <=> CN26normalize(u)=u %(cn26_canonical_definition)%
then
  forall u,v : CN26Code
  . CN26normalize(CN26normalize(u))=CN26normalize(u) %(cn26_projector_idempotent)% %implied
  . CN26decode(CN26normalize(u))=CN26decode(u) %(cn26_normalization_preserves_read)% %implied
  . CN26canonical(CN26normalize(u)) %(cn26_normalization_is_canonical)% %implied
  . CN26canonical(u) <=> (exists x : CN26Source . CN26encode(x)=u)
      %(cn26_canonical_exactly_image)% %implied
  . CN26normalize(u)=CN26normalize(v) <=> CN26decode(u)=CN26decode(v)
      %(cn26_normal_forms_exactly_same_read)% %implied
  . (CN26canonical(v) /\ CN26decode(v)=CN26decode(u)) <=> v=CN26normalize(u)
      %(cn26_unique_canonical_representative)% %implied
end
%% Proof: DED=D and EDED=ED by DE=id. A fixed u is E(Du); an E(x)
%% is fixed by DE=id. Applying D to equal normal forms gives equal reads;
%% equal reads give equal normal forms by applying E. If v is fixed and
%% Dv=Du, then v=EDv=EDu. Conversely v=EDu is fixed and reads as Du.

spec CN26_SplitObservations = CN26_Normalization then
  sort CN26Operation
  ops CN26run : CN26Operation * CN26Code -> CN26Code;
      CN26coarse,CN26proposed : CN26Operation * CN26Source -> CN26Source
  preds CN26descends,CN26lifts,CN26fibreConstant,CN26imageStable,CN26commutes : CN26Operation
  forall t : CN26Operation; y : CN26Source
  . CN26coarse(t,y)=CN26decode(CN26run(t,CN26encode(y))) %(cn26_coarse_definition)%
  . CN26descends(t) <=> (forall u : CN26Code .
      CN26decode(CN26run(t,u))=CN26coarse(t,CN26decode(u))) %(cn26_descent_definition)%
  . CN26lifts(t) <=> (forall y0 : CN26Source .
      CN26run(t,CN26encode(y0))=CN26encode(CN26coarse(t,y0))) %(cn26_lift_definition)%
  . CN26fibreConstant(t) <=> (forall u,v : CN26Code . CN26decode(u)=CN26decode(v) =>
      CN26decode(CN26run(t,u))=CN26decode(CN26run(t,v))) %(cn26_fibre_definition)%
  . CN26imageStable(t) <=> (forall u : CN26Code .
      CN26canonical(u) => CN26canonical(CN26run(t,u))) %(cn26_image_stability_definition)%
  . CN26commutes(t) <=> (forall u : CN26Code .
      CN26normalize(CN26run(t,u))=CN26run(t,CN26normalize(u))) %(cn26_commutation_definition)%
then
  forall s,t : CN26Operation; y : CN26Source
  . CN26descends(t) <=> CN26fibreConstant(t) %(cn26_descent_iff_fibre_constant)% %implied
  . CN26lifts(t) <=> CN26imageStable(t) %(cn26_lift_iff_image_stable)% %implied
  . (CN26descends(t) /\ CN26lifts(t)) <=> CN26commutes(t)
      %(cn26_both_squares_iff_commutation)% %implied
  . (forall u : CN26Code . CN26decode(CN26run(t,u))=CN26proposed(t,CN26decode(u))) =>
      CN26proposed(t,y)=CN26coarse(t,y) %(cn26_unique_descended_operation)% %implied
  . (forall x : CN26Source . CN26run(t,CN26encode(x))=CN26encode(CN26proposed(t,x))) =>
      CN26proposed(t,y)=CN26coarse(t,y) %(cn26_unique_lifted_operation)% %implied
  . CN26descends(s) \/ CN26lifts(t) =>
      CN26decode(CN26run(s,CN26run(t,CN26encode(y))))=CN26coarse(s,CN26coarse(t,y))
      %(cn26_one_sided_exact_composition)% %implied
end
%% Proof: u and Pu have the same read, so fibre constancy gives DT=DTP;
%% conversely DT=bar(T)D makes DT constant on each D-fibre. Image stability
%% applied to Ey gives TE=PTE=Ebar(T); the converse writes a fixed u=EDu.
%% The two squares give PT=Ebar(T)D=TP. From PT=TP apply D to obtain
%% DT=DTP, and evaluate at Ey to obtain PTE=TE. Uniqueness follows by
%% evaluating the descent square at Ey, or applying D to the lift square.
%% Finally DSTE=DSPTE if S descends (DS=DSP), or if T lifts (TE=PTE).
%% No linearity, inverse of E, arbitrary target-image equality, or choice.

spec CN26_ChartCanonicity = H24_TorserCharts then
  sort CN26Action
  ops CN26sourceAction : CN26Action * H24Payload -> H24Payload;
      CN26chartNormalize : TR26Point * H24ChartValue -> H24ChartValue;
      CN26chartLift : CN26Action * TR26Point * H24ChartValue -> H24ChartValue
  forall a : CN26Action; p : TR26Point; u : H24ChartValue
  . CN26chartNormalize(p,u)=h24ChartEncode(p,h24ChartDecode(p,u)) %(cn26_chart_normalize_definition)%
  . CN26chartLift(a,p,u)=h24ChartEncode(p,CN26sourceAction(a,h24ChartDecode(p,u)))
      %(cn26_chart_lift_definition)%
then
  forall a : CN26Action; p,q : TR26Point; u,v : H24ChartValue
  . h24Transition(q,p,h24Transition(p,q,u))=CN26chartNormalize(p,u)
      %(cn26_raw_chart_roundtrip_is_normalization)% %implied
  . h24InChart(q,v) /\ h24ChartDecode(q,v)=h24ChartDecode(p,u) => v=h24Transition(p,q,u)
      %(cn26_unique_marked_chart_comparison)% %implied
  . CN26chartLift(a,q,h24Transition(p,q,u))=h24Transition(p,q,CN26chartLift(a,p,u))
      %(cn26_operation_naturality)% %implied
  . h24ChartDecode(p,CN26chartLift(a,p,u))=CN26sourceAction(a,h24ChartDecode(p,u))
      %(cn26_operation_original_source_return)% %implied
end
%% Proof: expand T_pq=E_q D_p and cancel each adjacent D_q E_q. For
%% uniqueness, v=E_q D_q v=E_q D_p u. Both naturality sides become
%% E_q f D_p u. The image guard belongs to v, not arbitrary raw u.
%% Root's shared H24_Words/MG26 fold reuses this atomic square by induction;
%% no extra word datatype or independent composition axiom is introduced here.

spec CN26_CoefficientNormalization = MT26_GroupAlgebraReturn then
  op CN26normalizeCoefficients : MT26_M4 -> MT26_M4
  pred CN26coefficientCanonical : MT26_M4
  forall M : MT26_M4
  . CN26normalizeCoefficients(M)=MT26_encode6(MT26_decode6(M)) %(cn26_coefficient_normalize_definition)%
  . CN26coefficientCanonical(M) <=> CN26normalizeCoefficients(M)=M %(cn26_coefficient_image_definition)%
end
view CN26SixCoefficientCanonicity : CN26_Normalization to CN26_CoefficientNormalization =
  CN26Source |-> MT26_V6, CN26Code |-> MT26_M4,
  CN26encode |-> MT26_encode6, CN26decode |-> MT26_decode6,
  CN26normalize |-> CN26normalizeCoefficients, CN26canonical |-> CN26coefficientCanonical
end
%% This view's section obligation is MT26_algebra_coefficients_return,
%% proved from the actual six-coordinate formulas over a ring with 3 a unit.
%% It transports every normal-form theorem; it does not identify all M4
%% matrices with the six-dimensional block image or invert 2.

spec CN26_OriginalZeroNormalization = AT26_OriginalComplexPresentation then
  free type CN26MarkedZero ::= CN26zero(CN26zeroCharacter:GC26DirichletCharacter;CN26zeroPoint:RH26Complex)
  free type CN26ZeroCode ::= CN26zeroCode(CN26codeCharacter:GC26DirichletCharacter;CN26codeMatrix:MT26_M4)
  ops CN26encodeZero : CN26MarkedZero -> CN26ZeroCode;
      CN26decodeZero : CN26ZeroCode -> CN26MarkedZero;
      CN26normalizeZero : CN26ZeroCode -> CN26ZeroCode
  preds CN26zeroCanonical,CN26isOriginalLZero : CN26ZeroCode
  forall z : CN26MarkedZero; u : CN26ZeroCode
  . CN26zero(CN26zeroCharacter(z),CN26zeroPoint(z))=z %(cn26_marked_source_eta)% %implied
  . CN26zeroCode(CN26codeCharacter(u),CN26codeMatrix(u))=u %(cn26_marked_code_eta)% %implied
  . CN26encodeZero(z)=CN26zeroCode(CN26zeroCharacter(z),at26EncodeOriginalZero(CN26zeroPoint(z)))
      %(cn26_same_character_matrix_encoding)%
  . CN26decodeZero(u)=CN26zero(CN26codeCharacter(u),at26DecodeOriginalZero(CN26codeMatrix(u)))
      %(cn26_same_character_original_decoder)%
  . CN26normalizeZero(u)=CN26encodeZero(CN26decodeZero(u)) %(cn26_marked_zero_normalization)%
  . CN26zeroCanonical(u) <=> CN26normalizeZero(u)=u %(cn26_marked_zero_image)%
  . CN26isOriginalLZero(u) <=> rh26Strip(at26DecodeOriginalZero(CN26codeMatrix(u))) /\
      rh26L(CN26codeCharacter(u),at26DecodeOriginalZero(CN26codeMatrix(u)))=rh26C0
      %(cn26_same_character_original_zero_definition)%
then
  forall z : CN26MarkedZero; u : CN26ZeroCode
  . CN26decodeZero(CN26encodeZero(z))=z %(cn26_full_marked_zero_return)% %implied
  . CN26codeCharacter(CN26normalizeZero(u))=CN26codeCharacter(u)
      %(cn26_normalization_retains_character)% %implied
  . CN26isOriginalLZero(CN26normalizeZero(u)) <=> CN26isOriginalLZero(u)
      %(cn26_normalization_retains_original_L_zero)% %implied
end
view CN26SameSourceZeroCanonicity : CN26_Normalization to CN26_OriginalZeroNormalization =
  CN26Source |-> CN26MarkedZero, CN26Code |-> CN26ZeroCode,
  CN26encode |-> CN26encodeZero, CN26decode |-> CN26decodeZero,
  CN26normalize |-> CN26normalizeZero, CN26canonical |-> CN26zeroCanonical
end
%% Proof: free generation writes every pair as its constructor; selector
%% equations then prove both eta goals. Projections return the same character;
%% at26_every_original_zero_matrix_return returns the same original complex
%% point. The pair eta equation returns the whole marked source. Normalizing
%% therefore preserves both arguments of L exactly. No assertion that a zero
%% lies on the critical line, or that distinct character labels coincide.

spec CN26_StabilizerObstruction =
  sorts CN26Base,CN26Mark
  ops CN26baseMove : CN26Base -> CN26Base; CN26markMove : CN26Mark -> CN26Mark;
      CN26choose : CN26Base -> CN26Mark
  pred CN26incident : CN26Base * CN26Mark
then
  forall b : CN26Base
  . CN26baseMove(b)=b /\ (forall m : CN26Mark . CN26incident(b,m) => not CN26markMove(m)=m) =>
      not (CN26incident(b,CN26choose(b)) /\ CN26choose(CN26baseMove(b))=CN26markMove(CN26choose(b)))
      %(cn26_stabilizer_obstructs_equivariant_selection)% %implied
end
%% Proof: at the fixed base equivariance says choose(b)=markMove(choose(b));
%% incidence contradicts the stated no-fixed-mark premise. Native archive
%% MarkActionLaws.no_fixed_choice_of_first_endpoint supplies that premise
%% for each nonzero d with base transvect(d,-), mark moveNullMark(d,-).
%% EndpointChoiceTest derives the global obstruction. Our chart theorems
%% retain their frame/character marks and do not postulate such a selector.

spec CN26_CanonicityReturns = CN26_SplitObservations and CN26_ChartCanonicity and
  CN26_CoefficientNormalization and CN26_OriginalZeroNormalization and CN26_StabilizerObstruction
end

%% TROPICAL RETURNS: definitions, finite witnesses, valuation, actual charts.
%% Recovered FOUR construction: CONSOLIDATED_PROOFS.md section 3 and
%% Hexaholographic_Full_Goals_RH_GRH.het, HTTropicalSupport/Returns.
%% Primary references: Maclagan, Definitions 1.1/1.3/2.2, sections 1--2,
%% https://arxiv.org/pdf/1207.1925 ; Sturmfels, section 2 and Theorem 2.10,
%% https://math.berkeley.edu/~bernd/tropical/sec2.pdf .
%% No arbitrary optimizer, source cancellation law, or polynomial-time claim.
spec TP26_IdempotentSemiring =
  sort TP26Weight
  ops tp26Infinity,tp26Zero : TP26Weight;
      tp26Choice,tp26Compose : TP26Weight * TP26Weight -> TP26Weight
  pred tp26Le : TP26Weight * TP26Weight
  forall a,b,c : TP26Weight
  . tp26Choice(a,b)=tp26Choice(b,a) %(tp26_choice_commutative)%
  . tp26Choice(tp26Choice(a,b),c)=tp26Choice(a,tp26Choice(b,c)) %(tp26_choice_associative)%
  . tp26Choice(a,a)=a %(tp26_choice_idempotent)%
  . tp26Choice(tp26Infinity,a)=a %(tp26_choice_identity)%
  . tp26Compose(a,b)=tp26Compose(b,a) %(tp26_composition_commutative)%
  . tp26Compose(tp26Compose(a,b),c)=tp26Compose(a,tp26Compose(b,c)) %(tp26_composition_associative)%
  . tp26Compose(tp26Zero,a)=a %(tp26_composition_identity)%
  . tp26Compose(tp26Infinity,a)=tp26Infinity %(tp26_unavailable_absorbs)%
  . tp26Compose(a,tp26Choice(b,c))=tp26Choice(tp26Compose(a,b),tp26Compose(a,c)) %(tp26_distributive)%
  . tp26Le(a,b) <=> tp26Choice(a,b)=a %(tp26_order_definition)%
then
  forall a,b,c,d : TP26Weight
  . tp26Choice(a,tp26Choice(a,b))=tp26Choice(a,b) %(tp26_duplicate_branch_absorption)% %implied
  . tp26Le(a,a) /\ (tp26Le(a,b) /\ tp26Le(b,a) => a=b) %(tp26_order_reflexive_antisymmetric)% %implied
  . tp26Le(a,b) /\ tp26Le(b,c) => tp26Le(a,c) %(tp26_order_transitive)% %implied
  . tp26Le(a,b) => tp26Le(tp26Compose(a,c),tp26Compose(b,c)) %(tp26_composition_monotone)% %implied
  . tp26Choice(tp26Compose(a,c),tp26Choice(tp26Compose(a,d),tp26Compose(b,tp26Choice(c,d))))=
      tp26Compose(tp26Choice(a,b),tp26Choice(c,d)) %(tp26_common_factor_return)% %implied
end
spec TP26_Powers = TP26_IdempotentSemiring and H24_Naturals then
  op tp26Power : TP26Weight * GC26Nat -> TP26Weight
  forall a : TP26Weight; n : GC26Nat
  . tp26Power(a,GC26zero)=tp26Zero %(tp26_power_base)%
  . tp26Power(a,GC26succ(n))=tp26Compose(a,tp26Power(a,n)) %(tp26_power_step)%
then
  forall a,b : TP26Weight; m,n : GC26Nat
  . tp26Power(a,GC26nadd(m,n))=tp26Compose(tp26Power(a,m),tp26Power(a,n)) %(tp26_exponent_addition)% %implied
  . tp26Power(tp26Compose(a,b),n)=tp26Compose(tp26Power(a,n),tp26Power(b,n)) %(tp26_product_power)% %implied
end
%% Bipotence is separate: arbitrary idempotent semirings need not be total.
spec TP26_TotalOrder = TP26_IdempotentSemiring then
  pred tp26Less : TP26Weight * TP26Weight
  forall a,b : TP26Weight
  . tp26Choice(a,b)=a \/ tp26Choice(a,b)=b %(tp26_total_minimum)%
  . tp26Less(a,b) <=> tp26Le(a,b) /\ not a=b %(tp26_strict_order)%
  . not tp26Infinity=tp26Zero %(tp26_distinct_tropical_units)%
end
spec TP26_TotalTropical = TP26_TotalOrder and TP26_Powers then
  forall a,b : TP26Weight; n : GC26Nat
  . tp26Power(tp26Choice(a,b),n)=tp26Choice(tp26Power(a,n),tp26Power(b,n)) %(tp26_tropical_power_choice)% %implied
end
%% Concrete nonnegative work costs; availability is constructed, not assumed.
spec TP26_NaturalCosts = H24_Naturals and TR26BitCarrier then
  free type TP26Cost ::= tp26Unavailable | tp26Finite(GC26Nat)
  ops tp26NaturalMin : GC26Nat * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat;
      tp26CostChoice,tp26CostCompose : TP26Cost * TP26Cost -> TP26Cost;
      tp26Available : TP26Cost -> TR26Bit;
      tp26Or,tp26And : TR26Bit * TR26Bit -> TR26Bit
  forall m,n : GC26Nat; c : TP26Cost; b : TR26Bit
  . tp26NaturalMin(GC26zero,n)=GC26zero %(tp26_natural_min_zero)%
  . tp26NaturalMin(GC26succ(m),GC26zero)=GC26zero %(tp26_natural_min_right_zero)%
  . tp26NaturalMin(GC26succ(m),GC26succ(n))=GC26succ(tp26NaturalMin(m,n)) %(tp26_natural_min_step)%
  . tp26CostChoice(tp26Unavailable,c)=c /\ tp26CostChoice(c,tp26Unavailable)=c %(tp26_cost_choice_unavailable)%
  . tp26CostChoice(tp26Finite(m),tp26Finite(n))=tp26Finite(tp26NaturalMin(m,n)) %(tp26_cost_choice_finite)%
  . tp26CostCompose(tp26Unavailable,c)=tp26Unavailable /\ tp26CostCompose(c,tp26Unavailable)=tp26Unavailable %(tp26_cost_compose_unavailable)%
  . tp26CostCompose(tp26Finite(m),tp26Finite(n))=tp26Finite(GC26nadd(m,n)) %(tp26_cost_compose_finite)%
  . tp26Available(tp26Unavailable)=tr26Off /\ tp26Available(tp26Finite(n))=tr26On %(tp26_availability_definition)%
  . tp26Or(tr26Off,b)=b /\ tp26Or(tr26On,b)=tr26On %(tp26_boolean_or)%
  . tp26And(tr26Off,b)=tr26Off /\ tp26And(tr26On,b)=b %(tp26_boolean_and)%
  . c=tp26Unavailable \/ (exists k : GC26Nat . c=tp26Finite(k)) %(tp26_explicit_free_cost_coverage)%
then
  forall a,b : TP26Cost
  . tp26Available(tp26CostChoice(a,b))=tp26Or(tp26Available(a),tp26Available(b)) %(tp26_availability_preserves_choice)% %implied
  . tp26Available(tp26CostCompose(a,b))=tp26And(tp26Available(a),tp26Available(b)) %(tp26_availability_preserves_composition)% %implied
end
spec TP26_NaturalCostOrder = TP26_NaturalCosts then
  op tp26CostZero : TP26Cost
  pred tp26CostLe : TP26Cost * TP26Cost
  . tp26CostZero=tp26Finite(GC26zero) %(tp26_natural_cost_identity)%
  forall a,b : TP26Cost
  . tp26CostLe(a,b) <=> tp26CostChoice(a,b)=a %(tp26_natural_cost_order)%
end
view TP26NaturalCostSemiring : TP26_IdempotentSemiring to TP26_NaturalCostOrder =
  TP26Weight |-> TP26Cost, tp26Infinity |-> tp26Unavailable, tp26Zero |-> tp26CostZero,
  tp26Choice |-> tp26CostChoice, tp26Compose |-> tp26CostCompose, tp26Le |-> tp26CostLe
end
spec TP26_FourSupport = TP26_NaturalCosts and TR26PointCarrier then
  free type TP26Four ::= tp26Four(tp26Pos:TR26Bit;tp26Neg:TR26Bit)
  free type TP26SignedCost ::= tp26Costs(tp26PositiveCost:TP26Cost;tp26NegativeCost:TP26Cost)
  ops tp26NegateFour : TP26Four -> TP26Four;
      tp26JoinFour,tp26ComposeFour : TP26Four * TP26Four -> TP26Four;
      tp26Switch : TP26SignedCost -> TP26SignedCost;
      tp26SignedChoice,tp26SignedCompose : TP26SignedCost * TP26SignedCost -> TP26SignedCost;
      tp26Evidence : TP26SignedCost -> TP26Four;
      tp26Controller : TP26Four * TP26Four -> TR26Point;
      tp26FirstFour,tp26SecondFour : TR26Point -> TP26Four
  forall a,b,c,d : TP26Cost; i,j,k,l : TR26Bit; x : TP26Four; s : TP26SignedCost; p : TR26Point
  . tp26Four(tp26Pos(x),tp26Neg(x))=x /\ tp26Costs(tp26PositiveCost(s),tp26NegativeCost(s))=s %(tp26_product_eta)%
  . tp26NegateFour(tp26Four(i,j))=tp26Four(j,i) %(tp26_four_negation)%
  . tp26JoinFour(tp26Four(i,j),tp26Four(k,l))=tp26Four(tp26Or(i,k),tp26Or(j,l)) %(tp26_four_knowledge_join)%
  . tp26ComposeFour(tp26Four(i,j),tp26Four(k,l))=tp26Four(tp26Or(tp26And(i,k),tp26And(j,l)),tp26Or(tp26And(i,l),tp26And(j,k))) %(tp26_four_signed_product)%
  . tp26Switch(tp26Costs(a,b))=tp26Costs(b,a) %(tp26_signed_cost_switch)%
  . tp26SignedChoice(tp26Costs(a,b),tp26Costs(c,d))=tp26Costs(tp26CostChoice(a,c),tp26CostChoice(b,d)) %(tp26_signed_cost_choice)%
  . tp26SignedCompose(tp26Costs(a,b),tp26Costs(c,d))=tp26Costs(tp26CostChoice(tp26CostCompose(a,c),tp26CostCompose(b,d)),tp26CostChoice(tp26CostCompose(a,d),tp26CostCompose(b,c))) %(tp26_signed_cost_composition)%
  . tp26Evidence(tp26Costs(a,b))=tp26Four(tp26Available(a),tp26Available(b)) %(tp26_cost_evidence)%
  . tp26Controller(x,tp26Four(i,j))=tr26Point(tp26Pos(x),tp26Neg(x),i,j) %(tp26_four_pair_controller)%
  . tp26FirstFour(p)=tp26Four(tr26Input(p),tr26Carry(p)) /\ tp26SecondFour(p)=tp26Four(tr26N(p),tr26B(p)) %(tp26_controller_four_readings)%
then
  forall s,t : TP26SignedCost; x,y : TP26Four; p : TR26Point
  . tp26Evidence(tp26Switch(s))=tp26NegateFour(tp26Evidence(s)) %(tp26_tropical_negation_returns_FOUR)% %implied
  . tp26Evidence(tp26SignedChoice(s,t))=tp26JoinFour(tp26Evidence(s),tp26Evidence(t)) %(tp26_tropical_choice_returns_FOUR)% %implied
  . tp26Evidence(tp26SignedCompose(s,t))=tp26ComposeFour(tp26Evidence(s),tp26Evidence(t)) %(tp26_tropical_composition_returns_FOUR)% %implied
  . tp26FirstFour(tp26Controller(x,y))=x /\ tp26SecondFour(tp26Controller(x,y))=y %(tp26_two_FOUR_controller_source_return)% %implied
  . tp26Controller(tp26FirstFour(p),tp26SecondFour(p))=p %(tp26_actual_torser_controller_return)% %implied
end
%% The controller retains FOUR x FOUR, not unbounded costs or proof payloads.
%% Signed composition is Boolean C2 convolution; it is not truth-meet or XOR.
spec TP26_FiniteCandidates = TP26_TotalOrder and TR26BitCarrier then
  sort TP26Candidate
  free type TP26Candidates ::= tp26Single(TP26Candidate) | tp26More(TP26Candidate;TP26Candidates)
  ops tp26DecideLe : TP26Weight * TP26Weight -> TR26Bit;
      tp26CandidateCost : TP26Candidate -> TP26Weight;
      tp26CandidateMinimum : TP26Candidates -> TP26Weight;
      tp26Best : TP26Candidates -> TP26Candidate
  pred tp26Occurs : TP26Candidate * TP26Candidates
  forall a,b : TP26Weight
  . tp26DecideLe(a,b)=tr26On <=> tp26Le(a,b) %(tp26_supplied_comparison_on)%
  . tp26DecideLe(a,b)=tr26Off <=> not tp26Le(a,b) %(tp26_supplied_comparison_off)%
  forall c,d : TP26Candidate; cs : TP26Candidates
  . tp26Occurs(c,tp26Single(d)) <=> c=d %(tp26_single_membership)%
  . tp26Occurs(c,tp26More(d,cs)) <=> c=d \/ tp26Occurs(c,cs) %(tp26_more_membership)%
  . tp26CandidateMinimum(tp26Single(c))=tp26CandidateCost(c) /\ tp26Best(tp26Single(c))=c %(tp26_single_candidate)%
  . tp26CandidateMinimum(tp26More(c,cs))=tp26Choice(tp26CandidateCost(c),tp26CandidateMinimum(cs)) %(tp26_candidate_minimum_fold)%
  . tp26DecideLe(tp26CandidateCost(c),tp26CandidateCost(tp26Best(cs)))=tr26On => tp26Best(tp26More(c,cs))=c %(tp26_supplied_finite_choice_left)%
  . tp26DecideLe(tp26CandidateCost(c),tp26CandidateCost(tp26Best(cs)))=tr26Off => tp26Best(tp26More(c,cs))=tp26Best(cs) %(tp26_supplied_finite_choice_right)%
then
  forall c : TP26Candidate; cs : TP26Candidates
  . tp26Occurs(tp26Best(cs),cs) /\ tp26CandidateCost(tp26Best(cs))=tp26CandidateMinimum(cs) %(tp26_finite_selected_witness_return)% %implied
  . tp26Occurs(c,cs) => tp26Le(tp26CandidateCost(tp26Best(cs)),tp26CandidateCost(c)) %(tp26_finite_catalogue_minimum)% %implied
end
%% Valuation values may be negative: TP26Weight is NOT identified with Cost.
%% These are valuation/source-interface laws, weaker than field axioms.
spec TP26_AdditiveValuation = TP26_TotalOrder and
  { H24_AdditiveGroup with H24Scalar |-> TP26K, h24Zero |-> tp26K0,
      h24Add |-> tp26KAdd, h24Neg |-> tp26KNeg } then
  op tp26Val : TP26K -> TP26Weight
  forall x,y : TP26K
  . tp26Val(x)=tp26Infinity <=> x=tp26K0 %(tp26_valuation_zero_kernel)%
  . tp26Val(tp26KNeg(x))=tp26Val(x) %(tp26_valuation_negation)%
  . tp26Le(tp26Choice(tp26Val(x),tp26Val(y)),tp26Val(tp26KAdd(x,y))) %(tp26_valuation_triangle)%
then
  forall x,y : TP26K
  . not tp26Val(x)=tp26Val(y) => tp26Val(tp26KAdd(x,y))=tp26Choice(tp26Val(x),tp26Val(y)) %(tp26_unequal_valuations_exact_minimum)% %implied
  . not x=tp26K0 => tp26Val(tp26KAdd(x,tp26KNeg(x)))=tp26Infinity /\ not tp26Val(x)=tp26Infinity %(tp26_tie_cancellation_counterexample)% %implied
end
spec TP26_Valuation = TP26_AdditiveValuation and TP26_Powers and
  { H24_Ring with H24Scalar |-> TP26K, h24Zero |-> tp26K0, h24One |-> tp26K1,
      h24Add |-> tp26KAdd, h24Neg |-> tp26KNeg, h24Mul |-> tp26KMul } then
  op tp26KPower : TP26K * GC26Nat -> TP26K
  forall x,y : TP26K; n : GC26Nat
  . tp26Val(tp26K1)=tp26Zero %(tp26_valuation_one)%
  . tp26Val(tp26KMul(x,y))=tp26Compose(tp26Val(x),tp26Val(y)) %(tp26_valuation_multiplicative)%
  . tp26KPower(x,GC26zero)=tp26K1 /\ tp26KPower(x,GC26succ(n))=tp26KMul(x,tp26KPower(x,n)) %(tp26_ring_power_recursion)%
then
  forall x,c : TP26K; n : GC26Nat
  . tp26Val(tp26KMul(c,tp26KPower(x,n)))=tp26Compose(tp26Val(c),tp26Power(tp26Val(x),n)) %(tp26_evaluated_monomial_tropical_weight)% %implied
end
spec TP26_LeadingTermReturn = TP26_AdditiveValuation then
  free type TP26Terms ::= tp26NoTerms | tp26Term(TP26K;TP26Terms)
  ops tp26SumTerms : TP26Terms -> TP26K;
      tp26MinimumTerm : TP26Terms -> TP26Weight
  preds tp26UniqueLowest,tp26Bend : TP26Terms
  forall x : TP26K; ts : TP26Terms
  . tp26SumTerms(tp26NoTerms)=tp26K0 /\ tp26MinimumTerm(tp26NoTerms)=tp26Infinity %(tp26_empty_term_fold)%
  . tp26SumTerms(tp26Term(x,ts))=tp26KAdd(x,tp26SumTerms(ts)) %(tp26_sum_terms_fold)%
  . tp26MinimumTerm(tp26Term(x,ts))=tp26Choice(tp26Val(x),tp26MinimumTerm(ts)) %(tp26_minimum_terms_fold)%
  . not tp26UniqueLowest(tp26NoTerms) %(tp26_empty_no_leader)%
  . tp26UniqueLowest(tp26Term(x,ts)) <=> tp26Less(tp26Val(x),tp26MinimumTerm(ts)) \/
      (tp26Less(tp26MinimumTerm(ts),tp26Val(x)) /\ tp26UniqueLowest(ts)) %(tp26_unique_lowest_fold)%
  . tp26Bend(ts) <=> not tp26MinimumTerm(ts)=tp26Infinity /\ not tp26UniqueLowest(ts) %(tp26_finite_tropical_bend)%
then
  forall ts : TP26Terms
  . tp26Le(tp26MinimumTerm(ts),tp26Val(tp26SumTerms(ts))) %(tp26_finite_sum_valuation_bound)% %implied
  . tp26UniqueLowest(ts) => tp26Val(tp26SumTerms(ts))=tp26MinimumTerm(ts) /\ not tp26SumTerms(ts)=tp26K0 %(tp26_unique_leading_term_cannot_vanish)% %implied
  . tp26SumTerms(ts)=tp26K0 /\ not tp26MinimumTerm(ts)=tp26Infinity => tp26Bend(ts) %(tp26_zero_requires_tropical_hypersurface)% %implied
end
%% Evaluated monomial occurrences form the actual finite list. On the torus,
%% nonzero coefficients give finite weights; zero forces a repeated minimum.
%% This proves necessity only, not a root lift or arbitrary ideal-membership test.
spec TP26_PotentialCharts = HC24_ProofData and H24_FrameInterface then
  op tr26Origin : TR26Point
  ops tp26FramePotential : TR26Point * HC24Judgement -> HC24Charge;
      tp26Reweight : TR26Point * HC24Judgement * HC24Judgement * HC24Charge -> HC24Charge
  forall f : TR26Point; p,q : HC24Judgement; a : HC24Charge
  . tp26FramePotential(tr26Origin,p)=hc24Minus(hc24Potential(p)) %(tp26_calibration_origin_potential)%
  . tp26Reweight(f,p,q,a)=hc24Plus(a,hc24Plus(tp26FramePotential(f,q),hc24Minus(tp26FramePotential(f,p)))) %(tp26_endpoint_reweighting)%
then
  forall f,w : TR26Point; p,q,r : HC24Judgement; a,b : HC24Charge
  . tp26Reweight(f,p,r,hc24Plus(a,b))=hc24Plus(tp26Reweight(f,p,q,a),tp26Reweight(f,q,r,b)) %(tp26_potential_middle_cancellation)% %implied
  . tp26Reweight(f,p,p,a)=a %(tp26_cycle_cost_unchanged)% %implied
  . hc24NoMore(tp26Reweight(f,p,q,a),tp26Reweight(f,p,q,b)) <=> hc24NoMore(a,b) %(tp26_same_endpoint_comparison_return)% %implied
  . hc24NoMore(tp26Reweight(tr26Translate(f,w),p,q,a),tp26Reweight(tr26Translate(f,w),p,q,b)) <=> hc24NoMore(a,b) %(tp26_actual_torser_chart_comparison)% %implied
  . tp26Reweight(tr26Origin,p,q,a)=hc24LocalExcess(a,hc24Potential(p),q) %(tp26_original_Hilbert24_excess_return)% %implied
end
%% PROOFS: algebraic returns use associative/commutative regrouping and one
%% distribution. Powers and finite candidate/term folds use structural induction
%% with their arbitrary tail. Availability eliminates the two Cost constructors;
%% natural payloads remain universally quantified. FOUR then substitutes these
%% two proved laws componentwise. Valuation unequal-min uses x=(x+y)-y and
%% the triangle inequality twice; tied x,-x gives the explicit strict example.
%% A unique head beats the tail bound; a unique tail uses its induction value.
%% Zero has value infinity, so a finite unique minimum cannot sum to zero.
%% Potential shifts cancel at the identical middle judgement; comparisons
%% cancel the same endpoint shift in hc24Nonnegative(b-a). No total charge
%% order or minimum selector over all proofs is used in PotentialCharts.

%% RECOVERED ROUTE NORMALIZATION, WITH SOURCE OPERATIONS RETAINED.
%% Origin: HFPRouteData/Returns and HFPTwoCellData in the full-goals archive
%% (higher_paths/frame_paths.het). That pure-rechart route is generalized here
%% by retaining an ACTUAL source-operation word. Equal endpoints alone never
%% identify different computations. The ambient code has only a retraction;
%% the identity action is its projector, and is identity on admitted images.
spec MG26_ProgramData = H24_TorserCharts and
  { H24_WordAction with H24Letter |-> MG26Operation, H24Word |-> MG26Program,
    h24Nil |-> mg26Skip, h24Cons |-> mg26Do, h24Append |-> mg26Append,
    H24FoldValue |-> H24Payload, h24Step |-> mg26Act, h24Fold |-> mg26Evaluate }
then
  op mg26Lift : TR26Point * MG26Program * TR26Point * H24ChartValue -> H24ChartValue
  forall p,q : TR26Point; w : MG26Program; u : H24ChartValue
  . mg26Lift(p,w,q,u)=h24ChartEncode(q,mg26Evaluate(w,h24ChartDecode(p,u)))
      %(mg26_lift_the_actual_source_program)%
end
spec MG26_ProgramReturns = MG26_ProgramData then
  forall p,q,r : TR26Point; v,w : MG26Program; x : H24Payload; u : H24ChartValue
  . h24ChartDecode(q,mg26Lift(p,w,q,u))=mg26Evaluate(w,h24ChartDecode(p,u))
      %(mg26_original_program_readback)% %implied
  . mg26Lift(p,w,q,h24ChartEncode(p,x))=h24ChartEncode(q,mg26Evaluate(w,x))
      %(mg26_encoder_is_natural_for_actual_programs)% %implied
  . mg26Lift(q,v,r,mg26Lift(p,w,q,u))=
      h24ChartEncode(r,mg26Evaluate(v,mg26Evaluate(w,h24ChartDecode(p,u))))
      %(mg26_local_middle_chart_elimination)% %implied
  . mg26Lift(q,v,r,mg26Lift(p,w,q,u))=mg26Lift(p,mg26Append(w,v),r,u)
      %(mg26_computation_fusion_keeps_the_same_source_word)% %implied
  . h24Transition(q,r,mg26Lift(p,w,q,u))=mg26Lift(p,w,r,u)
      %(mg26_output_rechart_absorption)% %implied
  . mg26Lift(q,w,r,h24Transition(p,q,u))=mg26Lift(p,w,r,u)
      %(mg26_input_rechart_absorption)% %implied
end

spec MG26_RouteData = MG26_ProgramReturns then
  free type MG26Route ::= mg26Stay(TR26Point) |
      mg26Edge(TR26Point;MG26Program;TR26Point) | mg26Then(MG26Route;MG26Route)
  free type MG26Normal ::= mg26Normal(mg26NSource:TR26Point;
      mg26NProgram:MG26Program;mg26NTarget:TR26Point)
  ops mg26Source,mg26Target : MG26Route -> TR26Point;
      mg26Program : MG26Route -> MG26Program;
      mg26Run : MG26Route * H24ChartValue -> H24ChartValue;
      mg26Normalize : MG26Route -> MG26Normal;
      mg26NormalRun : MG26Normal * H24ChartValue -> H24ChartValue
  preds mg26Valid : MG26Route; mg26Composable,mg26SameNormal : MG26Route * MG26Route
  forall f,g : TR26Point; w : MG26Program; p,q : MG26Route; u : H24ChartValue; n : MG26Normal
  . mg26Source(mg26Stay(f))=f /\ mg26Target(mg26Stay(f))=f
  . mg26Source(mg26Edge(f,w,g))=f /\ mg26Target(mg26Edge(f,w,g))=g %(n25s_route_edge_endpoints)%
  . mg26Source(mg26Then(p,q))=mg26Source(p)
  . mg26Target(mg26Then(p,q))=mg26Target(q)
  . mg26Valid(mg26Stay(f)) /\ mg26Valid(mg26Edge(f,w,g)) %(n25s_route_leaf_valid)%
  . mg26Composable(p,q) <=> mg26Valid(p) /\ mg26Valid(q) /\ mg26Target(p)=mg26Source(q)
  . mg26Valid(mg26Then(p,q)) <=> mg26Composable(p,q)
  . mg26Program(mg26Stay(f))=mg26Skip
  . mg26Program(mg26Edge(f,w,g))=w %(n25s_route_edge_program)%
  . mg26Program(mg26Then(p,q))=mg26Append(mg26Program(p),mg26Program(q))
  . mg26Run(mg26Stay(f),u)=h24Transition(f,f,u)
  . mg26Run(mg26Edge(f,w,g),u)=mg26Lift(f,w,g,u)
  . mg26Run(mg26Then(p,q),u)=mg26Run(q,mg26Run(p,u))
  . mg26Normalize(p)=mg26Normal(mg26Source(p),mg26Program(p),mg26Target(p)) %(n25s_route_normalize)%
  . mg26NormalRun(n,u)=mg26Lift(mg26NSource(n),mg26NProgram(n),mg26NTarget(n),u)
  . mg26SameNormal(p,q) <=> mg26Normalize(p)=mg26Normalize(q) %(n25s_route_same_normal)%
end
spec MG26_RouteReturns = MG26_RouteData then
  forall p,q : MG26Route; u : H24ChartValue; x : H24Payload
  . mg26Valid(p) => mg26Run(p,u)=mg26NormalRun(mg26Normalize(p),u)
      %(mg26_route_normal_form_preserves_full_computation)% %implied
  . mg26Valid(p) => h24ChartDecode(mg26Target(p),mg26Run(p,u))=
      mg26Evaluate(mg26Program(p),h24ChartDecode(mg26Source(p),u))
      %(mg26_normal_form_returns_original_computation)% %implied
  . mg26Valid(p) /\ mg26Valid(q) /\ mg26SameNormal(p,q) => mg26Run(p,u)=mg26Run(q,u)
      %(mg26_certified_route_deduplication)% %implied
  . mg26Valid(p) => mg26Run(p,h24ChartEncode(mg26Source(p),x))=
      h24ChartEncode(mg26Target(p),mg26Evaluate(mg26Program(p),x))
      %(mg26_route_naturality_for_the_original_payload)% %implied
end
%% Induct on p. Stay uses the empty fold and E_f D_f; Edge is the lift
%% definition. Then uses the SAME middle frame, the two induction hypotheses,
%% D_mid E_mid=id, and the shared word-fold theorem. No states are enumerated.
%% SameNormal compares endpoints AND the operation word, not merely a price.

%% An explicit normal-form witness yields actual calculation savings. Count
%% only chart encodes/decodes here; source work remains in the unchanged word.
spec MG26_RouteCost = MG26_RouteReturns and H24_NaturalOrder then
  ops mg26Leaves,mg26ChartWork,mg26SavedChartWork : MG26Route -> GC26Nat
  forall f,g : TR26Point; w : MG26Program; p,q : MG26Route
  . mg26Leaves(mg26Stay(f))=GC26one
  . mg26Leaves(mg26Edge(f,w,g))=GC26one %(n25s_route_edge_leaves)%
  . mg26Leaves(mg26Then(p,q))=GC26nadd(mg26Leaves(p),mg26Leaves(q))
  . mg26ChartWork(p)=GC26nmul(GC26two,mg26Leaves(p)) %(n25s_route_chart_work)%
  . mg26SavedChartWork(p)=GC26nmul(GC26two,GC26pred(mg26Leaves(p)))
then
  forall p : MG26Route
  . GC26positive(mg26Leaves(p)) %(mg26_route_has_a_structural_leaf)% %implied
  . mg26ChartWork(p)=GC26nadd(GC26two,mg26SavedChartWork(p))
      %(mg26_exact_chart_work_eliminated_by_fusion)% %implied
end
%% Every leaf executes one decode/encode pair; for a VALID route its normal
%% form executes one pair for the complete source word. For k leaves the
%% eliminated work is exactly 2(k-1) such operations. The cost identity itself
%% is syntactic even for invalid routes, where equivalence is not asserted.
%% This unit-charge accounting does not estimate the cost of a source action.

%% Concrete interpretation in actual proof packets: every letter is a
%% HC24Move, and the free program is the already shared HC24Path.
spec MG26_ProofProgram = HC24_ProofPackets then
  op mg26Lift : TR26Point * HC24Path * TR26Point * HC24ProofPacket -> HC24ProofPacket
  forall f,g : TR26Point; u : HC24Path; x : HC24ProofPacket
  . mg26Lift(f,u,g,x)=hc24ProofPacketEncode(g,hc24Run(u,hc24ProofPacketDecode(f,x)))
      %(mg26_actual_proof_program_lift_definition)%
end
view MG26ActualProofProgram : MG26_ProgramData to MG26_ProofProgram =
  H24Payload |-> HC24Proof, H24ChartValue |-> HC24ProofPacket,
  h24ChartEncode |-> hc24ProofPacketEncode, h24ChartDecode |-> hc24ProofPacketDecode,
  h24Transition |-> hc24ProofPacketTransition, h24InChart |-> hc24ProofPacketAt,
  MG26Operation |-> HC24Move, MG26Program |-> HC24Path,
  mg26Skip |-> hc24Stay, mg26Do |-> hc24Next, mg26Append |-> hc24Append,
  mg26Act |-> hc24Apply, mg26Evaluate |-> hc24Run
end
%% Sentence preservation: the packet constructor supplies D_f E_f=id,
%% image eta and recharting. The shared word instance supplies precisely the
%% same append/fold recursion; the displayed lift is its mapped definition.
%% This actual view records those obligations rather than adding another
%% copy of the generic section as an axiom on the concrete proof packets.
spec MG26_ProofCalculationReturns = MG26_ProofProgram and HC24_CalibratedProofPackets then
  forall f,g : TR26Point; u : HC24Path; p : HC24Proof
  . hc24DecodePacket(mg26Lift(f,u,g,hc24ProofPacketEncode(f,p)))=hc24Run(u,p)
      %(mg26_fused_route_returns_the_actual_improved_proof)% %implied
  . hc24Admitted(u,p) =>
      hc24NoMore(hc24PacketWork(mg26Lift(f,u,g,hc24ProofPacketEncode(f,p))),hc24Work(p))
      %(mg26_fused_route_retains_the_Hilbert24_work_bound)% %implied
  forall f,g : TR26Point; u : HC24Path; p : HC24Proof; h : HC24Assumption
  . hc24Admitted(u,p) /\ hc24PacketUses(mg26Lift(f,u,g,hc24ProofPacketEncode(f,p)),h) => hc24Uses(p,h)
      %(mg26_fused_route_preserves_assumption_support)% %implied
end

%% A direct calculation in the SAME faithful block image. Only ring laws:
%% the product needs 12 scalar multiplications and 6 scalar additions in
%% this straight-line formula. Dense 4-by-4 evaluation uses 64 and 48.
%% These counts concern multiplication after the six coordinates are present;
%% encode/decode and source acquisition are charged separately.
spec MG26_SparseBlockCalculation = MT26_BlockCarrier then
  forall u,v,w,x,y,z,a,b,c,d,e,f : MT26_R
  . MT26_comp(MT26_block(u,v,w,x,y,z),MT26_block(a,b,c,d,e,f))=
      MT26_block(MT26_add(MT26_mul(u,a),MT26_mul(v,b)),
        MT26_add(MT26_mul(u,b),MT26_mul(v,a)),
        MT26_add(MT26_mul(w,c),MT26_mul(x,e)),
        MT26_add(MT26_mul(w,d),MT26_mul(x,f)),
        MT26_add(MT26_mul(y,c),MT26_mul(z,e)),
        MT26_add(MT26_mul(y,d),MT26_mul(z,f)))
      %(mg26_sparse_block_product_is_the_actual_matrix_product)% %implied
end
%% Expand application to an arbitrary vector, distribute, collect the two
%% blocks and use matrix extensionality. A symbolic polynomial check uses
%% independent indeterminates for all twelve coefficients, not sampled states.

%% A second costed route keeps C2 and avoids division by 2. Let p=u*a,
%% q=v*b, r=(u+v)*(a+b). Its two outputs are p+q and r-(p+q).
%% Reusing p+q reduces the full block product to 11 multiplications and
%% 8 additions/subtractions. With separately charged unary negation this is
%% 11 multiplications, 8 additions and 1 negation. No global faster claim:
%% compared with 12M+6A, choose it when M >= 2A+N in that charge model.
spec MG26_C2ProductFactoring = MG26_SparseBlockCalculation then
  op mg26CrossC2 : MT26_R * MT26_R * MT26_R * MT26_R -> MT26_R
  forall u,v,a,b : MT26_R
  . mg26CrossC2(u,v,a,b)=MT26_add(
      MT26_mul(MT26_add(u,v),MT26_add(a,b)),
      MT26_neg(MT26_add(MT26_mul(u,a),MT26_mul(v,b))))
      %(mg26_c2_factored_cross_term_definition)%
then
  forall u,v,w,x,y,z,a,b,c,d,e,f : MT26_R
  . mg26CrossC2(u,v,a,b)=MT26_add(MT26_mul(u,b),MT26_mul(v,a))
      %(mg26_c2_three_product_return_without_division)% %implied
  . MT26_comp(MT26_block(u,v,w,x,y,z),MT26_block(a,b,c,d,e,f))=
      MT26_block(MT26_add(MT26_mul(u,a),MT26_mul(v,b)),mg26CrossC2(u,v,a,b),
        MT26_add(MT26_mul(w,c),MT26_mul(x,e)),MT26_add(MT26_mul(w,d),MT26_mul(x,f)),
        MT26_add(MT26_mul(y,c),MT26_mul(z,e)),MT26_add(MT26_mul(y,d),MT26_mul(z,f)))
      %(mg26_shared_c2_factor_product_is_the_same_matrix)% %implied
end
%% Distributivity expands r into ua+ub+va+vb. Additive cancellation removes
%% p+q; substituting the result into the first block product proves the second
%% formula. This works in every commutative ring, including characteristic 2
%% and Z_2. Common-subexpression sharing, not the mathematical term tree,
%% supplies the stated straight-line cost; the replay harness builds that DAG.

spec MG26_CoefficientProduct = MT26_GroupAlgebraReturn and MG26_SparseBlockCalculation then
  op mg26CoefficientProduct : MT26_V6 * MT26_V6 -> MT26_V6
  forall a,b : MT26_V6
  . mg26CoefficientProduct(a,b)=MT26_decode6(MT26_comp(MT26_encode6(a),MT26_encode6(b)))
      %(mg26_original_coefficient_product_definition)%
then
  forall a,b : MT26_V6
  . MT26_encode6(mg26CoefficientProduct(a,b))=MT26_comp(MT26_encode6(a),MT26_encode6(b))
      %(mg26_coefficient_product_and_sparse_calculation_are_equivalent)% %implied
end
%% Every encoded vector is a block. The displayed product is again a block,
%% so encode6(decode6(product))=product by the existing block return. The
%% full C2 factor is retained; no doubled-standard-block substitution occurs.

%% EXACT DEFECT OF COARSE COMPOSITION. The additive structure is introduced
%% only here. The sole linearity premise is additivity of the composite D S;
%% neither the section E, the projection D alone, nor T is assumed additive.
spec MG26_AdditiveDefect = CN26_SplitObservations and
  { H24_AdditiveGroup with H24Scalar |-> CN26Code,
      h24Zero |-> mg26CodeZero, h24Add |-> mg26CodeAdd, h24Neg |-> mg26CodeNeg } and
  { H24_AdditiveGroup with H24Scalar |-> CN26Source,
      h24Zero |-> mg26SourceZero, h24Add |-> mg26SourceAdd, h24Neg |-> mg26SourceNeg }
then
  ops mg26ReadAfter : CN26Operation * CN26Code -> CN26Source;
      mg26Residual : CN26Operation * CN26Source -> CN26Code;
      mg26Defect : CN26Operation * CN26Operation * CN26Source -> CN26Source
  pred mg26AdditiveRead : CN26Operation
  forall s,t : CN26Operation; y : CN26Source; u : CN26Code
  . mg26ReadAfter(s,u)=CN26decode(CN26run(s,u)) %(mg26_read_after_definition)%
  . mg26AdditiveRead(s) <=> (forall a,b : CN26Code .
      mg26ReadAfter(s,mg26CodeAdd(a,b))=mg26SourceAdd(mg26ReadAfter(s,a),mg26ReadAfter(s,b)))
      %(mg26_only_composite_read_additivity)%
  . mg26Residual(t,y)=mg26CodeAdd(CN26run(t,CN26encode(y)),
      mg26CodeNeg(CN26normalize(CN26run(t,CN26encode(y))))) %(mg26_lift_residual_definition)%
  . mg26Defect(s,t,y)=mg26SourceAdd(CN26decode(CN26run(s,CN26run(t,CN26encode(y)))),
      mg26SourceNeg(CN26coarse(s,CN26coarse(t,y)))) %(mg26_composition_defect_definition)%
then
  forall s,t : CN26Operation; y : CN26Source; u,v : CN26Code
  . mg26AdditiveRead(s) => mg26ReadAfter(s,mg26CodeZero)=mg26SourceZero
      %(mg26_additive_read_preserves_zero)% %implied
  . mg26AdditiveRead(s) => mg26ReadAfter(s,mg26CodeNeg(u))=mg26SourceNeg(mg26ReadAfter(s,u))
      %(mg26_additive_read_preserves_negation)% %implied
  . mg26AdditiveRead(s) => mg26ReadAfter(s,mg26CodeAdd(u,mg26CodeNeg(v)))=
      mg26SourceAdd(mg26ReadAfter(s,u),mg26SourceNeg(mg26ReadAfter(s,v)))
      %(mg26_additive_read_preserves_difference)% %implied
  . mg26AdditiveRead(s) => mg26Defect(s,t,y)=mg26ReadAfter(s,mg26Residual(t,y))
      %(mg26_exact_split_composition_defect)% %implied
  . mg26Defect(s,t,y)=mg26SourceZero <=>
      CN26decode(CN26run(s,CN26run(t,CN26encode(y))))=CN26coarse(s,CN26coarse(t,y))
      %(mg26_zero_defect_iff_exact_composition)% %implied
  . CN26descends(s) \/ CN26lifts(t) => mg26Defect(s,t,y)=mg26SourceZero
      %(mg26_one_sided_coherence_kills_defect)% %implied
end
%% Put F=D S. From F(0)=F(0)+F(0), additive cancellation gives F(0)=0.
%% Apply F to u+(-u)=0 and uniqueness of additive inverses gives F(-u)=-F(u).
%% Thus F(a-b)=F(a)-F(b). Substitute a=T E y and b=E D T E y.
%% Expanding bar(S)bar(T) gives exactly F(b), proving the defect identity.
%% A-B=0 iff A=B uses only the source additive group, so that equivalence
%% needs no linearity. The preceding split-observation theorem kills the
%% defect when S descends OR T lifts, including nonlinear such operations.

%% Geometric meaning for a VALUED SCALAR read of the same actual defect.
%% This instance does not impose a valuation on arbitrary proof objects.
spec MG26_ValuedDefect = TP26_AdditiveValuation and
  { MG26_AdditiveDefect with CN26Source |-> TP26K,
      mg26SourceZero |-> tp26K0, mg26SourceAdd |-> tp26KAdd, mg26SourceNeg |-> tp26KNeg }
then
  forall s,t : CN26Operation; y : TP26K
  . tp26Val(mg26Defect(s,t,y))=tp26Infinity <=>
      CN26decode(CN26run(s,CN26run(t,CN26encode(y))))=CN26coarse(s,CN26coarse(t,y))
      %(mg26_valuation_detects_exact_coarse_composition)% %implied
  . not tp26Val(CN26decode(CN26run(s,CN26run(t,CN26encode(y)))))=
          tp26Val(CN26coarse(s,CN26coarse(t,y))) =>
      tp26Val(mg26Defect(s,t,y))=
        tp26Choice(tp26Val(CN26decode(CN26run(s,CN26run(t,CN26encode(y))))),
                   tp26Val(CN26coarse(s,CN26coarse(t,y)))) /\
      not mg26Defect(s,t,y)=tp26K0
      %(mg26_unequal_valuation_obstructs_exact_composition)% %implied
end
%% The first statement is the valuation zero-kernel law composed with the
%% zero-defect equivalence. For the second, apply unequal-valuation minimum
%% to A+(-B), use v(-B)=v(B), and note a minimum of two unequal values cannot
%% be infinity. Equality of the two valuations alone gives no cancellation.

spec MG26_DefectBend = MG26_ValuedDefect and TP26_LeadingTermReturn then
  op mg26DefectTerms : CN26Operation * CN26Operation * TP26K -> TP26Terms
  forall s,t : CN26Operation; y : TP26K
  . mg26DefectTerms(s,t,y)=tp26Term(CN26decode(CN26run(s,CN26run(t,CN26encode(y)))),
      tp26Term(tp26KNeg(CN26coarse(s,CN26coarse(t,y))),tp26NoTerms))
      %(mg26_same_composition_terms_for_tropicalization)%
then
  forall s,t : CN26Operation; y : TP26K
  . tp26SumTerms(mg26DefectTerms(s,t,y))=mg26Defect(s,t,y)
      %(mg26_tropicalized_terms_sum_to_original_defect)% %implied
  . tp26Bend(mg26DefectTerms(s,t,y)) <=>
      not tp26Val(CN26decode(CN26run(s,CN26run(t,CN26encode(y)))))=tp26Infinity /\
      tp26Val(CN26decode(CN26run(s,CN26run(t,CN26encode(y)))))=
        tp26Val(CN26coarse(s,CN26coarse(t,y)))
      %(mg26_two_term_bend_is_exactly_finite_tie)% %implied
  . CN26decode(CN26run(s,CN26run(t,CN26encode(y))))=CN26coarse(s,CN26coarse(t,y)) /\
      not CN26coarse(s,CN26coarse(t,y))=tp26K0 => tp26Bend(mg26DefectTerms(s,t,y))
      %(mg26_nonzero_exact_composition_lies_on_tropical_bend)% %implied
end
%% Unfold the two actual terms [A,-B]. Their sum is A-B; their weights are
%% v(A),v(B). If the weights differ, exactly one finite lowest term remains.
%% If they agree finitely, neither occurrence is unique lowest. If both are
%% infinity the required finite-minimum guard fails. This proves the bend
%% equivalence directly. Exact A=B!=0 implies its finite tie; a tie need not
%% imply A=B. Thus canonicity/composition and tropicalization share this SAME
%% residual, without replacing the original equality by its weaker bend test.

%% FINAL COVERAGE UNION. Select narrow theories for proving; this union does
%% not turn every source contract into a necessary premise of every result.
%% Natural transformations here are the displayed encoder/rechart squares.
%% On represented images, route identity and composition form the ordinary
%% category of charts and retained source programs. Parallel route equalities
%% give semantic 2-cells; the literal route syntax and original proof payloads
%% remain distinct unless a stated equality actually identifies them.
spec AT26_CalculationAtlas = AT26_NumberTheoryAtlas and
  CN26_CanonicityReturns and TP26_TotalTropical and TP26_NaturalCostOrder and
  TP26_FourSupport and TP26_FiniteCandidates and TP26_Valuation and TP26_PotentialCharts and
  MG26_RouteCost and MG26_ProofCalculationReturns and MG26_C2ProductFactoring and
  MG26_CoefficientProduct and MG26_DefectBend
end

%% CONSOLIDATION / ASSUMPTION LEDGER
%% ONE H24_Naturals carrier and divisor fold; ONE H24_WordCarrier and shared
%% append/fold induction; ONE additive-group/ring law family, renamed by views.
%% Legacy hc24_path_identity_and_associative_composition is the HC24 instance
%% of h24_word_right_identity + h24_word_associativity (left unit is recursion).
%% Legacy hc24_composition_at_the_actual_middle_proof is precisely the HC24
%% instance of h24_fold_append_fusion. Both duplicate goal clauses are removed.
%% Removed three redundant unrenamed aggregate imports, with their endpoints
%% still reached through UnitClassificationSource, PresentedDirichletConnection
%% and PositiveAxisInverse. Source-definition nodes are never merged with a
%% theorem/conjecture node just because their displayed formulas coincide.
%% Bit/point carriers are separate from XOR/group laws. Generic charts need
%% only their frame interface; FOUR x FOUR needs coordinate beta/eta alone.
%% HC24 path definitions use ProofData; minimum arguments enter PathReturns.
%% Theta/Weierstrass declarations use EisensteinSignature; analytic theorem
%% contracts enter the theorem layer. Sparse products need no inverse of 3;
%% the six-coefficient decoder explicitly retains its specified inverse of 3.
%% Valuation/leading-term/defect-bend laws need additive valuation only. The
%% scalar monomial layer adds multiplication and natural powers. A decidable
%% comparison is supplied only for the finite-candidate selection operation.
%% Unit-cost E/D savings count valid routes and retain every source operation.
%% Cost-only tropical minimization compares alternatives for a fixed endpoint
%% and admissible support. Required premises compose by sum. Dominance in one
%% charge model is not equality of proof objects or a globally weakest theory.

%% RECOVERED SOURCE IDENTITIES
%% Full_Targets_Tesseract_Metagraph.json | SHA256 b443567844654bcce53bd1e2e6dcf84a227b9c1cbbf948d13673c83771ee4e0b
%% Tesseract_Metatheorems.md | SHA256 9e95a8909f3f53f30c5f27c27d6f6ae89ef3da162796186bea8393c0d51a98c5
%% CONSOLIDATED_PROOFS.md | SHA256 8e7f7d8958fb528df0f25600b7999a8ce42460c1c33a711ee84e78ee8ce626fd
%% Torser_Canonical_Readings.het | SHA256 b4067b8912a70710afb951d784832350cf4c3a6b6c515ba5255b38c1638f2256
%% Torser_Canonical_Marks.het | SHA256 d88587b53555c0b97ad0a3a32d494b0775b25d13df0233eea8647ecef4254faf
%% Native canonicity origins: CanonicalRawReadback, CanonicalDecoderLaws,
%% FrameWordNaturality, MarkActionLaws and EndpointChoiceTest. Split observations
%% come from the actual recovered Tesseract_Metatheorems construction. Pure chart
%% routes come from HFPRouteData/Returns; source-operation words are now retained.
%% The source graph's original full RH/Dirichlet targets and actual frame/read
%% records were inspected. Its archived statuses are provenance, not fresh proofs.
%% Other GRH families in that graph are not silently equated with Dirichlet GRH.

%% BEGIN_TROPICAL_CONSTRUCTIVE_PROOFS
%% Write a+b for tropical choice and ab for tropical composition in this paragraph
%% only; 0_T is the unavailable element and 1_T is ordinary numerical zero.
%%
%% Duplicate absorption: a+(a+b)=(a+a)+b=a+b. For the order a<=b iff a+b=a,
%% reflexivity is idempotence; if both a<=b and b<=a, commutativity gives a=b.
%% For transitivity, a+c=(a+b)+c=a+(b+c)=a+b=a. Multiplicative monotonicity
%% follows from ac+bc=(a+b)c=ac. The common-factor goal expands
%% (a+b)(c+d)=a(c+d)+b(c+d)=(ac+ad)+b(c+d), then reassociates addition.
%% These five proofs use exactly the stated idempotent-semiring laws.
%%
%% Exponent addition: induct on n in a^(m+n). At zero, m+0=m and a^0=1_T.
%% At successor, m+S(n)=S(m+n); apply the power recursion, the induction
%% hypothesis and associativity. Product power: induct on n; the base is
%% 1_T*1_T=1_T. The successor is (ab)(a^n b^n)=a^(n+1)b^(n+1), by associativity
%% and commutativity. These are structural, unbounded proofs, not finite tests.
%%
%% Bipotence a+b in {a,b} is added only in TP26_TotalOrder, independently of
%% naturals or powers. TP26_TotalTropical combines TotalOrder with Powers for
%% the power-choice goal. First induct on n
%% to prove a<=b implies a^n<=b^n: the base is equality; for the step, multiply
%% the previous bound and a<=b in turn and compose the two bounds. If a+b=a,
%% then (a+b)^n=a^n=a^n+b^n; the other case is symmetric. This proves tropical
%% power-choice. The conclusion is NOT asserted for arbitrary idempotent
%% semirings, where mixed monomials can survive.
%%
%% Concrete natural-cost view
%% --------------------------
%% The actual map TP26NaturalCostSemiring maps Weight to Cost, infinity to
%% Unavailable, zero to finite(0), and the two operations to their constructor
%% definitions. Its obligations follow by cases on Unavailable/finite, followed
%% by natural induction. They are not inserted as target axioms.
%%
%% Natural minimum is recursively min(0,n)=0, min(S(m),0)=0,
%% min(S(m),S(n))=S(min(m,n)). Commutativity follows by induction on the pair;
%% associativity by cases on zero/successor in each of three arguments, stripping
%% one simultaneous successor when all three are positive. Idempotence follows
%% by induction on n. Natural addition's two-sided identity, associativity and
%% commutativity follow from the shared H24 recursion by induction. Translation
%% preserves minimum: min(k+m,k+n)=k+min(m,n), by induction on k (derive the
%% left-recursive addition clauses from H24's right-recursive clauses first).
%% That last identity is precisely finite tropical distributivity. Infinity
%% cases reduce definitionally. The view therefore uses the same H24 Nat; no
%% second arithmetic carrier or ad hoc finite cost table is introduced.
%%
%% Availability and the five recovered FOUR returns
%% ------------------------------------------------
%% For either scalar law, split a and b into the two cost constructors. If both
%% are finite, both the minimum and the sum are finite with arbitrary natural
%% payloads; availability is on, matching on OR on and on AND on. If one is
%% unavailable, minimum is the other cost and sum is unavailable, matching OR
%% and AND. If both are unavailable, both outputs are off. These four constructor
%% patterns exhaust all costs while leaving every natural number symbolic.
%% The explicit coverage clause only repeats the free datatype's generation
%% property for first-order exports that otherwise omit generation.
%%
%% For cost pairs (a,b),(c,d), substitution of those two proved scalar laws gives
%%   eps(choice) = (eps(a) OR eps(c), eps(b) OR eps(d)),
%%   eps(compose) = ((eps(a) AND eps(c)) OR (eps(b) AND eps(d)),
%%                   (eps(a) AND eps(d)) OR (eps(b) AND eps(c))).
%% These are exactly knowledge join and signedCompose. Swapping a,b swaps the
%% two evidence coordinates, proving strong-negation return. Product eta extends
%% the constructor calculations to arbitrary pairs. The two controller return
%% goals then use coordinate projection beta and product eta; no cost injection
%% is claimed by the evidence map.
%%
%% Finite candidate witness
%% ------------------------
%% The input is a nonempty supplied finite list of Candidate objects, together
%% with a supplied DECIDABLE comparison tp26DecideLe returning the existing
%% TR26BitCarrier's on/off bits. Its explicit correctness contract says on iff
%% the first weight is <= the second, and off iff it is not. These computational
%% comparison assumptions occur ONLY in TP26_FiniteCandidates; the semiring,
%% valuation geometry and Hilbert24 potential modules do not import them.
%% In particular no computable comparison on arbitrary classical real weights
%% is silently inferred from a total order. Best is defined by a one-element
%% base and a branch on this supplied comparison bit for the head versus the
%% recursively selected tail candidate; ties select the new head. This is
%% explicit finite data, not a minimum-selection axiom over an arbitrary class
%% of proofs. Induct on the list.
%% The base selects its sole member and returns its cost. In the step, bipotence
%% makes one of head/tail cheapest; the guarded recursive equations select that
%% same candidate. Membership is either head equality or tail membership.
%% Its cost equals the folded minimum. The folded minimum is below each member:
%% for the head use min<=head; for a tail member combine min<=tail-minimum with
%% the induction hypothesis. These prove both candidate goals.
%% If all costs are infinity the selected member is still a member, with
%% unavailable cost. No available proof has thereby been manufactured. Root's
%% typed metagraph may instantiate Candidate with actual admissible paths;
%% membership/validity of that supplied catalogue remains its own typed data.
%%
%% Valuation proofs and exact cancellation boundary
%% ------------------------------------------------
%% TP26_AdditiveValuation imports TotalOrder and the shared additive-group
%% instance only: no natural-number carrier, powers, ring multiplication or
%% field inverse. LeadingTermReturn uses this additive valuation directly.
%% TP26_Valuation separately adds the shared ring and Powers for the monomial
%% calculation. FiniteCandidates also uses TotalOrder without importing Powers.
%% The valuation's zero is infinity, and its triangle law says
%% min(v(x),v(y)) <= v(x+y). Its multiplicative law alone is an equality.
%% The codomain is the generic total tropical carrier, allowing negative values;
%% it is deliberately not identified with nonnegative work costs. Standard
%% instances include v_2 on Q_2 or rational numbers, and order on Puiseux series.
%%
%% Suppose v(x)=a < b=v(y), and put s=v(x+y). The triangle law gives a<=s.
%% The additive-group identity x=(x+y)+(-y), together with v(-y)=v(y), gives
%% min(s,b)<=a. If s differs from a, then a<s and a<b. Bipotence says min(s,b)
%% is s or b, both strictly above a: contradiction. Thus s=a. The case b<a is
%% symmetric. This proves the unequal-valuation theorem. Its proof needs only
%% the additive-group, order, negation and triangle data, although the same
%% specification also contains multiplication for the monomial theorem.
%%
%% For x!=0, x+(-x)=0, so v(x+(-x))=infinity while v(x)<infinity and v(-x)=v(x).
%% This is the explicit tied-minimum cancellation counterexample. It prevents
%% the ordinary addition-to-tropical-choice equation being used universally.
%%
%% For monomials, induct on n to obtain v(x^n)=v(x)^n: the base uses v(1)=0_T
%% and the successor uses the multiplicative law. Multiply by v(c) to obtain the
%% displayed evaluated-monomial weight. Products of several variables follow by
%% repeated composition; coefficient weights and exponents remain explicit.
%%
%% Finite leading-term obstruction
%% -------------------------------
%% For a finite list ts, SumTerms is its actual ring sum and MinimumTerm is the
%% fold of the valuations. Induction gives MinimumTerm(ts)<=v(SumTerms(ts)).
%% The empty case is infinity<=infinity. At a head, monotonicity of minimum,
%% the tail bound and the triangle law give the next bound.
%%
%% UniqueLowest is a recursive, exact unique-FINITE-minimum test: it is false on
%% empty; at a head it says either the head weight is strictly below the entire
%% tail minimum, or the tail minimum is strictly below the head and remains
%% unique in the tail. If the head wins, the tail valuation bound places its
%% actual sum strictly above the head. Unequal-value equality returns the head
%% weight. If the tail wins, its induction value is its minimum, below the head;
%% the same unequal-value theorem returns that minimum. In both cases the
%% weight is finite because infinity is the order top. The zero-kernel law
%% therefore proves that the sum is nonzero. This is the unique-leading goal.
%%
%% If the actual sum vanishes and the minimum is finite, uniqueness is impossible
%% by the preceding theorem. This is precisely Bend. For a finite list, a finite
%% minimum is attained (induct on its head/tail fold). Failure of uniqueness then
%% means that at least two term occurrences attain it, by the same two-way split.
%% For the evaluated nonzero monomials of a polynomial at a torus point, this is
%% the necessary tropical hypersurface condition. It does not imply that the
%% particular source point is a root, nor provide a root lift at an arbitrary
%% valuation point. Repeated equal monomials should first be combined when
%% interpreting the usual polynomial support; the list theorem itself remains
%% valid for any finite sum of evaluated occurrences.
%%
%% Hilbert24 potential and actual torser charts
%% -------------------------------------------
%% For frame f and actual HC24 judgements p,q, write
%% delta_f(p,q)=FramePotential(f,q)-FramePotential(f,p).
%% Reweight(f,p,q,a)=a+delta_f(p,q). For p->q->r, the same middle potential
%% cancels, giving delta(p,q)+delta(q,r)=delta(p,r) and the composition goal.
%% For a loop p=p it is zero, so the original cost returns.
%%
%% In the comparison predicate hc24NoMore, compare (a+delta) and (b+delta).
%% Their difference is (b+delta)-(a+delta)=b-a. Hence the very same
%% hc24Nonnegative proposition is tested. This uses only the additive-group
%% laws and the definition of hc24NoMore; the charge order need not be total.
%% The identical argument applies in frame tr26Translate(f,w), giving the
%% actual torser-chart comparison with the same two endpoints and source costs.
%%
%% At tr26Origin the frame potential is -hc24Potential. Thus the reweighted
%% cost is a+Potential(p)-Potential(q), exactly hc24LocalExcess with the potential
%% of a single actual premise. This is the exact Hilbert24 connection; proof
%% trees with several premises still retain their original sum of premise
%% potentials and are not replaced by a minimum over required branches.
%% END_TROPICAL_CONSTRUCTIVE_PROOFS

%% HP25: THE RANKED TIGHT-POLICY CONSTRUCTOR ON THE EXISTING HC24 TREES.
%% Recovered from Hilbert24Policy.build/constructMinimum/returnOriginal in
%% Reversible_H24_Hets_Agda_Lean.zip v0 and ConstructMinimum_Fixed.zip v1.
%% The policy is explicit data checked by a predicate; no minimum or completed
%% proof is an input. All premise occurrences, their order and rule tags remain.
spec HP25_PolicyData = HC24_ProofData and H24_NaturalOrder then
  sort HP25Policy
  ops hp25ChosenRule : HP25Policy * HC24Judgement -> HC24Rule;
      hp25ChosenCharge : HP25Policy * HC24Judgement -> HC24Charge;
      hp25PremiseFamily : HP25Policy * HC24Judgement -> HC24Slots;
      hp25Rank : HP25Policy * HC24Judgement -> GC26Nat
  preds hp25AllBelow : HP25Policy * HC24Slots * GC26Nat;
        hp25WellFounded,hp25TightPolicy : HP25Policy
  forall P : HP25Policy; q : HC24Judgement; qs : HC24Slots; n : GC26Nat
  . hp25AllBelow(P,hc24EmptySlots,n) %(hp25_empty_family_rank)%
  . hp25AllBelow(P,hc24ConsSlot(q,qs),n) <=>
      h24LtN(hp25Rank(P,q),n) /\ hp25AllBelow(P,qs,n) %(hp25_complete_family_rank)%
  . hp25WellFounded(P) <=> (forall x : HC24Judgement .
      hp25AllBelow(P,hp25PremiseFamily(P,x),hp25Rank(P,x))) %(hp25_ranked_policy_definition)%
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) <=> hp25WellFounded(P) /\ (forall x : HC24Judgement .
      hc24Rule(hp25ChosenRule(P,x),hp25ChosenCharge(P,x),hp25PremiseFamily(P,x),x) /\
      hc24LocalExcess(hp25ChosenCharge(P,x),hc24PremisePotential(hp25PremiseFamily(P,x)),x)=hc24Zero)
      %(hp25_admitted_locally_tight_policy_definition)%
end

spec HP25_ConstructorData = HP25_PolicyData then
  ops hp25BuildAt : HP25Policy * GC26Nat * HC24Judgement -> HC24Proof;
      hp25BuildForestAt : HP25Policy * GC26Nat * HC24Slots -> HC24Forest;
      hp25Build : HP25Policy * HC24Judgement -> HC24Proof;
      hp25NodeCount : HC24Proof -> GC26Nat;
      hp25ForestNodeCount : HC24Forest -> GC26Nat
  forall P : HP25Policy; n : GC26Nat; q : HC24Judgement; qs : HC24Slots;
         p : HC24Proof; ps : HC24Forest; w : HC24Charge; r : HC24Rule
  . hp25BuildAt(P,GC26zero,q)=hc24Step(q,hp25ChosenCharge(P,q),hp25ChosenRule(P,q),hc24Empty)
      %(hp25_unreachable_zero_rank_branch)%
  . hp25BuildAt(P,GC26succ(n),q)=hc24Step(q,hp25ChosenCharge(P,q),hp25ChosenRule(P,q),
      hp25BuildForestAt(P,n,hp25PremiseFamily(P,q))) %(hp25_construct_chosen_rule_with_every_child)%
  . hp25BuildForestAt(P,n,hc24EmptySlots)=hc24Empty %(hp25_construct_empty_family)%
  . hp25BuildForestAt(P,n,hc24ConsSlot(q,qs))=
      hc24Cons(hp25BuildAt(P,n,q),hp25BuildForestAt(P,n,qs)) %(hp25_construct_complete_ordered_family)%
  . hp25Build(P,q)=hp25BuildAt(P,GC26succ(hp25Rank(P,q)),q) %(hp25_constructor_rank_derived_fuel)%
  . hp25NodeCount(hc24Step(q,w,r,ps))=GC26succ(hp25ForestNodeCount(ps)) %(hp25_node_count_step)%
  . hp25ForestNodeCount(hc24Empty)=GC26zero %(hp25_node_count_empty)%
  . hp25ForestNodeCount(hc24Cons(p,ps))=GC26nadd(hp25NodeCount(p),hp25ForestNodeCount(ps))
      %(hp25_node_count_all_premises)%
end
%% Total recursion is lexicographic on fuel, phase and the finite slot list:
%% BuildAt(succ n) calls ForestAt(n); ForestAt(n,Cons) calls BuildAt(n)
%% and its strict list tail. The zero branch is unreachable under rank(q)<fuel.
%% Public fuel is computed as rank(q)+1, never a user search/depth budget.

spec HP25_ConstructorReturns = HP25_ConstructorData and HC24_MinimumReturn then
  forall P : HP25Policy; q : HC24Judgement; qs : HC24Slots; n : GC26Nat; p : HC24Proof
  . hc24Conclusion(hp25BuildAt(P,n,q))=q %(hp25_every_constructed_root_keeps_judgement)% %implied
  . hc24Premises(hp25BuildForestAt(P,n,qs))=qs %(hp25_every_premise_occurrence_return)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) /\ hp25AllBelow(P,qs,n) =>
      hc24ForestValid(hp25BuildForestAt(P,n,qs)) /\ hc24ForestTight(hp25BuildForestAt(P,n,qs))
      %(hp25_ranked_complete_family_valid_and_tight)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) /\ h24LtN(hp25Rank(P,q),n) =>
      hc24Valid(hp25BuildAt(P,n,q)) /\ hc24Tight(hp25BuildAt(P,n,q))
      %(hp25_ranked_constructor_valid_and_tight)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) => hc24Conclusion(hp25Build(P,q))=q /\
      hc24Valid(hp25Build(P,q)) /\ hc24Tight(hp25Build(P,q)) /\ hc24Excess(hp25Build(P,q))=hc24Zero
      %(hp25_constructed_original_proof_zero_excess)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) => hc24Work(hp25Build(P,q))=hc24Potential(q)
      %(hp25_constructor_exact_proof_charge)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) => hc24Minimum(hp25Build(P,q))
      %(hp25_constructed_global_same_judgement_minimum)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) /\ hc24Valid(p) /\ hc24Conclusion(p)=q =>
      hc24NoMore(hc24Work(hp25Build(P,q)),hc24Work(p)) %(hp25_every_original_competitor_bound)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) /\ hc24Valid(p) => (hc24Minimum(p) <=> hc24Excess(p)=hc24Zero)
      %(hp25_complete_minimum_fibre_is_zero_excess)% %implied
end
%% PROOF. Outer induction on fuel, with every judgement and slot list arbitrary;
%% inner structural induction on the list proves the two family conclusions.
%% For fuel=0 the rank guard is impossible; the guarded family is empty.
%% At succ n, rank(q)<succ n gives rank(q)<=n. Every selected child has rank
%% <rank(q), hence <n. The induction hypotheses therefore return ALL its valid,
%% tight children and their identical premise slots. Admission and local zero
%% excess close the node. Instantiating fuel=succ(rank(q)) proves the public
%% return. HC24's already-proved Stokes identity gives Work=Potential, and its
%% lower bound covers every valid original proof with that conclusion.
%% Conversely any minimum competes with the constructed tight proof. Its work
%% is both >= and <= Potential; pointedness of the charge cone gives equality,
%% hence zero excess. No total order or global proof search is needed.

%% Dependent interpretation: retain a meaning at EACH original premise.
%% A value can inhabit several judgements; no untyped quotient is imposed.
spec HP25_RuleInterpretation = HC24_ProofData and
  { H24_WordCarrier with H24Letter |-> HP25Original, H24Word |-> HP25Originals,
    h24Nil |-> hp25NoOriginals, h24Cons |-> hp25ConsOriginal } then
  ops hp25OriginalConstructor : HC24Rule * HC24Charge * HC24Slots * HC24Judgement * HP25Originals -> HP25Original;
      hp25Interpret : HC24Proof -> HP25Original;
      hp25InterpretForest : HC24Forest -> HP25Originals
  preds hp25Meaning : HP25Original * HC24Judgement;
        hp25Meanings : HP25Originals * HC24Slots
  forall o : HP25Original; os : HP25Originals; q : HC24Judgement; qs : HC24Slots;
         p : HC24Proof; ps : HC24Forest; r : HC24Rule; w : HC24Charge
  . hp25Meanings(hp25NoOriginals,hc24EmptySlots) %(hp25_empty_dependent_meanings)%
  . not hp25Meanings(hp25NoOriginals,hc24ConsSlot(q,qs)) %(hp25_no_dropped_premise)%
  . not hp25Meanings(hp25ConsOriginal(o,os),hc24EmptySlots) %(hp25_no_extra_premise)%
  . hp25Meanings(hp25ConsOriginal(o,os),hc24ConsSlot(q,qs)) <=>
      hp25Meaning(o,q) /\ hp25Meanings(os,qs) %(hp25_complete_dependent_meanings)%
  . hc24Rule(r,w,qs,q) /\ hp25Meanings(os,qs) =>
      hp25Meaning(hp25OriginalConstructor(r,w,qs,q,os),q) %(hp25_actual_source_rule_interpretation)%
  . hp25Interpret(hc24Step(q,w,r,ps))=
      hp25OriginalConstructor(r,w,hc24Premises(ps),q,hp25InterpretForest(ps)) %(hp25_original_proof_fold)%
  . hp25InterpretForest(hc24Empty)=hp25NoOriginals %(hp25_original_empty_fold)%
  . hp25InterpretForest(hc24Cons(p,ps))=hp25ConsOriginal(hp25Interpret(p),hp25InterpretForest(ps))
      %(hp25_original_complete_family_fold)%
then
  forall p : HC24Proof; ps : HC24Forest
  . hc24Valid(p) => hp25Meaning(hp25Interpret(p),hc24Conclusion(p))
      %(hp25_interpretation_returns_original_judgement)% %implied
  . hc24ForestValid(ps) => hp25Meanings(hp25InterpretForest(ps),hc24Premises(ps))
      %(hp25_interpretation_returns_every_original_premise)% %implied
end
%% Simultaneous induction on proof/forest: empty matches empty; Cons preserves
%% both head meaning and tail family; Step applies the ACTUAL typed source
%% rule to exactly those meanings. No premise is replaced by a summary bit.

spec HP25_OriginalMinimumReturn = HP25_ConstructorReturns and HP25_RuleInterpretation then
  ops hp25ReturnOriginal : HP25Policy * HC24Judgement -> HP25Original;
      hp25Grade : HC24Judgement * HP25Original -> HC24Charge;
      hp25GradeSum : HC24Slots * HP25Originals -> HC24Charge;
      hp25EmbedOriginal : HC24Judgement * HP25Original -> HC24Proof
  forall P : HP25Policy; q : HC24Judgement; qs : HC24Slots; r : HC24Rule; w : HC24Charge;
         o : HP25Original; os : HP25Originals
  . hp25ReturnOriginal(P,q)=hp25Interpret(hp25Build(P,q)) %(hp25_construct_and_return_original)%
  . hp25GradeSum(hc24EmptySlots,hp25NoOriginals)=hc24Zero %(hp25_original_grade_empty)%
  . hp25GradeSum(hc24ConsSlot(q,qs),hp25ConsOriginal(o,os))=
      hc24Plus(hp25Grade(q,o),hp25GradeSum(qs,os))
      %(hp25_original_grade_all_premises)%
  . hc24Rule(r,w,qs,q) /\ hp25Meanings(os,qs) =>
      hp25Grade(q,hp25OriginalConstructor(r,w,qs,q,os))=hc24Plus(w,hp25GradeSum(qs,os))
      %(hp25_actual_source_constructor_grade)%
  . hp25Meaning(o,q) => hc24Valid(hp25EmbedOriginal(q,o)) /\ hc24Conclusion(hp25EmbedOriginal(q,o))=q /\
      hp25Interpret(hp25EmbedOriginal(q,o))=o /\ hc24Work(hp25EmbedOriginal(q,o))=hp25Grade(q,o)
      %(hp25_actual_original_competitor_embedding)%
then
  forall P : HP25Policy; q : HC24Judgement; o : HP25Original; p : HC24Proof
  . hc24Valid(p) => hp25Grade(hc24Conclusion(p),hp25Interpret(p))=hc24Work(p)
      %(hp25_grade_of_original_fold_is_exact_proof_work)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) => hp25Meaning(hp25ReturnOriginal(P,q),q) /\
      hp25Grade(q,hp25ReturnOriginal(P,q))=hc24Potential(q) %(hp25_constructed_original_exact_return)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) /\ hp25Meaning(o,q) =>
      hc24NoMore(hp25Grade(q,hp25ReturnOriginal(P,q)),hp25Grade(q,o))
      %(hp25_global_minimum_over_complete_original_family)% %implied
end
%% Fold-grade equality is simultaneous proof/forest induction using the
%% local source-constructor grade equation. Embed EVERY original competitor,
%% apply the native same-judgement minimum, and substitute both exact grades.
%% The concrete recovered Candidate family has grade n+1 for a verified model
%% and n+2 for fallback. A minimum fallback refutes every original assignment:
%% any verified model would be a strictly cheaper embedded competitor.

spec HP25_ConstructedProofPackets = HP25_ConstructorReturns and HC24_ProofPackets then
  op hp25BuildPacket : HP25Policy * HC24Judgement * TR26Point -> HC24ProofPacket
  forall P : HP25Policy; q : HC24Judgement; f,g : TR26Point
  . hp25BuildPacket(P,q,f)=hc24ProofPacketEncode(f,hp25Build(P,q)) %(hp25_entire_constructed_proof_packet)%
then
  forall P : HP25Policy; q : HC24Judgement; f,g : TR26Point; h : HC24Assumption
  . hc24DecodePacket(hp25BuildPacket(P,q,f))=hp25Build(P,q) %(hp25_packet_returns_actual_constructed_proof)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) => hc24PacketWork(hp25BuildPacket(P,q,f))=hc24Potential(q)
      %(hp25_packet_exact_calibrated_grade)% %implied
  . hc24PacketUses(hp25BuildPacket(P,q,f),h) <=> hc24Uses(hp25Build(P,q),h)
      %(hp25_packet_retains_actual_rule_assumptions)% %implied
  . hc24ProofPacketTransition(f,g,hp25BuildPacket(P,q,f))=hp25BuildPacket(P,q,g)
      %(hp25_constructor_natural_under_retained_frame_change)% %implied
end
%% Expand the constructed packet, project its payload, and use its existing
%% source-return law. Recharting changes only the frame, never the built proof.
%% Node count, proof charge, coefficient acquisition and machine bit steps
%% remain distinct observables. No polynomial producer clock follows merely
%% from these rank, exact-grade, or global-minimum theorems.


%% PN25: THE RECOVERED NATIVE PARACOLLATZ NORMALIZER AND EXACT RETURNS.
%% Source: H24_Native_Reductions.txt v0, Hilbert24_ConstructMinimum_Fixed.zip v1,
%% HRENativeData/Returns and GPCPeriodicData/Returns in Metalogic_Hilbert24_Torser.
%% Distinct dyadic scalar carrier: never impose separated dyadic congruences
%% on the complex-field MT26_R. The existing ring/matrix theory is reused.
spec PN25_Carrier = MT26_GroupAlgebraCoordinates with
  MT26_R |-> PN25Scalar, MT26_V6 |-> PN25Six,
  MT26_V4 |-> PN25Four, MT26_M4 |-> PN25Matrix
end
spec PN25_VectorData = PN25_Carrier then
  ops pn25Tau,pn25B,pn25L00,pn25L01,pn25L10,pn25L11 : PN25Six -> PN25Scalar;
      pn25Zero,pn25W : PN25Six;
      pn25Add,pn25Sub : PN25Six * PN25Six -> PN25Six;
      pn25Scale : PN25Scalar * PN25Six -> PN25Six;
      pn25U,pn25U2,pn25U3,pn25Read,pn25Q : PN25Six -> PN25Six;
      pn25PortSum : PN25Six -> PN25Scalar
  forall a,b,c,d,e,f,r : PN25Scalar; v,w : PN25Six
  . pn25Tau(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))=a /\ pn25B(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))=b /\
      pn25L00(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))=c /\ pn25L01(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))=d /\
      pn25L10(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))=e /\ pn25L11(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))=f %(pn25_coordinate_projections)%
  . MT26_v6(pn25Tau(v),pn25B(v),pn25L00(v),pn25L01(v),pn25L10(v),pn25L11(v))=v %(pn25_coordinate_eta)%
  . pn25Zero=MT26_v6(MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z) %(pn25_zero_vector)%
  . pn25W=MT26_v6(MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_o,MT26_o,MT26_o) %(pn25_four_port_column)%
  . pn25Add(v,w)=MT26_v6(MT26_add(pn25Tau(v),pn25Tau(w)),MT26_add(pn25B(v),pn25B(w)),
      MT26_add(pn25L00(v),pn25L00(w)),MT26_add(pn25L01(v),pn25L01(w)),
      MT26_add(pn25L10(v),pn25L10(w)),MT26_add(pn25L11(v),pn25L11(w))) %(pn25_vector_addition)%
  . pn25Scale(r,v)=MT26_v6(MT26_mul(r,pn25Tau(v)),MT26_mul(r,pn25B(v)),
      MT26_mul(r,pn25L00(v)),MT26_mul(r,pn25L01(v)),MT26_mul(r,pn25L10(v)),MT26_mul(r,pn25L11(v))) %(pn25_vector_scaling)%
  . pn25Sub(v,w)=pn25Add(v,pn25Scale(MT26_neg(MT26_o),w)) %(pn25_vector_subtraction)%
  . pn25U(MT26_v6(a,b,c,d,e,f))=MT26_v6(b,c,d,e,f,a) %(pn25_actual_six_channel_shift)%
  . pn25U2(v)=pn25U(pn25U(v)) /\ pn25U3(v)=pn25U(pn25U2(v)) %(pn25_shift_powers)%
  . pn25Read(v)=pn25Sub(v,pn25Scale(MT26_two,pn25U(v))) %(pn25_original_readback)%
  . pn25PortSum(v)=MT26_add(MT26_add(pn25L00(v),pn25L01(v)),MT26_add(pn25L10(v),pn25L11(v))) %(pn25_original_ordered_port_sum)%
  . pn25Q(v)=pn25Scale(pn25PortSum(v),pn25W) %(pn25_rank_one_original_contraction)%
then
  forall v,w : PN25Six; r : PN25Scalar
  . pn25U3(pn25U3(v))=v %(pn25_six_period_and_actual_involution)% %implied
  . pn25U(pn25Add(v,w))=pn25Add(pn25U(v),pn25U(w)) /\ pn25U(pn25Scale(r,v))=pn25Scale(r,pn25U(v))
      %(pn25_shift_is_linear)% %implied
end

%% The archived triangular C2 chart and the current regular C2 chart are
%% integrally conjugate. tau,b become u=tau-b,v=b; no division by two.
spec PN25_NativeChart = PN25_VectorData then
  ops pn25NativeMatrix,pn25ArchivedMatrix : PN25Six -> PN25Matrix;
      pn25NativeRead : PN25Matrix -> PN25Six;
      pn25Change,pn25ChangeInverse : PN25Matrix
  forall a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p : PN25Scalar; v : PN25Six
  . pn25NativeMatrix(v)=MT26_block(MT26_sub(pn25Tau(v),pn25B(v)),pn25B(v),
      pn25L00(v),pn25L01(v),pn25L10(v),pn25L11(v)) %(pn25_same_six_channels_regular_chart)%
  . pn25NativeRead(MT26_mat4(a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h,i,j,k,l,m,n,o,p))=MT26_v6(MT26_add(a,b),b,k,l,o,p) %(pn25_native_chart_decoder)%
  . pn25ArchivedMatrix(v)=MT26_mat4(MT26_sub(pn25Tau(v),MT26_mul(MT26_two,pn25B(v))),MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,
      MT26_z,pn25L00(v),pn25L01(v),MT26_z,MT26_z,pn25L10(v),pn25L11(v),MT26_z,
      pn25B(v),MT26_z,MT26_z,pn25Tau(v)) %(pn25_original_archived_sparse_matrix)%
  . pn25Change=MT26_mat4(MT26_o,MT26_neg(MT26_o),MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,
      MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z) %(pn25_integral_change_of_basis)%
  . pn25ChangeInverse=MT26_mat4(MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o,
      MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_z,MT26_o,MT26_z) %(pn25_integral_inverse_change_of_basis)%
then
  forall v : PN25Six
  . pn25NativeRead(pn25NativeMatrix(v))=v %(pn25_actual_six_channel_matrix_return)% %implied
  . MT26_comp(pn25Change,pn25ChangeInverse)=MT26_I4 /\ MT26_comp(pn25ChangeInverse,pn25Change)=MT26_I4
      %(pn25_unimodular_chart_inverse)% %implied
  . pn25ArchivedMatrix(v)=MT26_comp(pn25Change,MT26_comp(pn25NativeMatrix(v),pn25ChangeInverse))
      %(pn25_archived_and_regular_programme_matrix_are_same_chart)% %implied
end

%% First source involution: the actual Gram dagger, separately from U^3.
spec PN25_DaggerResolvent = PN25_NativeChart then
  ops pn25Gram,pn25DaggerLinear,pn25DaggerInverse : PN25Six -> PN25Six;
      pn25Residual,pn25Restore : PN25Six * PN25Six -> PN25Six
  forall v,b,r : PN25Six
  . pn25Gram(v)=pn25NativeRead(MT26_dagger(pn25NativeMatrix(v))) %(pn25_actual_gram_involution)%
  . pn25DaggerLinear(v)=pn25Sub(v,pn25Scale(MT26_two,pn25Gram(v))) %(pn25_I_minus_two_dagger)%
  . pn25DaggerInverse(v)=pn25Scale(MT26_neg(MT26_third),pn25Add(v,pn25Scale(MT26_two,pn25Gram(v)))) %(pn25_odd_unit_dagger_resolvent)%
  . pn25Residual(b,v)=pn25Sub(pn25DaggerLinear(v),b) /\ pn25Restore(b,r)=pn25DaggerInverse(pn25Add(r,b)) %(pn25_retained_affine_residual_and_restore)%
then
  forall v,b,r : PN25Six
  . pn25Gram(pn25Gram(v))=v %(pn25_native_gram_square)% %implied
  . pn25NativeMatrix(pn25Gram(v))=MT26_dagger(pn25NativeMatrix(v)) %(pn25_same_native_dagger_chart)% %implied
  . pn25DaggerLinear(pn25DaggerInverse(v))=v /\ pn25DaggerInverse(pn25DaggerLinear(v))=v %(pn25_original_dagger_resolvent_both_returns)% %implied
  . pn25Restore(b,pn25Residual(b,v))=v /\ pn25Residual(b,pn25Restore(b,r))=r %(pn25_affine_residual_original_source_return)% %implied
  . pn25Residual(b,v)=pn25Zero <=> v=pn25Restore(b,pn25Zero) %(pn25_constructed_unique_zero_residual)% %implied
end

%% Second source normalization: six-channel cycle. Unit 63 is the precise
%% extra scalar hypothesis; it holds in Z[1/63], Z_2 and all dyadic cuts.
spec PN25_CyclicResolvent = PN25_VectorData then
  ops pn25SixtyThree,pn25Inv63 : PN25Scalar;
      pn25H,pn25K,pn25Normalize,pn25TransportQ : PN25Six -> PN25Six
  . pn25SixtyThree=MT26_mul(MT26_seven,MT26_nine) %(pn25_denominator_63_definition)%
  . MT26_mul(pn25SixtyThree,pn25Inv63)=MT26_o %(pn25_supplied_odd_unit_63)%
  forall v : PN25Six
  . pn25H(v)=pn25Scale(MT26_neg(pn25Inv63),pn25Add(v,pn25Scale(MT26_eight,pn25U3(v)))) %(pn25_involution_factor_no_half_projectors)%
  . pn25K(v)=pn25Add(v,pn25Add(pn25Scale(MT26_two,pn25U(v)),pn25Scale(MT26_four,pn25U2(v)))) %(pn25_three_term_factor)%
  . pn25Normalize(v)=pn25K(pn25H(v)) %(pn25_actual_closed_normalizer)%
  . pn25TransportQ(v)=pn25Normalize(pn25Q(pn25Read(v))) %(pn25_transported_original_contraction)%
then
  forall v,w : PN25Six; r : PN25Scalar
  . pn25Read(pn25K(v))=pn25Sub(v,pn25Scale(MT26_eight,pn25U3(v))) %(pn25_three_term_geometric_factor)% %implied
  . pn25Read(pn25Normalize(v))=v /\ pn25Normalize(pn25Read(v))=v %(pn25_six_cycle_explicit_inverse_both_returns)% %implied
  . pn25Normalize(pn25Add(v,w))=pn25Add(pn25Normalize(v),pn25Normalize(w)) /\
      pn25Normalize(pn25Scale(r,v))=pn25Scale(r,pn25Normalize(v)) %(pn25_normalization_linear)% %implied
  . pn25Read(w)=v => w=pn25Normalize(v) %(pn25_same_source_normalized_solution_unique)% %implied
  . pn25TransportQ(v)=pn25Scale(pn25PortSum(pn25Read(v)),pn25Normalize(pn25W)) %(pn25_transported_contraction_rank_one_factor)% %implied
  . pn25Read(pn25TransportQ(pn25Normalize(v)))=pn25Q(v) %(pn25_all_original_ports_and_multiplicities_return)% %implied
end
view PN25NormalizationChart : H24_Retraction to PN25_CyclicResolvent =
  H24Source |-> PN25Six, H24Target |-> PN25Six, h24Encode |-> pn25Normalize, h24Decode |-> pn25Read
end
spec PN25_Naturality = PN25_CyclicResolvent then
  sort PN25Action
  ops pn25Action,pn25NormalizedAction : PN25Action * PN25Six -> PN25Six
  forall a : PN25Action; v : PN25Six
  . pn25NormalizedAction(a,v)=pn25Normalize(pn25Action(a,pn25Read(v))) %(pn25_same_operation_transported)%
then
  forall a : PN25Action; v : PN25Six
  . pn25NormalizedAction(a,pn25Normalize(v))=pn25Normalize(pn25Action(a,v)) %(pn25_actual_normalization_naturality)% %implied
  . pn25Read(pn25NormalizedAction(a,pn25Normalize(v)))=pn25Action(a,v) %(pn25_same_original_operation_return)% %implied
end

%% The only natural carrier is the existing H24 one. Congruence means the
%% actual ideal 2^m, not equality of an arbitrary fixed finite-state header.
spec PN25_Precision = PN25_CyclicResolvent and H24_NaturalOrder then
  ops pn25TwoPower : GC26Nat -> PN25Scalar;
      pn25IterateU,pn25Series : GC26Nat * PN25Six -> PN25Six
  pred pn25Cong : GC26Nat * PN25Six * PN25Six
  forall m : GC26Nat; v,w : PN25Six
  . pn25TwoPower(GC26zero)=MT26_o /\ pn25TwoPower(GC26succ(m))=MT26_mul(MT26_two,pn25TwoPower(m)) %(pn25_dyadic_scale_recursion)%
  . pn25IterateU(GC26zero,v)=v /\ pn25IterateU(GC26succ(m),v)=pn25U(pn25IterateU(m,v)) %(pn25_cyclic_iterate_recursion)%
  . pn25Series(GC26zero,v)=pn25Zero /\ pn25Series(GC26succ(m),v)=pn25Add(v,pn25Scale(MT26_two,pn25U(pn25Series(m,v)))) %(pn25_causal_geometric_prefix)%
  . pn25Cong(m,v,w) <=> (exists q : PN25Six . pn25Sub(v,w)=pn25Scale(pn25TwoPower(m),q)) %(pn25_actual_dyadic_congruence)%
then
  forall m,n : GC26Nat; v,w : PN25Six
  . pn25Read(pn25Series(m,v))=pn25Sub(v,pn25Scale(pn25TwoPower(m),pn25IterateU(m,v))) %(pn25_exact_finite_geometric_residual)% %implied
  . pn25Cong(m,pn25Series(m,v),pn25Normalize(v)) %(pn25_constructed_prefix_returns_requested_precision)% %implied
  . pn25Cong(m,v,w) <=> pn25Cong(m,pn25Normalize(v),pn25Normalize(w)) %(pn25_normalizer_preserves_and_reflects_each_cut)% %implied
  . h24LeN(m,n) /\ pn25Cong(n,v,w) => pn25Cong(m,v,w) %(pn25_restriction_to_every_lower_cut)% %implied
  . h24LeN(m,n) => pn25Cong(m,pn25Series(n,v),pn25Series(m,v)) %(pn25_normalized_prefixes_form_the_same_compatible_family)% %implied
end
%% This optional separated dyadic instance is on PN25Scalar, not on C.
%% A full point is already constructed by the closed N, so no inverse-limit
%% choice/completeness premise is required merely to exhibit that point.
spec PN25_SeparatedReturn = PN25_Precision then
  forall v,w : PN25Six
  . (forall m : GC26Nat . pn25Cong(m,v,w)) => v=w %(pn25_separated_dyadic_instance)%
then
  forall v,w : PN25Six
  . (forall m : GC26Nat . pn25Cong(m,w,pn25Series(m,v))) => w=pn25Normalize(v) %(pn25_all_precision_original_point_return)% %implied
end

%% Algebraic certificate of the embedded actual causal six-full-adder producer.
%% Its Boolean gate implementation proves the digit equation below; this
%% equation is a local transition definition, not an asserted final return.
spec PN25_DigitProducer = PN25_Precision then
  ops pn25InputDigit,pn25OutputDigit,pn25Carry,pn25Previous,
      pn25InputPrefix,pn25OutputPrefix : GC26Nat -> PN25Six
  forall m : GC26Nat
  . pn25Carry(GC26zero)=pn25Zero /\ pn25Previous(GC26zero)=pn25Zero %(pn25_producer_initial_registers)%
  . pn25Previous(GC26succ(m))=pn25OutputDigit(m) %(pn25_retained_previous_output_column)%
  . pn25OutputDigit(m)=pn25Sub(pn25Add(pn25InputDigit(m),pn25Add(pn25U(pn25Previous(m)),pn25Carry(m))),
      pn25Scale(MT26_two,pn25Carry(GC26succ(m)))) %(pn25_actual_full_adder_digit_equation)%
  . pn25InputPrefix(GC26zero)=pn25Zero /\ pn25OutputPrefix(GC26zero)=pn25Zero %(pn25_producer_prefix_base)%
  . pn25InputPrefix(GC26succ(m))=pn25Add(pn25InputPrefix(m),pn25Scale(pn25TwoPower(m),pn25InputDigit(m))) %(pn25_input_prefix_fold)%
  . pn25OutputPrefix(GC26succ(m))=pn25Add(pn25OutputPrefix(m),pn25Scale(pn25TwoPower(m),pn25OutputDigit(m))) %(pn25_output_prefix_fold)%
then
  forall m : GC26Nat
  . pn25Sub(pn25InputPrefix(m),pn25Read(pn25OutputPrefix(m)))=
      pn25Scale(pn25TwoPower(m),pn25Add(pn25Carry(m),pn25U(pn25Previous(m)))) %(pn25_exact_causal_digit_loop_invariant)% %implied
  . pn25Normalize(pn25InputPrefix(m))=pn25Add(pn25OutputPrefix(m),
      pn25Scale(pn25TwoPower(m),pn25Normalize(pn25Add(pn25Carry(m),pn25U(pn25Previous(m)))))) %(pn25_digit_circuit_equals_archived_closed_normalizer)% %implied
  . pn25Cong(m,pn25OutputPrefix(m),pn25Normalize(pn25InputPrefix(m))) %(pn25_actual_circuit_returns_same_precision_value)% %implied
end

%% Reversible base-3/2 carries use the HOMOGENEOUS integer numerator, so
%% no inverse of 2 is inserted into the dyadic ring. Fixed padding survives.
spec PN25_CarryTapes = PN25_Precision then
  free type PN25Tape ::= pn25Nil | pn25Cons(PN25Scalar;PN25Tape)
  free type PN25CarryMove ::= pn25Move(GC26Nat;PN25Scalar)
  ops pn25TapeLength : PN25Tape -> GC26Nat;
      pn25TapeNumerator : PN25Tape -> PN25Scalar;
      pn25CarryAt : GC26Nat * PN25Scalar * PN25Tape -> PN25Tape;
      pn25CarryStep : PN25CarryMove * PN25Tape -> PN25Tape
  forall a,b,q : PN25Scalar; k : GC26Nat; t : PN25Tape
  . pn25TapeLength(pn25Nil)=GC26zero /\ pn25TapeLength(pn25Cons(a,t))=GC26succ(pn25TapeLength(t)) %(pn25_actual_register_length)%
  . pn25TapeNumerator(pn25Nil)=MT26_z /\ pn25TapeNumerator(pn25Cons(a,t))=
      MT26_add(MT26_mul(a,pn25TwoPower(pn25TapeLength(t))),MT26_mul(MT26_three,pn25TapeNumerator(t))) %(pn25_homogeneous_three_halves_numerator)%
  . pn25CarryAt(k,q,pn25Nil)=pn25Nil /\ pn25CarryAt(GC26zero,q,pn25Cons(a,pn25Nil))=pn25Cons(a,pn25Nil) %(pn25_original_out_of_register_noop)%
  . pn25CarryAt(GC26zero,q,pn25Cons(a,pn25Cons(b,t)))=
      pn25Cons(MT26_sub(a,MT26_mul(MT26_three,q)),pn25Cons(MT26_add(b,MT26_mul(MT26_two,q)),t)) %(pn25_actual_retained_adjacent_carry)%
  . pn25CarryAt(GC26succ(k),q,pn25Cons(a,t))=pn25Cons(a,pn25CarryAt(k,q,t)) %(pn25_carry_at_original_address)%
  . pn25CarryStep(pn25Move(k,q),t)=pn25CarryAt(k,q,t) %(pn25_word_action_is_the_actual_carry)%
then
  forall q : PN25Scalar; k : GC26Nat; t : PN25Tape
  . pn25CarryAt(k,MT26_neg(q),pn25CarryAt(k,q,t))=t %(pn25_actual_local_carry_inverse)% %implied
  . pn25TapeLength(pn25CarryAt(k,q,t))=pn25TapeLength(t) /\ pn25TapeNumerator(pn25CarryAt(k,q,t))=pn25TapeNumerator(t)
      %(pn25_local_carry_preserves_full_numerator_and_extent)% %implied
end
spec PN25_CarryWords = PN25_CarryTapes and
  { H24_WordAction with H24Letter |-> PN25CarryMove, H24Word |-> PN25CarryWord,
      H24FoldValue |-> PN25Tape, h24Nil |-> pn25NoCarry, h24Cons |-> pn25CarryCons,
      h24Append |-> pn25CarryAppend, h24Step |-> pn25CarryStep, h24Fold |-> pn25CarryRun } then
  op pn25ReverseCarries : PN25CarryWord -> PN25CarryWord
  forall k : GC26Nat; q : PN25Scalar; w : PN25CarryWord
  . pn25ReverseCarries(pn25NoCarry)=pn25NoCarry %(pn25_inverse_empty_carry_word)%
  . pn25ReverseCarries(pn25CarryCons(pn25Move(k,q),w))=
      pn25CarryAppend(pn25ReverseCarries(w),pn25CarryCons(pn25Move(k,MT26_neg(q)),pn25NoCarry)) %(pn25_reverse_order_and_signed_carry_marks)%
then
  forall w : PN25CarryWord; t : PN25Tape
  . pn25CarryRun(pn25ReverseCarries(w),pn25CarryRun(w,t))=t /\
      pn25CarryRun(w,pn25CarryRun(pn25ReverseCarries(w),t))=t %(pn25_every_retained_carry_word_has_both_source_returns)% %implied
  . pn25TapeLength(pn25CarryRun(w,t))=pn25TapeLength(t) /\ pn25TapeNumerator(pn25CarryRun(w,t))=pn25TapeNumerator(t)
      %(pn25_whole_carry_word_preserves_original_rational_readback)% %implied
end
%% For an integer tape of length d>0, its rational value is Numerator/2^(d-1).
%% Retaining the sign and original odd denominator therefore returns that
%% SAME rational coefficient. 3/2 itself is not an element of Z_2.
%% Source-return proofs use ring algebra, structural induction and explicit
%% inverse words. Machine work is charged by BT25/the embedded actual gate compiler;
%% no clock theorem is inferred from tropical grade or a finite state count.


%% BT25. PAID BIT-STEP CLOCK, PREFIX TERMINATION, AND ORIGINAL-SIZE BOUNDS.
%% The fixed finite producer is bt25M, a finite bit word. Its interpreter,
%% primitive transition costs and checked-result predicate are implementation
%% parameters, not an existentially selected optimizer. A concrete instance
%% must count parsing, compilation, routing, lookup, exact arithmetic,
%% policy acquisition, proof checking and required original-source readback.
%% HC24 work/minimum grade is not identified with this clock.

spec BT25_ClockArithmetic = H24_NaturalOrder and HC24_OrderedChargeGroup then
  ops bt25Unit : HC24Charge;
      bt25Charge : GC26Nat -> HC24Charge
  pred bt25ChargeLe : HC24Charge * HC24Charge
  forall n,m : GC26Nat; a,b : HC24Charge
  . bt25Charge(GC26zero)=hc24Zero %(bt25_clock_zero)%
  . bt25Charge(GC26succ(n))=hc24Plus(bt25Charge(n),bt25Unit)
      %(bt25_clock_successor)%
  . bt25ChargeLe(a,b) <=> hc24Nonnegative(hc24Plus(b,hc24Minus(a)))
      %(bt25_charge_order)%
  . bt25ChargeLe(bt25Charge(n),bt25Charge(m)) <=> h24LeN(n,m)
      %(bt25_faithful_natural_clock_embedding)%
then
  forall n,m : GC26Nat; a,b,c : HC24Charge
  . bt25Charge(GC26nadd(n,m))=hc24Plus(bt25Charge(n),bt25Charge(m))
      %(bt25_charge_of_paid_sum)% %implied
  . bt25ChargeLe(a,b) /\ bt25ChargeLe(b,c) => bt25ChargeLe(a,c)
      %(bt25_charge_order_transitive)% %implied
  . bt25ChargeLe(hc24Plus(a,c),hc24Plus(b,c)) <=> bt25ChargeLe(a,b)
      %(bt25_same_potential_shift_cancels)% %implied
end
%% These use natural recursion and the existing additive group/cone laws.
%% The faithful embedding is explicit: an arbitrary HC24 proof-grade group
%% need not contain the natural operational clock. Integers with unit 1 do.

spec BT25_FixedProducer = H24_NaturalOrder and TR26BitCarrier and
  { H24_WordCarrier with H24Letter |-> TR26Bit, H24Word |-> BT25Word,
    h24Nil |-> bt25Empty, h24Cons |-> bt25Cons } then
  sorts BT25State,BT25Answer
  ops bt25M : BT25Word;
      bt25Length : BT25Word -> GC26Nat;
      bt25Start : BT25Word * BT25Word -> BT25State;
      bt25Next : BT25Word * BT25State -> BT25State;
      bt25StepCost : BT25Word * BT25State -> GC26Nat;
      bt25Source : BT25State -> BT25Word;
      bt25ReadyBit : BT25State -> TR26Bit;
      bt25Read : BT25State -> BT25Answer;
      bt25Run : BT25Word * GC26Nat -> BT25State;
      bt25Paid : BT25Word * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat
  preds bt25Domain : BT25Word;
        bt25Checked : BT25Word * BT25State;
        bt25OriginalAnswer : BT25Word * BT25Answer;
        bt25Prefix,bt25FirstReady,bt25ClockWithin : BT25Word * GC26Nat;
        bt25UniformClock : GC26Nat * GC26Nat
  forall x : BT25Word; b : TR26Bit; s : BT25State; n,t,N : GC26Nat
  . bt25Length(bt25Empty)=GC26zero %(bt25_original_empty_length)%
  . bt25Length(bt25Cons(b,x))=GC26succ(bt25Length(x))
      %(bt25_original_bit_length)%
  . bt25Run(x,GC26zero)=bt25Start(bt25M,x) %(bt25_same_machine_start)%
  . bt25Run(x,GC26succ(n))=bt25Next(bt25M,bt25Run(x,n))
      %(bt25_same_machine_next)%
  . bt25Source(bt25Start(bt25M,x))=x %(bt25_original_input_retained_at_start)%
  . bt25Source(bt25Next(bt25M,s))=bt25Source(s)
      %(bt25_original_input_retained_at_every_step)%
  . bt25Checked(x,s) <=> bt25Source(s)=x /\ bt25ReadyBit(s)=tr26On
      %(bt25_checked_event_is_the_stored_original_source_ready_bit)%
  . bt25Paid(x,GC26zero)=GC26zero %(bt25_paid_empty)%
  . bt25Paid(x,GC26succ(n))=GC26nadd(bt25Paid(x,n),bt25StepCost(bt25M,bt25Run(x,n)))
      %(bt25_paid_every_executed_step)%
  . bt25Prefix(x,GC26zero) %(bt25_prefix_empty)%
  . bt25Prefix(x,GC26succ(n)) <=> bt25Prefix(x,n) /\ not bt25Checked(x,bt25Run(x,n))
      %(bt25_prefix_stops_at_first_checked_event)%
  . bt25Prefix(x,GC26succ(n)) => h24LeN(GC26one,bt25StepCost(bt25M,bt25Run(x,n)))
      %(bt25_every_pre_event_step_has_positive_paid_cost)%
  . bt25FirstReady(x,n) <=> bt25Prefix(x,n) /\ bt25Checked(x,bt25Run(x,n))
      %(bt25_first_checked_event)%
  . bt25Checked(x,s) => bt25OriginalAnswer(x,bt25Read(s))
      %(bt25_checked_event_returns_the_same_original_input_answer)%
  . bt25ClockWithin(x,t) <=> (exists k : GC26Nat .
      bt25FirstReady(x,k) /\ h24LeN(bt25Paid(x,k),t))
      %(bt25_actual_paid_deadline_definition)%
  . bt25UniformClock(N,t) <=> (forall y : BT25Word .
      bt25Domain(y) /\ bt25Length(y)=N => bt25ClockWithin(y,t))
      %(bt25_worst_clock_over_original_length)%
then
  forall x : BT25Word; n,t,u : GC26Nat
  . bt25Prefix(x,n) => h24LeN(n,bt25Paid(x,n))
      %(bt25_prefix_steps_are_paid)% %implied
  . bt25ClockWithin(x,t) /\ h24LeN(t,u) => bt25ClockWithin(x,u)
      %(bt25_paid_deadline_is_monotone)% %implied
  . bt25ClockWithin(x,t) => (exists a : BT25Answer . bt25OriginalAnswer(x,a))
      %(bt25_deadline_returns_an_original_answer)% %implied
end
%% bt25Next is total: a crashed/stuck implementation must be represented by
%% a continuing positive-cost failure state, and cannot pass the theorem
%% below. The observation is a checked output event, not an absorbing full
%% configuration of an injective reversible update. After the first event,
%% no further state is included in bt25Prefix. Nontermination simply fails
%% bt25ClockWithin for every finite t. No total runtime has been postulated.
%% Readiness is the stored Boolean bit set after the paid check/readback,
%% not an oracle deciding whether an uncomputed answer exists.

spec BT25_PrefixPotential = BT25_FixedProducer and BT25_ClockArithmetic then
  ops bt25Phi,bt25Issued,bt25IssuedTotal : BT25Word * GC26Nat -> HC24Charge;
      bt25InitialBudget,bt25NewBudget : GC26Nat -> GC26Nat
  preds bt25Accounted : BT25Word * GC26Nat;
        bt25UniformPotentialBudget : ()
  forall x : BT25Word; n : GC26Nat
  . bt25IssuedTotal(x,GC26zero)=hc24Zero %(bt25_issued_empty)%
  . bt25IssuedTotal(x,GC26succ(n))=hc24Plus(bt25IssuedTotal(x,n),bt25Issued(x,n))
      %(bt25_issued_prefix_sum)%
  . bt25Accounted(x,GC26zero) %(bt25_account_empty)%
  . bt25Accounted(x,GC26succ(n)) <=> bt25Accounted(x,n) /\
      bt25ChargeLe(hc24Plus(bt25Charge(bt25StepCost(bt25M,bt25Run(x,n))),
          bt25Phi(x,GC26succ(n))),hc24Plus(bt25Phi(x,n),bt25Issued(x,n)))
      %(bt25_local_paid_cost_plus_next_potential_bound)%
  . bt25UniformPotentialBudget <=> (forall y : BT25Word . bt25Domain(y) =>
      bt25ChargeLe(bt25Phi(y,GC26zero),bt25Charge(bt25InitialBudget(bt25Length(y)))) /\
      (forall k : GC26Nat . bt25Prefix(y,k) => bt25Accounted(y,k) /\
        hc24Nonnegative(bt25Phi(y,k)) /\
        bt25ChargeLe(bt25IssuedTotal(y,k),bt25Charge(bt25NewBudget(bt25Length(y))))))
      %(bt25_all_prefix_budget_on_the_original_domain)%
then
  forall x : BT25Word; n,N : GC26Nat
  . bt25Accounted(x,n) =>
      bt25ChargeLe(hc24Plus(bt25Charge(bt25Paid(x,n)),bt25Phi(x,n)),
        hc24Plus(bt25Phi(x,GC26zero),bt25IssuedTotal(x,n)))
      %(bt25_actual_paid_prefix_telescope)% %implied
  . bt25UniformPotentialBudget /\ bt25Domain(x) /\ bt25Prefix(x,n) =>
      h24LeN(bt25Paid(x,n),GC26nadd(bt25InitialBudget(bt25Length(x)),
        bt25NewBudget(bt25Length(x))))
      %(bt25_paid_prefix_budget)% %implied
  . bt25UniformPotentialBudget =>
      bt25UniformClock(N,GC26nadd(bt25InitialBudget(N),bt25NewBudget(N)))
      %(bt25_positive_progress_and_all_prefix_budget_force_checked_deadline)% %implied
end
%% CONSTRUCTOR PROOF. Induct on n. The successor inequality adds the new
%% local certificate to the induction hypothesis and cancels Phi_n; sum
%% embedding and cone closure retain exactly the two endpoint potentials.
%% Nonnegative Phi_n and the initial/issued bounds give Paid_n<=K(N)+B(N).
%% For termination let D=K(N)+B(N). Traverse the unique finite run through
%% D+1 transitions, stopping at its first checked event. If none occurs,
%% prefix(D+1), positivity and the budget imply D+1<=Paid_(D+1)<=D, impossible
%% in the free naturals. Return that first event, its actual paid prefix and
%% original answer. This is a finite constructor with a PROVED deadline;
%% an ATP search timeout is not used as the producer or as this deadline.

spec BT25_Polynomials = H24_NaturalOrder then
  free type BT25Polynomial ::= bt25Constant(GC26Nat) | bt25Variable |
      bt25Sum(BT25Polynomial;BT25Polynomial) |
      bt25Product(BT25Polynomial;BT25Polynomial)
  op bt25Eval : BT25Polynomial * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat
  forall p,q : BT25Polynomial; c,n : GC26Nat
  . bt25Eval(bt25Constant(c),n)=c %(bt25_polynomial_constant)%
  . bt25Eval(bt25Variable,n)=n %(bt25_polynomial_variable)%
  . bt25Eval(bt25Sum(p,q),n)=GC26nadd(bt25Eval(p,n),bt25Eval(q,n))
      %(bt25_polynomial_sum)%
  . bt25Eval(bt25Product(p,q),n)=GC26nmul(bt25Eval(p,n),bt25Eval(q,n))
      %(bt25_polynomial_product)%
end

spec BT25_SharedKeyData = BT25_FixedProducer and BT25_Polynomials then
  ops bt25Keys,bt25KeyBits,bt25Precision,bt25Setup,bt25Readback,bt25PerKeyPaid : BT25Word -> GC26Nat;
      bt25PKeys,bt25PKeyBits,bt25PPrecision,bt25PSetup,bt25PReadback : BT25Polynomial
end

spec BT25_SharedKeyClock = BT25_SharedKeyData then
  ops
      bt25ImplementationConstant : GC26Nat;
      bt25PDimension,bt25PPerKey,bt25PWhole : BT25Polynomial;
      bt25DimensionBudget,bt25AcquiredBudget : BT25Word -> GC26Nat
  preds bt25ActualAcquiredKeyProduction,bt25PaidDimensionBound,bt25PolynomialDimensions : ()
  forall x : BT25Word
  . bt25DimensionBudget(x)=GC26nmul(bt25ImplementationConstant,
      GC26nmul(GC26succ(bt25Keys(x)),GC26npow(
        GC26succ(GC26nadd(bt25KeyBits(x),bt25Precision(x))),GC26two)))
      %(bt25_explicit_key_lookup_arithmetic_dimension_budget)%
  . bt25AcquiredBudget(x)=GC26nadd(bt25Setup(x),
      GC26nadd(GC26nmul(bt25Keys(x),bt25PerKeyPaid(x)),bt25Readback(x)))
      %(bt25_whole_acquired_key_cost_includes_both_endpoints)%
  . bt25PDimension=bt25Sum(bt25Sum(bt25PKeyBits,bt25PPrecision),bt25Constant(GC26one))
      %(bt25_dimension_polynomial)%
  . bt25PPerKey=bt25Product(bt25Constant(bt25ImplementationConstant),
      bt25Product(bt25Sum(bt25PKeys,bt25Constant(GC26one)),
        bt25Product(bt25PDimension,bt25PDimension)))
      %(bt25_paid_per_key_polynomial)%
  . bt25PWhole=bt25Sum(bt25PSetup,
      bt25Sum(bt25Product(bt25PKeys,bt25PPerKey),bt25PReadback))
      %(bt25_constructed_whole_clock_polynomial)%
  . bt25ActualAcquiredKeyProduction <=> (forall y : BT25Word . bt25Domain(y) =>
      bt25ClockWithin(y,bt25AcquiredBudget(y)))
      %(bt25_actual_acquisition_completion_and_paid_clock_premise)%
  . bt25PaidDimensionBound <=> (forall y : BT25Word . bt25Domain(y) =>
      h24LeN(bt25PerKeyPaid(y),bt25DimensionBudget(y)))
      %(bt25_bit_implementation_dimension_bound_premise)%
  . bt25PolynomialDimensions <=> (forall y : BT25Word . bt25Domain(y) =>
      h24LeN(bt25Keys(y),bt25Eval(bt25PKeys,bt25Length(y))) /\
      h24LeN(bt25KeyBits(y),bt25Eval(bt25PKeyBits,bt25Length(y))) /\
      h24LeN(bt25Precision(y),bt25Eval(bt25PPrecision,bt25Length(y))) /\
      h24LeN(bt25Setup(y),bt25Eval(bt25PSetup,bt25Length(y))) /\
      h24LeN(bt25Readback(y),bt25Eval(bt25PReadback,bt25Length(y))))
      %(bt25_all_dimension_bounds_use_original_input_length)%
then
  forall x : BT25Word; N : GC26Nat
  . bt25PaidDimensionBound /\ bt25PolynomialDimensions /\ bt25Domain(x) =>
      h24LeN(bt25AcquiredBudget(x),bt25Eval(bt25PWhole,bt25Length(x)))
      %(bt25_size_width_precision_bounds_compose_to_paid_work_bound)% %implied
  . bt25ActualAcquiredKeyProduction /\ bt25PaidDimensionBound /\ bt25PolynomialDimensions =>
      bt25UniformClock(N,bt25Eval(bt25PWhole,N))
      %(bt25_fixed_producer_original_length_polynomial_clock_transport)% %implied
end
%% The dimension expression is a sufficient implementation budget, not a
%% law of arbitrary algorithms. It can pay a sequential dictionary scan of
%% K bounded keys and quadratic fixed-width arithmetic; the caller must
%% prove its concrete per-key implementation fits it, including routing and
%% checking. A deterministic trie may give a smaller bound. The acquisition
%% premise includes termination/readback and may instead be constructed by
%% BT25_PrefixPotential. Polynomial key width does not imply polynomial K.
%% Proof: monotonicity of + and * substitutes all five size bounds, giving
%% the displayed polynomial by its four constructor equations; widen each
%% actual deadline and then quantify over inputs of the same ORIGINAL N.

spec BT25_ParacollatzPipeline = BT25_SharedKeyData then
  ops bt25PNConstant : GC26Nat;
      bt25AcquisitionPaid,bt25VerificationPaid,bt25PNBudget : BT25Word -> GC26Nat;
      bt25PAcquisition,bt25PVerification,bt25PNPolynomial : BT25Polynomial
  preds bt25PNCompletedPipeline,bt25PNPolynomialInputs : ()
  forall x : BT25Word
  . bt25PNBudget(x)=GC26nadd(bt25Setup(x),GC26nadd(bt25AcquisitionPaid(x),
      GC26nadd(GC26nmul(bt25PNConstant,GC26nmul(bt25Keys(x),GC26succ(bt25Precision(x)))),
        GC26nadd(bt25VerificationPaid(x),bt25Readback(x)))))
      %(bt25_paid_five_phase_paracollatz_pipeline)%
  . bt25PNPolynomial=bt25Sum(bt25PSetup,bt25Sum(bt25PAcquisition,
      bt25Sum(bt25Product(bt25Constant(bt25PNConstant),bt25Product(bt25PKeys,
          bt25Sum(bt25PPrecision,bt25Constant(GC26one)))),
        bt25Sum(bt25PVerification,bt25PReadback))))
      %(bt25_constructed_paracollatz_clock_polynomial)%
  . bt25PNCompletedPipeline <=> (forall y : BT25Word . bt25Domain(y) =>
      bt25ClockWithin(y,bt25PNBudget(y)))
      %(bt25_actual_fixed_pipeline_completion_and_paid_account)%
  . bt25PNPolynomialInputs <=> (forall y : BT25Word . bt25Domain(y) =>
      h24LeN(bt25Keys(y),bt25Eval(bt25PKeys,bt25Length(y))) /\
      h24LeN(bt25Precision(y),bt25Eval(bt25PPrecision,bt25Length(y))) /\
      h24LeN(bt25Setup(y),bt25Eval(bt25PSetup,bt25Length(y))) /\
      h24LeN(bt25AcquisitionPaid(y),bt25Eval(bt25PAcquisition,bt25Length(y))) /\
      h24LeN(bt25VerificationPaid(y),bt25Eval(bt25PVerification,bt25Length(y))) /\
      h24LeN(bt25Readback(y),bt25Eval(bt25PReadback,bt25Length(y))))
      %(bt25_pipeline_bounds_at_original_input_length)%
then
  forall x : BT25Word; N : GC26Nat
  . bt25PNPolynomialInputs /\ bt25Domain(x) =>
      h24LeN(bt25PNBudget(x),bt25Eval(bt25PNPolynomial,bt25Length(x)))
      %(bt25_concrete_normalizer_and_acquisition_bounds_compose)% %implied
  . bt25PNCompletedPipeline /\ bt25PNPolynomialInputs =>
      bt25UniformClock(N,bt25Eval(bt25PNPolynomial,N))
      %(bt25_one_fixed_paracollatz_producer_has_original_length_polynomial_clock)% %implied
end
%% Concrete finite-normalization domain: six already supplied m-bit lanes,
%% y[j,i]=(x[j,i]+y[j+1,i-1]+carry[j,i]) mod 2 and the quotient-by-two carry.
%% Six full adders per column, the specified reversible history schedule,
%% clean readback and global uncomputation give 330*m logical gates. A fixed
%% local-tape implementation witness pays routing/control by cPN*(m+1).
%% This is the PN branch's actual algorithm; see its embedded gate constructor.
%% For K such supplied keys, the displayed term is cPN*K*(m+1). Acquisition
%% includes discovering keys, lookup, original payload creation and choosing
%% any ranked tight policy; verification/readback are separately charged.
%% The sharper PN theorem does not need a key-width premise once those costs
%% are already bounded. Supplied finite m-bit normalization is polynomial;
%% its correctness and paid clock do not assert a polynomial acquisition of
%% a key catalogue for every succinct original problem. As in the previous
%% theorem, natural monotonicity constructs the explicit sum/product bound.

spec BT25_CleanReversibleClock = H24_Naturals then
  sort BT25ReversibleJob
  ops bt25ComputeClock,bt25CopyClock,bt25UncomputeClock,
      bt25CleanClock : BT25ReversibleJob -> GC26Nat
  forall x : BT25ReversibleJob
  . bt25UncomputeClock(x)=bt25ComputeClock(x)
      %(bt25_inverse_executes_the_same_number_of_paid_primitive_steps)%
  . bt25CleanClock(x)=GC26nadd(bt25ComputeClock(x),
      GC26nadd(bt25CopyClock(x),bt25UncomputeClock(x)))
      %(bt25_clean_program_runs_compute_copy_uncompute)%
then
  forall x : BT25ReversibleJob
  . bt25CleanClock(x)=GC26nadd(GC26nmul(GC26two,bt25ComputeClock(x)),bt25CopyClock(x))
      %(bt25_clean_reversible_clock_is_two_g_plus_r)% %implied
end
%% Here g and r are already LOCAL paid bit costs. If g is only an addressed
%% logical-gate count, a proved routing/compiler simulation bound must first
%% convert it to a paid bit clock; inverse state return does not erase g.


%% GT25: PRIOR CLOSED CONSTRUCTOR, restricted to its ACTUAL source syntax.
%% Woker_Closed_Guarded_Tomography_Return.tex (9 Nov 2026), Thms 3.1,4.1,5.2.
%% SOURCE port for that constructed algorithm; not a fresh kernel replay.
%% Its fixed machine is separate from the generic BT25 producer/pipeline.
spec GT25_SOURCE_GuardedClock =
  { BT25_FixedProducer with BT25State |-> GT25State, BT25Answer |-> GT25Answer,
    bt25M |-> gt25M, bt25Start |-> gt25Start, bt25Next |-> gt25Next,
    bt25StepCost |-> gt25StepCost, bt25Source |-> gt25Source, bt25ReadyBit |-> gt25ReadyBit,
    bt25Read |-> gt25Read, bt25Run |-> gt25Run, bt25Paid |-> gt25Paid,
    bt25Domain |-> gt25Domain, bt25Checked |-> gt25Checked, bt25OriginalAnswer |-> gt25OriginalAnswer,
    bt25Prefix |-> gt25Prefix, bt25FirstReady |-> gt25FirstReady,
    bt25ClockWithin |-> gt25ClockWithin, bt25UniformClock |-> gt25UniformClock }
  and BT25_Polynomials then
  free type GT25Answer ::= gt25Hit(BT25Word;GC26Nat) | gt25Zero(BT25Word)
  ops gt25Precision,gt25Budget : BT25Word -> GC26Nat;
      gt25C,gt25Sixty : GC26Nat;
      gt25PolyPower : BT25Polynomial * GC26Nat -> BT25Polynomial;
      gt25OriginalSizePolynomial : BT25Polynomial -> BT25Polynomial
  preds gt25FiniteAddressedTypedGraph,gt25SixResidueInputs,gt25PolynomialGates,
        gt25FixedOddDivisors,gt25PositiveDelays,gt25AcyclicWithoutDelays,
        gt25IdempotentEnables,gt25AcquiredCoefficientsCharged : BT25Word;
        gt25Legal,gt25CheckedSection : BT25Word * BT25Word;
        gt25SeedAt,gt25ObservedZero : BT25Word * BT25Word * GC26Nat
  forall x,path,section : BT25Word; i,n : GC26Nat; p : BT25Polynomial
  . gt25Domain(x) <=> GC26positive(gt25Precision(x)) /\ gt25FiniteAddressedTypedGraph(x) /\
      gt25SixResidueInputs(x) /\ gt25PolynomialGates(x) /\ gt25FixedOddDivisors(x) /\
      gt25PositiveDelays(x) /\ gt25AcyclicWithoutDelays(x) /\ gt25IdempotentEnables(x) /\
      gt25AcquiredCoefficientsCharged(x) %(gt25_exact_prior_constructor_source_class)%
  . gt25OriginalAnswer(x,gt25Hit(path,i)) <=>
      gt25Legal(x,path) /\ gt25SeedAt(x,path,i) /\ not gt25ObservedZero(x,path,i)
      %(gt25_hit_retains_original_typed_path_and_seed)%
  . gt25OriginalAnswer(x,gt25Zero(section)) <=> gt25CheckedSection(x,section) /\
      (forall w : BT25Word; j : GC26Nat . gt25Legal(x,w) /\ gt25SeedAt(x,w,j) => gt25ObservedZero(x,w,j))
      %(gt25_zero_retains_checked_all_path_section)%
  . gt25Sixty=GC26nmul(h24Six,h24Ten) %(gt25_fixed_exponent_sixty)%
  . GC26positive(gt25C) %(gt25_prior_fixed_implementation_constant)%
  . gt25Budget(x)=GC26nmul(gt25C,GC26npow(
      GC26nadd(GC26nadd(bt25Length(x),gt25Precision(x)),GC26two),gt25Sixty)) %(gt25_actual_two_parameter_budget)%
  . gt25Domain(x) => gt25ClockWithin(x,gt25Budget(x)) %(gt25_SOURCE_constructed_guarded_tomography_theorem)%
  . gt25PolyPower(p,GC26zero)=bt25Constant(GC26one) %(gt25_polynomial_power_zero)%
  . gt25PolyPower(p,GC26succ(n))=bt25Product(p,gt25PolyPower(p,n)) %(gt25_polynomial_power_step)%
  . gt25OriginalSizePolynomial(p)=bt25Product(bt25Constant(gt25C),gt25PolyPower(
      bt25Sum(bt25Sum(bt25Variable,p),bt25Constant(GC26two)),gt25Sixty)) %(gt25_original_size_polynomial_constructor)%
end
%% Parser predicates denote the prior program's actual finite syntax checks:
%% constants/variables/+/-/* and fixed odd-coefficient division; bounded-arity
%% addressed nodes; six residue inputs per context; any finite hidden register
%% count/degree; delays 2^ell with ell>=1 and acyclic zero-delay dependency graph.
%% Initial-value programs have no free inputs; transition/observation programs have six.
%% N is the complete written input, INCLUDING acquired coefficient prefixes.
%% There is no arbitrary function-evaluation or same-aperture floor(x/2) gate.
%% Path/section words retain the original context labels and proof references.
%% PRIOR CONSTRUCTION PROOF. Factorial coordinates give D<=64*k*m^6 and module
%% length L<=64*k*m^7. Increasing precision plus zero-delay topological order
%% constructs every gate table in <=g*m demands; if ell>=j a delayed gate is 0.
%% Admit only span-independent raw observations; schedule their typed pullbacks
%% and products. At most L admissions give J<=b+rL+L(L+1)/2 jobs. Raw provenance
%% returns a nonzero seed along <=L legal arrows; zero on all generators yields
%% the shared all-path proof by word induction. Phi=t+r(L-a)+(L(L+1)-a(a+1))/2
%% decreases by >=1 per job. Exact module arithmetic/membership plus sequential
%% record access costs <=c0*H^41 per job, while Phi0<=2145*H^16. Include feedback
%% tables, parsing, final readback and replay: fixed C*H^60, H=N+m+2. Thus the
%% prior theorem constructs completion AND the bound on this named class.

spec GT25_OriginalSizeReturn = GT25_SOURCE_GuardedClock then
  forall x : BT25Word; p : BT25Polynomial; n,N : GC26Nat
  . bt25Eval(gt25PolyPower(p,n),N)=GC26npow(bt25Eval(p,N),n) %(gt25_constructed_polynomial_power_value)% %implied
  . gt25Domain(x) /\ h24LeN(gt25Precision(x),bt25Eval(p,bt25Length(x))) =>
      gt25ClockWithin(x,bt25Eval(gt25OriginalSizePolynomial(p),bt25Length(x)))
      %(gt25_guarded_constructor_original_size_clock)% %implied
  . (forall y : BT25Word . gt25Domain(y) => h24LeN(gt25Precision(y),bt25Eval(p,bt25Length(y)))) =>
      gt25UniformClock(N,bt25Eval(gt25OriginalSizePolynomial(p),N))
      %(gt25_guarded_uniform_original_length_polynomial)% %implied
  . gt25Domain(x) => (exists a : GT25Answer . gt25OriginalAnswer(x,a))
      %(gt25_constructed_answer_keeps_original_hit_or_zero)% %implied
end
%% Induct on n for polynomial power; natural +,* and power are monotone.
%% Substitute m<=M(N), widen the SAME fixed producer's actual paid deadline,
%% and quantify over its original input length. No all-H24 source embedding
%% or all-CNF acquisition bound is introduced by this source-domain theorem.

%% Concrete admitted sublanguage: finite linear recipes on the SAME six lanes.
spec GT25_ParacollatzRecipe = PN25_CyclicResolvent then
  free type GT25Recipe ::= gt25Id | gt25U | gt25Two | gt25Four | gt25Eight | gt25NegOver63 |
      gt25Plus(GT25Recipe;GT25Recipe) | gt25Compose(GT25Recipe;GT25Recipe)
  ops gt25EvaluateRecipe : GT25Recipe * PN25Six -> PN25Six;
      gt25HRecipe,gt25KRecipe,gt25PNRecipe : GT25Recipe
  forall a,b : GT25Recipe; v : PN25Six
  . gt25EvaluateRecipe(gt25Id,v)=v /\ gt25EvaluateRecipe(gt25U,v)=pn25U(v) %(gt25_recipe_wires)%
  . gt25EvaluateRecipe(gt25Two,v)=pn25Scale(MT26_two,v) /\
      gt25EvaluateRecipe(gt25Four,v)=pn25Scale(MT26_four,v) /\
      gt25EvaluateRecipe(gt25Eight,v)=pn25Scale(MT26_eight,v) %(gt25_recipe_integral_scalings)%
  . gt25EvaluateRecipe(gt25NegOver63,v)=pn25Scale(MT26_neg(pn25Inv63),v) %(gt25_recipe_fixed_odd_division)%
  . gt25EvaluateRecipe(gt25Plus(a,b),v)=pn25Add(gt25EvaluateRecipe(a,v),gt25EvaluateRecipe(b,v)) %(gt25_recipe_add)%
  . gt25EvaluateRecipe(gt25Compose(a,b),v)=gt25EvaluateRecipe(a,gt25EvaluateRecipe(b,v)) %(gt25_recipe_composition)%
  . gt25HRecipe=gt25Compose(gt25NegOver63,gt25Plus(gt25Id,
      gt25Compose(gt25Eight,gt25Compose(gt25U,gt25Compose(gt25U,gt25U))))) %(gt25_actual_H_recipe)%
  . gt25KRecipe=gt25Plus(gt25Id,gt25Plus(gt25Compose(gt25Two,gt25U),
      gt25Compose(gt25Four,gt25Compose(gt25U,gt25U)))) %(gt25_actual_K_recipe)%
  . gt25PNRecipe=gt25Compose(gt25KRecipe,gt25HRecipe) %(gt25_actual_normalizer_recipe)%
then
  forall v : PN25Six
  . gt25EvaluateRecipe(gt25PNRecipe,v)=pn25Normalize(v) %(gt25_admitted_recipe_is_same_paracollatz_operation)% %implied
end
%% Expanding the finite term gives exactly K(H(v)); these are fixed coordinate
%% additions/scalings and wires, with only the odd unit 63. Compile each recipe
%% constructor to its displayed finite gate recipe: the result is acyclic and
%% has precisely six external lanes, hence is an admitted LOCAL Q_gt operation.
%% Its existing precision/retraction theorem supplies the same residue return.
%% This binding does not map arbitrary proof families into six polynomial
%% variables. The embedded explicit 330*m gate implementation gives the sharper
%% finite normalization clock; any separately run wrapper keeps its paid cost.


%% GT25: the ACTUAL generic Candidate objective, specialized to Q_gt.
%% Source: Hilbert24_ConstructMinimum_Fixed.zip v1, formal/
%% Hilbert24_SAT_Return.lean: Candidate, grade, minimumFromCertificate (14--76).
%% There A is arbitrary. Here A is ALL finite original typed paths/seed indices;
%% verify is legality, seed typing and nonzero ORIGINAL observation. n=|x|.
%% The two source grades n+1,n+2 are not discovery time or arbitrary proof work.
spec GT25_GuardedMinimum = GT25_OriginalSizeReturn then
  free type GT25Candidate ::= gt25Fallback | gt25CandidateHit(BT25Word;GC26Nat)
  free type GT25MinimumPacket ::= gt25MinimumPacket(GT25Candidate;GT25Answer)
  ops gt25CandidateOf : GT25Answer -> GT25Candidate;
      gt25WrapMinimum : GT25Answer -> GT25MinimumPacket;
      gt25MinimumChoice : GT25MinimumPacket -> GT25Candidate;
      gt25MinimumEvidence : GT25MinimumPacket -> GT25Answer;
      gt25CandidateGrade : BT25Word * GT25Candidate -> GC26Nat
  preds gt25CandidateValid,gt25CandidateLeast : BT25Word * GT25Candidate
  forall x,w : BT25Word; i : GC26Nat; a : GT25Answer; c : GT25Candidate
  . gt25CandidateValid(x,gt25Fallback) %(gt25_original_fallback_is_a_candidate)%
  . gt25CandidateValid(x,gt25CandidateHit(w,i)) <=> gt25OriginalAnswer(x,gt25Hit(w,i)) %(gt25_exact_original_hit_competitors)%
  . gt25CandidateGrade(x,gt25Fallback)=GC26nadd(bt25Length(x),GC26two) %(gt25_source_fallback_grade)%
  . gt25CandidateGrade(x,gt25CandidateHit(w,i))=GC26succ(bt25Length(x)) %(gt25_source_verified_candidate_grade)%
  . gt25CandidateOf(gt25Hit(w,i))=gt25CandidateHit(w,i) %(gt25_same_original_hit_choice)%
  . gt25CandidateOf(gt25Zero(w))=gt25Fallback %(gt25_zero_selects_literal_fallback)%
  . gt25WrapMinimum(a)=gt25MinimumPacket(gt25CandidateOf(a),a) %(gt25_actual_certificate_retaining_constructor)%
  . gt25MinimumChoice(gt25MinimumPacket(c,a))=c /\ gt25MinimumEvidence(gt25MinimumPacket(c,a))=a %(gt25_packet_projections)%
  . gt25CandidateLeast(x,c) <=> gt25CandidateValid(x,c) /\
      (forall d : GT25Candidate . gt25CandidateValid(x,d) => h24LeN(gt25CandidateGrade(x,c),gt25CandidateGrade(x,d)))
      %(gt25_every_original_candidate_minimum_definition)%
then
  forall x : BT25Word; a : GT25Answer; c : GT25Candidate; k : GC26Nat
  . gt25MinimumEvidence(gt25WrapMinimum(a))=a %(gt25_wrapped_minimum_retains_exact_hit_or_zero_section)% %implied
  . gt25OriginalAnswer(x,a) => gt25CandidateLeast(x,gt25MinimumChoice(gt25WrapMinimum(a)))
      %(gt25_original_certificate_constructs_global_candidate_minimum)% %implied
  . gt25OriginalAnswer(x,a) /\ gt25CandidateValid(x,c) =>
      h24LeN(gt25CandidateGrade(x,gt25CandidateOf(a)),gt25CandidateGrade(x,c))
      %(gt25_global_minimum_against_every_original_finite_path_competitor)% %implied
  . gt25FirstReady(x,k) => gt25CandidateLeast(x,gt25CandidateOf(gt25Read(gt25Run(x,k))))
      %(gt25_actual_guarded_producer_constructs_the_original_candidate_minimum)% %implied
end
%% Proof is the source's two cases. Hit has grade n+1, below either grade.
%% Zero's retained all-path section rules out EVERY valid Hit competitor;
%% only fallback remains, of equal grade n+2. No path-length cap is introduced.
%% Packet projection returns the exact original certificate, including Zero's
%% shared section. Its leastness proof does not erase that section as fallback.

spec GT25_GuardedMinimumClock = GT25_GuardedMinimum then
  ops gt25WrapperConstant : GC26Nat;
      gt25WrapperPaid : BT25Word * GT25Answer -> GC26Nat;
      gt25MinimumPaid : BT25Word * GC26Nat -> GC26Nat;
      gt25MinimumPolynomial : BT25Polynomial -> BT25Polynomial
  pred gt25MinimumWithin : BT25Word * GC26Nat
  forall x : BT25Word; k,t : GC26Nat; p : BT25Polynomial
  . GC26positive(gt25WrapperConstant) %(gt25_fixed_literal_packaging_constant)%
  . gt25FirstReady(x,k) => h24LeN(gt25WrapperPaid(x,gt25Read(gt25Run(x,k))),
      GC26nmul(gt25WrapperConstant,GC26succ(GC26nadd(bt25Length(x),gt25Paid(x,k)))))
      %(gt25_SOURCE_literal_packet_wrapper_local_paid_bound)%
  . gt25MinimumPaid(x,k)=GC26nadd(gt25Paid(x,k),gt25WrapperPaid(x,gt25Read(gt25Run(x,k)))) %(gt25_pay_constructor_then_wrapper)%
  . gt25MinimumWithin(x,t) <=> (exists j : GC26Nat . gt25FirstReady(x,j) /\
      gt25CandidateLeast(x,gt25CandidateOf(gt25Read(gt25Run(x,j)))) /\ h24LeN(gt25MinimumPaid(x,j),t)) %(gt25_actual_wrapped_minimum_deadline)%
  . gt25MinimumPolynomial(p)=bt25Sum(gt25OriginalSizePolynomial(p),bt25Product(bt25Constant(gt25WrapperConstant),
      bt25Sum(bt25Sum(bt25Variable,gt25OriginalSizePolynomial(p)),bt25Constant(GC26one)))) %(gt25_explicit_wrapped_original_size_polynomial)%
then
  forall x : BT25Word; p : BT25Polynomial
  . gt25Domain(x) /\ h24LeN(gt25Precision(x),bt25Eval(p,bt25Length(x))) =>
      gt25MinimumWithin(x,bt25Eval(gt25MinimumPolynomial(p),bt25Length(x)))
      %(gt25_original_guarded_candidate_minimum_has_polynomial_paid_generation)% %implied
end
%% The wrapper is the displayed branch/tag/retain constructor, implemented by
%% a sequential copy with a fixed number of headers; it runs no optimizer.
%% Its local paid bound is the explicit implementation port: the SOURCE K_gt
%% bit machine already charges writing/checking the entire returned path/seed
%% or section, so a fixed scan/copy costs <=cW*(N+Paid+1). No uncharged oracle
%% or arbitrary hp25Build tree is substituted for that execution.
%% Given its actual first event with Paid<=P(N), add the separately paid wrapper
%% and use monotonicity: Paid+Wrap<=P(N)+cW*(N+P(N)+1). This is the displayed
%% polynomial, with the SAME original question, minimum and retained evidence.


%% A CONSTRUCTED MINIMUM FOR THE ACTUAL CHART-CALCULATION CLASS.
%% The candidate is constructed from its endpoints and retained operation word.
%% Every competitor is an arbitrary valid route with that same normal form.
spec PC25_ChartMinimum = MG26_RouteCost then
  op pc25CanonicalRoute : MG26Route -> MG26Route
  forall r : MG26Route
  . pc25CanonicalRoute(r)=mg26Edge(mg26Source(r),mg26Program(r),mg26Target(r))
      %(pc25_construct_the_source_retaining_normal_route)%
then
  forall r,s : MG26Route; u : H24ChartValue
  . mg26Valid(pc25CanonicalRoute(r)) /\ mg26SameNormal(pc25CanonicalRoute(r),r)
      %(pc25_constructed_route_valid_with_identical_source_program)% %implied
  . mg26Valid(r) => mg26Run(pc25CanonicalRoute(r),u)=mg26Run(r,u)
      %(pc25_minimum_route_returns_the_original_computation)% %implied
  . mg26ChartWork(pc25CanonicalRoute(r))=GC26two
      %(pc25_constructed_route_attains_two_chart_calls)% %implied
  . h24LeN(GC26two,mg26ChartWork(s)) %(pc25_every_route_has_the_same_lower_bound)% %implied
  . mg26Valid(s) /\ mg26SameNormal(s,r) =>
      h24LeN(mg26ChartWork(pc25CanonicalRoute(r)),mg26ChartWork(s))
      %(pc25_minimum_against_every_original_route_competitor)% %implied
end
%% Proof: the Edge constructor returns its stated endpoints and literal word;
%% its sole leaf costs exactly two calls. Every free route has at least one
%% leaf by structural induction, so every competitor costs at least two calls.
%% The MG26 route theorem returns the identical original computation. No route
%% is searched or enumerated. This minimum concerns chart adapter calls; source
%% actions, proof policy acquisition and the original bit clock retain their
%% explicit costs. A single stack traversal constructs the root normal form
%% for an explicitly written route. Expanded source-word length is included
%% in that input/output convention; no exponential expansion of a shared DAG
%% is priced as a single written symbol.

%% PAID GATE COUNTS OF THE EMBEDDED CAUSAL SIX-LANE NORMALIZER.
%% One full adder: p=a xor b; s=p xor c; q=a*b; r=p*c; h=q xor r.
%% On fresh zero wires, three XORs use six controlled-NOT Toffoli instances,
%% and two products use two Toffolis. Six lanes therefore use 48 gates.
%% Copy the twelve (sum,carry) outputs and reverse those gates: 108.
%% In each column, input fill/clear=12, output copy=6, store/clear old state=24,
%% and twelve state swaps are Fredkin(ONE,-,-). Thus 108+12+6+24+12=162.
%% Compute all columns, copy 6m result bits, and reverse: 2*162m+6m=330m.
spec PG25_NormalizerGateClock = H24_NaturalOrder then
  ops pg25Eight,pg25Nine,pg25TwentyFour,pg25FortyEight,pg25OneZeroEight,
      pg25OneSixtyTwo,pg25ThreeZeroSix,pg25ThreeThirty : GC26Nat;
      pg25ForwardGates,pg25CleanGates,pg25ToffoliGates,pg25FredkinGates : GC26Nat -> GC26Nat
  . pg25Eight=GC26nmul(GC26two,GC26four)
  . pg25Nine=GC26nmul(GC26three,GC26three)
  . pg25TwentyFour=GC26nmul(GC26four,h24Six)
  . pg25FortyEight=GC26nmul(h24Six,pg25Eight)
  . pg25OneZeroEight=GC26nadd(GC26nmul(GC26two,pg25FortyEight),GC26nmul(GC26two,h24Six))
  . pg25OneSixtyTwo=GC26nadd(pg25OneZeroEight,GC26nmul(pg25Nine,h24Six))
  . pg25ThreeThirty=GC26nadd(GC26nmul(GC26two,pg25OneSixtyTwo),h24Six)
  . pg25ThreeZeroSix=GC26nmul(GC26succ(GC26nmul(h24Five,h24Ten)),h24Six)
  forall m : GC26Nat
  . pg25ForwardGates(GC26zero)=GC26zero %(pg25_empty_forward_word)%
  . pg25ForwardGates(GC26succ(m))=GC26nadd(pg25ForwardGates(m),pg25OneSixtyTwo)
      %(pg25_fixed_forward_column_constructor)%
  . pg25CleanGates(m)=GC26nadd(GC26nmul(GC26two,pg25ForwardGates(m)),GC26nmul(h24Six,m))
      %(pg25_actual_compute_copy_uncompute_word)%
  . pg25ToffoliGates(m)=GC26nmul(pg25ThreeZeroSix,m) %(pg25_toffoli_word_length)%
  . pg25FredkinGates(m)=GC26nmul(pg25TwentyFour,m) %(pg25_fredkin_word_length)%
then
  forall m : GC26Nat
  . pg25ForwardGates(m)=GC26nmul(pg25OneSixtyTwo,m)
      %(pg25_forward_clock_for_every_precision)% %implied
  . pg25CleanGates(m)=GC26nmul(pg25ThreeThirty,m)
      %(pg25_clean_normalizer_linear_gate_clock)% %implied
  . pg25CleanGates(m)=GC26nadd(pg25ToffoliGates(m),pg25FredkinGates(m))
      %(pg25_exact_fredkin_toffoli_cost_split)% %implied
end
%% Natural induction counts ALL columns, not a sampled prefix. The Boolean
%% full-adder identity is checked symbolically modulo bit idempotence in the
%% embedded executable. Its loop invariant and the PN25 inverse identify the
%% output with -(I+2U+4U^2)(I+8U^3)/63 at every cut. Each primitive gate is its
%% own inverse; reversing the actual word clears every work/history bit.
%% The fixed local window has 61 cells, with sequential input/output/history
%% tapes; total allocated wire count is 61+30m in the explicit replay circuit.
%% Gate routing/control and tape motion have an input-independent simulation
%% constant. BT25 prices that implementation witness as cPN*(m+1), and also
%% pays acquisition, lookup, checking and original-source readback. The Python
%% addressed-circuit replay is not asserted to be this local machine's clock.


%% THE SAME CONSTRUCTED PROOF WITH ITS SEPARATELY TYPED COEFFICIENT PORT.
%% A six-lane coefficient chart is never substituted for the entire proof.
%% This concrete product retains that proof packet, its frame and its support.
spec PP25_ProofCoefficientReturn = HP25_ConstructedProofPackets and
  PN25_CyclicResolvent then
  free type PP25State ::= pp25State(HC24ProofPacket;PN25Six)
  ops pp25Packet : PP25State -> HC24ProofPacket;
      pp25Coefficient : PP25State -> PN25Six;
      pp25Normalize,pp25Read : PP25State -> PP25State;
      pp25Rechart : TR26Point * TR26Point * PP25State -> PP25State;
      pp25Built : HP25Policy * HC24Judgement * TR26Point * PN25Six -> PP25State
  forall p : HC24ProofPacket; v : PN25Six; f,g : TR26Point;
         P : HP25Policy; q : HC24Judgement
  . pp25Packet(pp25State(p,v))=p /\ pp25Coefficient(pp25State(p,v))=v
      %(pp25_both_original_components_retained)%
  . pp25Normalize(pp25State(p,v))=pp25State(p,pn25Normalize(v))
      %(pp25_apply_actual_normalizer_to_coefficient_port)%
  . pp25Read(pp25State(p,v))=pp25State(p,pn25Read(v))
      %(pp25_actual_inverse_on_coefficient_port)%
  . pp25Rechart(f,g,pp25State(p,v))=pp25State(hc24ProofPacketTransition(f,g,p),v)
      %(pp25_same_retained_torser_frame_change)%
  . pp25Built(P,q,f,v)=pp25Normalize(pp25State(hp25BuildPacket(P,q,f),v))
      %(pp25_construct_minimum_and_normalize_its_coefficient_port)%
then
  forall s : PP25State; P : HP25Policy; q : HC24Judgement;
         f,g : TR26Point; v : PN25Six; h : HC24Assumption
  . pp25Read(pp25Normalize(s))=s /\ pp25Normalize(pp25Read(s))=s
      %(pp25_entire_original_state_both_returns)% %implied
  . pp25Normalize(pp25Rechart(f,g,s))=pp25Rechart(f,g,pp25Normalize(s))
      %(pp25_normalization_is_natural_under_actual_frame_change)% %implied
  . hc24DecodePacket(pp25Packet(pp25Built(P,q,f,v)))=hp25Build(P,q) /\
      pn25Read(pp25Coefficient(pp25Built(P,q,f,v)))=v
      %(pp25_same_complete_constructed_proof_and_coefficient_return)% %implied
  . hp25TightPolicy(P) => hc24Minimum(hc24DecodePacket(pp25Packet(pp25Built(P,q,f,v))))
      %(pp25_original_minimum_survives_paracollatz_normalization)% %implied
  . hc24PacketUses(pp25Packet(pp25Built(P,q,f,v)),h) <=> hc24Uses(hp25Build(P,q),h)
      %(pp25_every_original_assumption_occurrence_survives)% %implied
end
view PP25ConstructedNormalizationChart : H24_Retraction to PP25_ProofCoefficientReturn =
  H24Source |-> PP25State, H24Target |-> PP25State,
  h24Encode |-> pp25Normalize, h24Decode |-> pp25Read
end
%% Proof: product beta/eta reduces both inverse equations to RN=NR=I.
%% The two naturality routes act on different retained components and hence
%% have the identical pair. Projection returns the literal constructed proof;
%% HP25's minimum and exact support apply without modifying its judgement.
%% Coefficient normalization costs the actual 330*m gates. Constructing,
%% serializing or checking the complete proof remains paid by its own phase;
%% a retained reference is not claimed to make its full output length zero.


spec AT25_ConstructivePolynomialAtlas = AT26_CalculationAtlas and
  HP25_OriginalMinimumReturn and PP25_ProofCoefficientReturn and
  PN25_DaggerResolvent and PN25_Naturality and PN25_SeparatedReturn and
  PN25_DigitProducer and PN25_CarryWords and BT25_PrefixPotential and
  BT25_SharedKeyClock and BT25_ParacollatzPipeline and BT25_CleanReversibleClock and
  GT25_GuardedMinimumClock and GT25_ParacollatzRecipe and
  PC25_ChartMinimum and PG25_NormalizerGateClock
end

